hadiths[600]{hadithnumber,text}:
"2401","2097 - And Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Khushaysh narrated to us, he said: Muslim ibn Ibrahim al-Azdi narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Abi Abdillah narrated to us, then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it."
"2402","2098 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: I heard Yazid ibn Harun, he said: Hammam narrated to us, from Qatada, from Anas."
"2403","And from Qatada, from Abu Isa al-Uswari, from Abu Said, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"2404","2099 - And Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us. And Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Ismail narrated to us, they said: Hammad ibn Salama narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Ikrima, from Abu Hurayra, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it. Abu Jafar said: So in these reports the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade drinking standing, so we sought the meaning for which he forbade that."
"2405","2100 - So we found Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Salama ibn Shabib narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, from Mamar, from al-Amash, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If one who drinks standing knew what is in his belly, he would vomit. So that reached Ali ibn Abi Talib, peace be upon him, and he stood and drank standing."
"2406","2101 - And we found Abu Umayya narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Bahr ibn Barri narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Yusuf narrated to us, he said: Mamar narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Hurayra, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with the like of it. Mamar said, and al-Amash mentioned it from Abu Salih from Abu Hurayra: Al-Amash said: So that reached Ali, peace be upon him, from Abu Hurayra's saying, and he stood and drank standing. So we stood by what we narrated from Abu Hurayra from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this meaning upon the cause for which was his prohibition of drinking standing, and that was of the ailment befalling people in their bellies from their drinking standing, so he forbade them that out of compassion, mercy and bodily welfare. And this hadith was narrated from Abu Hurayra from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with other than these wordings."
"2407","2102 - As Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Shuba narrated to us, from Abu Ziyad client of al-Hasan ibn Ali, he said: I heard Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, narrate from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he saw a man drinking standing, so he said to him: Vomit. He said: Why? He said: Would you like a cat to drink with you? He said: No. So he said: One worse than a cat drank with you: Satan. He said: So in this is that he only forbade that for Satan's drinking with one drinking standing. Then an objector said: How do you accept this while with you is from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what opposes it."
"2408","2103 - Then he mentioned what Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj informed me, from Muhammad ibn Ali ibn al-Husayn, from his father, from his grandfather, he said: Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, said to me: Bring me ablution water, so I brought him and he performed ablution, then stood with the remainder of his ablution water and drank it standing, so I marveled at that. So he said: Do you marvel, O my son? I saw your father the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) do that."
"2409","2104 - And what Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Umar al-Zahrani narrated to us, he said: Shuba narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Maysara, from al-Nazzal ibn Sabra, he said: I saw Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, drink the remainder of his ablution water standing, then said: People dislike drinking standing, yet I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) do what I did."
"2410","2105 - And what Bakkar ibn Qutayba narrated to us, he said: Abu Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Misar narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Maysara, then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it."
"2411","2106 - And what Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salama narrated to us, from Ata ibn al-Saib, from Zadham, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, that he drank standing, so it was said to him therein. So he said: If I drink standing, I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) drink standing, and if I drink sitting, I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) do that."
"2412","2107 - And what al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Warqa ibn Umar narrated to us, from Ata ibn al-Saib, from Zadham and Maysara, from Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with the like of it."
"2413","2108 - And what Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Asim al-Ahwal, from al-Shabi, from Abdullah ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them, he said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) drink while standing."
"2414","2109 - And what Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Asbahani narrated to us, he said: Sharik informed us, from al-Shaybani, from Amir, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them, he said: I handed the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a bucket of Zamzam water and he drank while standing."
"2415","2110 - And what Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abd al-Karim ibn Malik informed me, he said: al-Bara ibn Zayd informed me that Umm Sulaym narrated to him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) drank standing from the mouth of a waterskin."
"2416","2111 - And what Abu Umayya narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, from Humayd, from Anas, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) drank from a hanging waterskin while standing. So our answer therein by Allah's guidance and help was that in these reports in this last section of this chapter of the Messenger's drinking standing may be from before his standing upon what Abu Hurayra related from him, then after that he stood upon what Abu Hurayra related from him therein and forbade it for what is therein upon its doers, so things were upon their إطلاق and permissibility till the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood upon what is therein upon its doers and rebuked it and forbade it out of compassion for his nation, mercy and seeking their welfare. So by praise of Allah all we narrated in this chapter came out with none contradicting another, and we ask Allah for guidance."
"2417","Chapter 338 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: Allah sent no prophet nor appointed any caliph but he has two intimates, an intimate ordering him with good and urging him upon it, and an intimate sparing him no corruption. 2112 - Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Abd al-Hakam narrated to us, he said: My father and Shuayb ibn al-Layth narrated to me. And Harun ibn Kamil narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Each of them narrated to me al-Layth, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Abi Jafar narrated to me, he said: Safwan narrated to me, from Abu Salama, from Abu Ayyub that he said: I heard the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Allah sent no prophet nor was after him any caliph but he has two intimates, an intimate ordering him with right and forbidding him evil, and an intimate sparing him no corruption, so whoever is shielded from the evil intimate is shielded."
"2418","2113 - And what Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Abu Salama ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Said al-Khudri, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) he said: Allah sent no prophet nor appointed any caliph but he had two intimates, an intimate ordering him with good and urging him upon it, and an intimate ordering him with evil and urging him upon it, so the protected is whom Allah protects."
"2419","2114 - Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Abdullah narrated to us, he said: Ayyub ibn Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Uways narrated to me, from Sulayman ibn Bilal, he said: Yahya said: Ibn Shihab informed us, from Abu Salama ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Said al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned the like of it."
"2420","2115 - Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ismail narrated to us, he said: Ayyub, meaning Ibn Sulayman, narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr narrated to us, from Sulayman, from Muhammad ibn Abi Atiq and Musa ibn Uqba, from Ibn Shihab, from Abu Salama, from Abu Said al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned the like of it."
"2421","2116 - Bakkar ibn Qutayba narrated to us, he said: Muammal narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salama narrated to us, he said: Burd ibn Sinan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Salama, from Abu Hurayra that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no prophet nor caliph, or he said imam, but he has two intimates, an intimate ordering him with right, and another intimate sparing no corruption, so whoever is shielded from the evil of his second intimate is shielded, and he is of the one prevailing over him of them. Abu Jafar said: This is the last of what Bakkar narrated."
"2422","2117 - Sulayman ibn Shuayb al-Kaysani narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Bakr narrated to us, he said: al-Awzai narrated to me, he said: al-Zuhri narrated to me, he said: Abu Salama ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to me, he said: Abu Hurayra narrated to me, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no governor but he has two intimates, an intimate ordering him with right and forbidding him evil, and an intimate sparing him no corruption, so whoever is shielded from its evil is shielded, and he is of the one prevailing over him of them. Abu Jafar said: So we considered these reports to stand upon what is intended thereby if Allah wills. His saying: Allah sent no prophet nor appointed any caliph but he has two intimates upon what I mentioned, each of those two intimates of what he mentioned thereof of praise and other. So we found the prophets, Allah's blessings upon them, call people to what they were sent with to them, so that is cause for their coming to them and mixing with them till they are thereby intimates to them, and the prophets employ in that in their affairs what they stand upon thereof, so they praise therein whom they stand upon deserving praise by his outward and bring him near and count him of their allies, and distance from them whom they stand upon what they do not praise, and count him of their enemies, and Allah knows best what He conceals of known praise and blame, then Allah informs His prophets of their inward, as He said to our Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): And of those around you of bedouins are hypocrites and of Medina's people persistent in hypocrisy, you know them not, the verse. So this is the blameworthy intimate sparing no corruption its companion. And the other intimate is whom Allah informs them of its opposite and what it is upon toward its prophet, as Allah informed our Prophet of the states of the believers in him of their honoring him, supporting him and following what must be followed, as He said: So those who believed in him, honored him, supported him and followed the light sent with him, those are the successful, and as He said in their description: Muhammad is Allah's Messenger and those with him severe upon unbelievers merciful among them, then He described them till He sealed the sura that was sent therein. So these two intimates are the two that were with our Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and likewise the intimates with the prophets before him. Then we considered his saying: and he is of the one prevailing over him of them, and that in our view, and Allah knows best, refers to other than the prophets of those mentioned in these reports, not to the prophets, for the prophets are protected and are not with one whose character and ways are not praised. Then an objector said: How may that be as you mentioned, while in these reports the speech returns upon those mentioned therein of prophets and others? So our answer therein by Allah's guidance and help was that this speech is Arabic speech addressed to Arab people understanding what their addresser intends, and Arabs address the like of this upon a group then return it to some apart from the rest, of that is Allah's saying: O company of jinn and men, did there not come to you messengers of you, while the address therein mentions jinn and men though understood the messengers are of men not jinn. And like that is the Messenger's saying in Ubada ibn al-Samit's hadith: Pledge me upon not associating with Allah anything, and he read the verse of the test, and therein is association, theft and adultery, and it is His saying: Pledging you upon not associating with Allah anything nor stealing nor committing adultery, and we shall mention that hadith later in this book if Allah wills. And therein: Whoever of that befalls something then is punished therefor it is expiation for him, while we know whoever is punished for association, that is not expiation for him. And we understood thereby his saying: Whoever of that befalls something, is only upon something of some of those things in the verse not upon everything therein, so like that is his saying in the reports we narrated: and he is of the one prevailing over him of them, returning upon whoever may be such, not upon the prophets who are not such. So it became clear by what we mentioned all the difficult meanings in these reports, praise and favor be to Allah, and Him we ask for guidance."
"2423","Chapter 339 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning one with parents striving against the enemy, is it better for him or staying with his parents and leaving striving? 2118 - Ali ibn Maabad and Abu Umayya narrated to us, they said: Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Kunasa al-Asadi narrated to us, he said: al-Amash narrated to us, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Abdullah ibn Babah, from Abdullah ibn Amr, may Allah be pleased with them, he said: A man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: I want jihad. So he said: Are your parents alive? He said: Yes. He said: Then in them strive."
"2424","2119 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Misar narrated to us, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Abu al-Abbas, from Abdullah ibn Amr, may Allah be pleased with them, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"2425","2120 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud, Yaqub ibn Ishaq and Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, they said: Shuba narrated to us, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Abu al-Abbas, and he was a poet and approved, as Wahb said in other than this hadith of what Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us from him, then we returned to his hadith, from Abu Dawud, Yaqub and Wahb, from Abdullah ibn Amr, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"2426","2121 - Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, from Sufyan al-Thawri, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it. Abu Jafar said: And people differ concerning Abu al-Abbas the poet, companion of this hadith; some say he is Abdullah ibn Babah, and some say he is al-Saib ibn Farrukh, and of those saying he is Abdullah ibn Babah was Ahmad ibn Salih, and what is in these reports indicates what he said, for Misar and Shuba narrated his hadith in this chapter from Habib ibn Abi Thabit from him, and kunya'd him Abu al-Abbas, and al-Amash narrated it from Habib from him and mentioned he is Abdullah ibn Babah, so that indicates he is Abdullah ibn Babah. Then some said: How can a man be in latitude of leaving jihad with devotion to his parents while Allah said: If you do not go forth He will punish you painful punishment, and this threat is only in an obligation, and we found the obligatory pilgrimage is not cut off by staying with parents for whoever finds way thereto? So our answer therein by Allah's guidance and help was that what he recited of threat in jihad is upon an obligation as mentioned, except it is a general obligation sufficed by the particular from the rest of its people, like washing our dead, praying upon them and burying them in graves, all that is obligation upon us, and whoever of us undertakes it, the obligation drops from the rest, and had we all left it we would be of the threat he recited, while the obligation of hajj is of the general obligation none undertakes for others, so what was of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to one who came asking about the jihad others undertake for him, was his ordering him to stay with his parents which none undertakes for him, for when he does that both obligations drop from him, for one drops by his doing it and the other drops by others' doing it of the Muslims, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered him with what drops thereby two obligations and leaving what if he does drops one obligation, and likewise he ordered others in this meaning."
"2427","2122 - As Imran ibn Musa al-Tai narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to me, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ata ibn al-Saib, from his father, from Abdullah ibn Amr ibn al-As, he said: A man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: I came to pledge you while I left my parents weeping. He said: Return to them and make them laugh as you made them weep."
"2428","2123 - And as Abu Umayya narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Qadim narrated to us, he said: Misar narrated to us, from Ata ibn al-Saib, from his father, from Abdullah ibn Amr, may Allah be pleased with them, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it and added: And he refused to pledge him."
"2429","2124 - And as Imran ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Abu Salama Musa ibn Ismail narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salama narrated to us, from Ata ibn al-Saib, from his father, from Abdullah ibn Amr ibn al-As, he said: A man came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: I came to pledge you upon emigration while I left my parents weeping. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I shall not pledge you till you return to them and make them laugh as you made them weep. Abu Jafar said: And in this is strengthening what we narrated before it. And there was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) also what he informed therein that dutifulness to parents is better than jihad."
"2430","2125 - And it is what Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Adam ibn Abi Iyas narrated to us, from Shuba, from al-Walid ibn al-Ayzar, he said: I heard Abu Amr al-Shaybani say: I heard the master of this house, meaning Ibn Masud, say: I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Which deeds are most beloved to Allah Almighty? He said: Prayer at its time. So I said: Then which? He said: Then dutifulness to parents. Then I said: Then which? He said: Jihad in Allah's way. And had I asked more he would have given me more."
"2431","2126 - And what Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Yaqub ibn Ishaq ibn Abi Abbad narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Tahman narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq al-Hamdani, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abdullah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"2432","2127 - And what Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Abu Muawiya Amr ibn Abdullah al-Nakhai narrated to us, he said: Abu Amr al-Shaybani narrated to me, he said: The master of this house, meaning Abdullah ibn Masud, narrated to me, he said: I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) which deed is best? He said: Prayer at its appointed time. I said: Then what, O Messenger of Allah? He said: Dutifulness to parents. I said: Then what, O Messenger of Allah? He said: That people be safe from your tongue. And he was silent, and had I asked more he would have given me more. Abu Jafar said: Do you not see that in this hadith the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed that dutifulness to parents is better than jihad, so that also confirms what we narrated in the first reports and supports what we carried them upon of the aspects we carried them upon, and Allah knows best the Messenger's intents therein, except they came out agreeing with one another, and we ask Allah for guidance."
"2433","Chapter 340 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning the stallion which he forbade taking in charity. 2128 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Ansari narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to me, from Thumama, from Anas that in the book Abu Bakr al-Siddiq wrote concerning charity, and he wrote therein for him: It is the charity of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) which Allah Almighty obligated upon His creation, so whoever is asked above it, give him not: That there be not taken in charity an old, nor one with defect, nor a billy-goat, except if the collector wills. And thus Ibrahim narrated it to us with kasr, meaning thereby the governor over charity."
"2434","2129 - And likewise Bakkar ibn Qutayba narrated to us, from Abu Umar al-Darir, from Hammad ibn Salama that Thumama sent him with that book to Thabit."
"2435","2130 - And likewise al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, from Asad, from Hammad, each of them mentioned this letter with kasr. Abu Jafar said: And Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz permitted me from Abu Ubayd that he said: The hadith scholars say in this hadith: Except if the collector wills, with kasr, while I see it: Except if the collected-from wills, with fath, meaning the owner of wealth. Abu Jafar said: And it is in my view as Abu Ubayd said, and Allah knows best, for if the billy-goat exceeded the age obligatory upon the owner in what is obligated in his wealth, it would be unlawful for the collector to take it for what is therein of increase over the obligatory upon its owner taken from him, and if it were below the obligatory upon its owner, it would be unlawful for the collector to take it from its owner for what is upon him in his wealth of what is above it, and if it were like it in value, it is other than the kind ordered to be taken for its obligatoriness upon its owner, so unlawful for him to take it without its owner's goodwill, so that indicates the collector was not intended by what is mentioned in this hadith, and the intended thereby is the owner of wealth not the collector, so to him is the choice to give above what is upon him or like what is upon him of other than the kind of what is upon him, and to the collector is acceptance thereof from him if he sees that as benefit for what he administers of charity, and we ask Allah Almighty for guidance."
"2436","Chapter 341 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning one with one of his parents, is his dutifulness by staying with him better than jihad, or jihad better than it. 2131 - Ali ibn Maabad narrated to us, he said: Attab ibn Ziyad al-Marwazi narrated to us, he said: Abu Hamza narrated to us, from Ata ibn al-Saib, from his father, from Abdullah ibn Amr, he said: A man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: I pledge you upon emigration. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Have you a father or mother? He said: Yes. He said: Then in them strive."
"2437","2132 - Ali ibn Maabad narrated to us, he said: al-Hajjaj ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, he said: Muhammad ibn Talha ibn Abdullah ibn Abd al-Rahman informed me, from his father Talha, from Muawiya ibn Jahima al-Sulami."
"2438","And Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Muhammad ibn Talha, from his father, from Muawiya ibn Jahima. Then they agreed and said: Jahima came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, I wanted to raid, and I came consulting you. So he said: Have you a mother? He said: Yes. He said: Then stay with her, for Paradise is at her foot, then the second, then the third in various sittings with the like of this saying."
"2439","2133 - And Abu Umayya narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim and Hajjaj ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Muhammad ibn Talha ibn Abdullah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from his father, from Muawiya ibn Jahima, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it. Abu Jafar said: So in what we narrated the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered the man to stay with one of his parents for dutifulness, and that it is better for him than jihad, and in that is what indicates one of them therein is as both therein, and we mentioned before in this book his ordering with this meaning concerning both, and in what we narrated in this chapter of Muawiya ibn Jahima's hadith is what indicates it is in the mother as in both, and in the other hadith is what indicates it is in each one as in both together, for the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to his asker therein: Have you a father or mother? He said: Yes. He said: Then in them strive. So that indicates each one stands therein the place of both together therein, and we ask Allah Almighty for guidance."
"2440","Chapter 342 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning the two intended by Allah's saying: And if you turn away He will replace you with a people other than you, then they will not be like you. 2134 - Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Muslim ibn Khalid narrated to us, from al-Ala ibn Abd al-Rahman, from his father, from Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited this verse: And if you turn away He will replace you with a people other than you, then they will not be like you. They said: O Messenger of Allah, who are those who if we turn away will be replaced for us and will not be like us? So he struck the thigh of Salman and said: This and his people. And had religion been at the Pleiades, men of Persia would have reached it."
"2441","2135 - Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad al-Darawardi narrated to us, he said: al-Ala ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from his father, from Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: When there was sent down: And if you turn away He will replace you with a people other than you, they said: Who are they, O Messenger of Allah? He said, while Salman was beside him: They are the Persians, this and his people."
"2442","2136 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Maabad narrated to us. And Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, then they agreed and each said: Ismail ibn Jafar narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Jafar ibn Najih narrated to me, from al-Ala ibn Abd al-Rahman, from his father, from Abu Hurayra, he said: Some Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, in Fahd's hadith: O Messenger of Allah, who are those whom Allah mentioned in the Quran, if we turn away replaced then not like us? He said: While Salman was beside the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) struck the thigh of Salman and said: This and his people. By Him in Whose hand is my soul, had faith been at the Pleiades, men of Persia would have attained it. Abu Jafar said: And what carried us to bring this second hadith, though its isnad is corrupt by Abdullah ibn Jafar whom Ismail ibn Jafar narrated from, and he is Abu Ali ibn al-Madini, for the unanimity of hadith people upon leaving his narration, was fear a man would take it out of this isnad and the hadith would return to Ismail ibn Jafar from al-Ala, for he is one of the narrators from him, and with Ismail is of majesty, precedence in knowledge and firmness in narration what is thereof, so whoever stands upon that would count us leaving a hadith in this chapter unbecoming our like to leave from him, so we mentioned it in this chapter for that. Then we considered the meaning therein and found it a severe threat to those mentioned therein if they turn away, of replacement by others unlike them therein, so we found the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) are those addressed thereby if they turn away, but they did not turn away, praise and favor be to Allah, so they do not deserve that threat, may Allah be pleased with them. And we found threat may be intended toward one while another is meant, and of that is Allah's saying to His Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): And it was revealed to you and to those before you: If you associate, your deeds will be nullified and you will be of the losers, while of what Allah knew He will not have it from him, for He undertook him and protected him and prepared for him His pleasure and Paradise, and the intended by that threat was other, meaning: Since his rank (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with Allah was this rank which is for none else, and were he to associate the threat in this verse would befall him, while association will not be from him, then whoever association may be from, when he associates, is worthier of that threat and likelier of its befalling him. And like Allah's saying to him: And had he fabricated against Us some sayings, We would have seized him by the right, then cut from him the lifeline. Abu Jafar said: Al-watin is the heart's aorta. Then He knew that will not be from him, so He informed them that had that been from him this threat would befall, that they may know if that is from them, and of them is one imagined it may be from him if his Lord does not protect him therefrom, that they by that threat's befalling them when it is from them are worthier and its befalling them likelier. So like that is His saying to them: And if you turn away He will replace you with a people other than you, while they are His choice for His Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and He prepared for them what He prepared in the Hereafter of His honor and pleasure with what will not be from them therewith in this world of turning away from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so that threat was for others than them who may turn away, and by his turning away he is of that threat's people and deserving of its befalling him, and we ask Allah تعالى for guidance."
"2443","Chapter 343 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of what we know certainly they said only by his informing them thereof, in the meaning of Allah's saying: And nullify not your deeds. 2137 - Jafar ibn Muhammad ibn al-Hasan al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Yaqub al-Talqani narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Bukayr ibn Maruf, from Muqatil ibn Hayyan, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: We, the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), used to see none of our good deeds but accepted, till this verse was sent down: Obey Allah and obey the Messenger and nullify not your deeds. So we considered what is this nullifying our deeds, and we said: The mortal major sins and indecencies, till there was sent down: Allah forgives not that partners be associated with Him and forgives less than that for whom He wills. So when it was sent down we refrained from speech, and we feared for whoever committed major sins and hoped for whoever did not commit them. Abu Jafar said: So what is in this hadith indicates what they were upon at first before this verse, that whoever had major sins, good deeds after that were not accepted from him, till Allah sent down this recited verse in this hadith, so they knew thereby He forgives not association with Him and forgives less than that for whom He wills, so they understood thereby He may forgive major-sin people when with them they associate nothing with Him, and we ask Allah for guidance."
"2444","Chapter 344 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning righteousness and sin, what they are. 2138 - Fahd ibn Sulayman and Harun ibn Kamil narrated to us, they said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Muawiya ibn Salih narrated to me, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Jubayr ibn Nufayr, from his father, from Nawwas ibn Siman, he said: I stayed with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in Madina a year, nothing preventing me from emigrating but asking, for when one of us emigrated he would not ask the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about anything. He said: So I asked him about righteousness and sin. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Righteousness is good character, and sin is what rankles in your soul and you hate people to see."
"2445","2139 - Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Muhammad narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salama narrated to us, from al-Zubayr Abu Abd al-Salam, from Ayyub ibn Abdullah ibn Mikraz, from Wabisa al-Asadi, he said: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intending to leave nothing of righteousness and sin but ask him about it, so I ended to him with a group of Muslims asking him fatwa around him, so I stepped over them to draw near the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So some of them rebuked me and said: Away, O Wabisa, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So I said: Leave me, for by Allah the most beloved people to me to draw near is the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So he said: Leave Wabisa. Then he said: Call Wabisa. Then he said: Bring Wabisa near. So he brought me near till I sat before him. So he said: Ask or shall I inform you? So I said: No, rather inform me. He said: You came to ask about righteousness and sin. I said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah. So he made tapping with them in my breast and said: O Wabisa, ask your soul, he said it thrice: Righteousness is what the soul is tranquil with and the heart is tranquil with, and sin is what rankles in your soul and hesitates in the breast though people give you fatwa and give you fatwa. Abu Jafar said: So we considered these two hadiths and found in al-Nawwas's thereof that righteousness is good character, and in Wabisa's thereof that righteousness is what the soul is tranquil with. And we found them both returning to one meaning, for when the soul is tranquil, thereof is good character, and sin therewith is its opposite of absence of tranquility from the soul, and therewith is bad character and what hesitates in breasts at its like, and people's fatwa does not take its companion out. And like that is what al-Hasan ibn Ali narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"2446","2140 - As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Shuba narrated to us from Burayd ibn Abi Maryam, from Abu al-Hawra al-Sadi, from al-Hasan ibn Ali, may Allah be pleased with them, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say: Truthfulness is tranquility and lying is doubt. Abu Jafar said: And tranquility therewith is good character, and doubt therewith is bad character and what hesitates in breasts, and people's fatwa does not take it out, so it returned, praise and favor be to Allah, in this chapter from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to some confirming others, not to some contradicting others, and we ask Allah Almighty for guidance."
"2447","Chapter 345 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning Allah's admonisher in the believer's heart. 2141 - Nasr ibn Marzuq, Fahd ibn Sulayman and Harun ibn Kamil narrated to us, they said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Muawiya ibn Salih narrated to me that Abd al-Rahman ibn Jubayr narrated to him, from his father, from Nawwas ibn Siman al-Ansari, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: Allah struck a parable of a straight path, and upon the two sides of the path are walls with opened doors, and upon the doors are lowered curtains, and upon the door of the path is a caller saying: O people, enter the path all and deviate not, and a caller calling from above the path: When one wants, as if meaning a man, to open any of those doors, he says: Woe to you, open it not, for if you open it you will enter it. So the path is Islam, and the curtains are Allah's bounds, and the opened doors are Allah's prohibitions, and that caller upon the head of the path is Allah's Book, and the caller above it, as if meaning the path, is Allah's admonisher in the heart of every Muslim."
"2448","2142 - And Hashim ibn Muhammad al-Ansari, one of the muezzins of Bayt al-Maqdis, Abu al-Darda, and Nasr ibn Marzuq both narrated to us, they said: Adam ibn Abi Iyas narrated to us, from al-Layth ibn Sad, from Muawiya ibn Salih, then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it and added: When a man wants to open any of those doors."
"2449","2143 - And Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Khattab ibn Uthman, Haywa ibn Shurayh and Yazid ibn Abd Rabbih narrated to us, they said: Baqiyya ibn al-Walid narrated to us from Bahir ibn Sad, from Khalid ibn Madan, from Jubayr ibn Nufayr, from al-Nawwas ibn Siman, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah struck a parable of a straight path, upon the two edges of the path are two walls with opened doors, and upon the doors are curtains, and a caller calling upon the head of the path, and a caller calling from above it: And Allah calls to the abode of peace and guides whom He wills to a straight path. So the doors to the two edges of the path are Allah's bounds; none falls into Allah's bounds till Allah's veil is lifted, and the one calling from above it is Allah's admonisher. Abu Jafar said: So we considered this hadith and found all therein disclosed in meaning but what is therein of Allah's admonisher in every Muslim's heart, for we needed to stand upon its reality what it is, so we looked therein and found the admonisher of humans is who forbids people falling into what Allah forbade them, so we understood thereby its like in the Muslim's heart are Allah's proofs forbidding him entering what Allah prevented him and prohibited upon him, and that they are Allah's admonisher in his heart, of the insights He made therein and the knowledge He deposited in him, so their forbidding him that and rebuking him therefrom is as the forbidding of others of people in whose hearts is its like, and we ask Allah for guidance."
"2450","Chapter 346 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning vowing with what is disobedience. 2144 - Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Sulayman al-Wasiti narrated to us, he said: Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, from Ubaydullah ibn Umar, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, from Aisha, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) he said: Whoever vows to obey Allah, let him obey Him, and whoever vows to disobey Allah, let him not disobey Him."
"2451","Hafs said: And I heard Ibn Muhayriz while with Ubaydullah, and he mentioned it from al-Qasim from Aisha, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it, and he said: He expiates for his oath. Abu Jafar said: So we considered the isnad of this hadith and found Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated it from Ubaydullah ibn Umar from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, while its outward was Ubaydullah's hearing it from al-Qasim, so we uncovered that and found he had not heard it from him but took it from another."
"2452","2145 - As Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Idris narrated to us, from Ubaydullah ibn Umar, from Talha ibn Abd al-Malik, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, from Aisha, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever vows to obey Allah, let him obey Him, and whoever vows to disobey Allah, let him not disobey Him. So we understood thereby Ubaydullah ibn Umar only took it from Talha as Malik ibn Anas took it from him from al-Qasim."
"2453","2146 - As Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik informed him from Talha ibn Abd al-Malik al-Ayli, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, from Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with this hadith. Abu Jafar said: Then we considered what Hafs narrated from Ibn Muhayriz and found therein the Messenger's ordering the vower of disobedience with expiation without inability on his part to attain that by his deeds, but for his inability thereof by the Sharia's preventing him therefrom, so we understood thereby the Sharia's preventing him therefrom is as his inability in his vow to do it, and upon him therefor is expiation, and that he is thereby in the meaning of one whose vow dropped and expiation became obligatory upon him in leaving its deed. And we found of what enters this chapter what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of what he ordered Uqba ibn Amir to order his sister therewith."
"2454","2147 - As Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Sulayman al-Wasiti narrated to us, from Sharik ibn Abdullah, from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman, client of Talha's family, from Kurayb, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them, he said: A man came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, my sister vowed to perform hajj walking. So he said: Allah has no need of your sister's hardship in anything; let her perform hajj riding and expiate her oath. And this hadith was narrated from other than this aspect with addition over what this hadith was narrated with."
"2455","2148 - As Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Huyayy ibn Abdullah al-Maafiri informed me, from Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Hubuli, from Uqba ibn Amir al-Juhani that his sister vowed to walk to the Kaba barefoot uncovered, so Uqba mentioned that to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Let her ride, cover, and fast three days. Abu Jafar said: So Uqba's sister's uncovering her head was unlawful upon her, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered her with expiation therefor for the Sharia's preventing her therefrom, and Allah knows best, and of it was also:"
"2456","2149 - What Ali ibn Shayba narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Said informed us, from Ubaydullah ibn Zahr, that he heard Abu Said al-Ruayni mention from Abdullah ibn Malik that he heard Uqba ibn Amir, then he mentioned this hadith letter by letter. Abu Jafar said: The name of Abu Said is Juthul, and he was judge of Ifriqiyya."
"2457","2150 - And of it is what Ubayd ibn Rijal narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Said ibn Abi Ayyub narrated to me, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Abu al-Khayr, from Uqba ibn Amir that his sister vowed to perform hajj walking with her hair uncovered, so Uqba asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he said: Let her ride and fast three days. So in what we narrated was the Messenger's ordering Uqba to order his sister with expiation in what was thereof of disobedience and leaving that disobedience, since the Sharia prevents her therefrom."
"2458","2151 - And we found Ali ibn Shayba narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Hammam ibn Yahya informed us, from Qatada, from Ikrima, from Ibn Abbas that Uqba ibn Amir al-Juhani came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and informed him his sister vowed to walk to the Kaba barefoot with her hair uncovered, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Order her to ride, cover, and offer a hady."
"2459","2152 - And we found Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Isa ibn Ibrahim al-Biraki narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muslim al-Qasmali narrated to us, he said: Matar al-Warraq narrated to us, from Ikrima, from Uqba ibn Amir al-Juhani, he said: My sister vowed to walk to the Kaba, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came upon her and said: What is with her? They said: She vowed to walk to the Kaba. So he said: Allah is in no need of her walking; order her to ride and offer a camel. Then an objector said: You narrated Ibn Abbas's hadith from Qatada from Ikrima from him, and from Matar from Ikrima from him, in what was of the Messenger concerning Uqba ibn Amir's sister in the two aspects you narrated them, upon what is in each of those two aspects. And Hisham ibn Abi Abdillah al-Dastuwai narrated it from Qatada from Ikrima from Ibn Abbas, and did not mention therein the hady in those two hadiths."
"2460","2153 - Then he mentioned what Abu Umayya narrated to us, he said: Muslim ibn Ibrahim al-Azdi narrated to us, he said: Hisham narrated to us, he said: Qatada narrated to us, from Ikrima, from Ibn Abbas, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was informed that Uqba ibn Amir's sister vowed to perform hajj walking, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Allah is in no need of her vow, so order her to ride. He said: And Hisham is more retentive than Hammam, so how did you accept Hammam's addition from Qatada over him? Our answer therein by Allah's guidance and help was that we accepted it since Hammam, had he narrated a hadith alone therewith, would be accepted from him, so likewise his addition in the hadith you mentioned is accepted from him, especially as Matar from Ikrima agreed with him thereupon, and with Allah is guidance. So an asker asked about what occurred in these reports of the Messenger's ordering in some with expiation as the swearer by Allah expiates, and in some with hady as one short in something of his hajj offers for what he shortened therein: Is in everything thereof contradiction or disagreement? So our answer therein by Allah's guidance and help was that there is no contradiction in anything thereof nor disagreement therein, for Uqba ibn Amir's sister had in her vow walking to Allah's House for her hajj, and that was of obediences not disobediences, so it became obligatory upon her, then when she shortened therefrom the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered her with the like of what one short in his hajj of something thereof is ordered, of circumambulation carried with ability upon walking, and it is the hady, and she was in her vow in the meaning of the swearer for her uncovering her hair in her walking, so there was not from her what she swore upon for the Sharia's preventing her therefrom, so she was ordered with expiation therefor as the swearer is ordered with expiation for his oath when he breaks it. And like that is what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of what has."
"2461","2154 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith informed me, from Kab ibn Alqama, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Shimasa, from Uqba ibn Amir, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The expiation of a vow is the expiation of an oath."
"2462","2155 - Abu Jafar said: Yunus said to us: And Ibn Wahb had narrated it to us also, and he said: From Abd al-Rahman ibn Shimasa, from Abu al-Khayr, from Uqba ibn Amir, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said:"
"2463","2156 - And of what Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Abdullah ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash narrated to us, he said: Muhammad al-Thaqafi narrated to us. Abu Jafar said: And he is Muhammad ibn Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, client of al-Mughira ibn Shuba, and more than one of the Egyptians narrated from him, from Kab ibn Alqama, from Abu Khayr, from Uqba ibn Amir al-Juhani, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it. He said: And of what has."
"2464","2157 - Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr narrated to us, he said: Muhammad, client of al-Mughira ibn Shuba, narrated to us. Kab narrated to us, then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it. So all we narrated in this chapter is mention of what became obligatory upon Uqba's sister for her shortening of her walking in her hajj and her shortening of fulfilling her vow for the Sharia's preventing her of fulfilling it, and with Allah is guidance."
"2465","Chapter 347 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: There is no vow in disobedience to Allah, and its expiation is the expiation of an oath. 2158 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Abu Salama, from Aisha, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no vow in disobedience to Allah, and its expiation is the expiation of an oath. Abu Jafar said: And this hadith was irregular for what we mentioned of its kind in the first chapter, except that we found it corrupt in isnad."
"2466","2159 - As Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ayyub ibn Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Uways narrated to me, from Sulayman ibn Bilal, from Muhammad ibn Abi Atiq and Musa ibn Uqbah, from Ibn Shihab, from Sulayman ibn Arqam, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir who used to live in al-Yamamah, who narrated to him that he heard Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman reporting from Aishah that she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no vow in disobedience, and its expiation is the expiation of an oath. So this hadith goes back to Ibn Shihab from Sulayman ibn Arqam, and Sulayman ibn Arqam is not among those whose hadith the people of isnad accept. Had this hadith been sound it would have agreed with what we mentioned of its kind in the chapter before this chapter. And Allah we ask for success."
"2467","Chapter 348 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his saying: There is no vow in anger, and its expiation is the expiation of an oath. 2160 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Jarir ibn Hazim informed us, from Muhammad ibn al-Zubayr al-Tamimi, from his father, from Imran ibn Husayn, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no vow in anger, and its expiation is the expiation of an oath."
"2468","2161 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hassan narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn al-Zubayr al-Hanzali, from his father, from Imran ibn Husayn, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the same."
"2469","2162 - Ahmad ibn Abd al-Mumin al-Marwazi narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn al-Hasan ibn Shaqiq narrated to us, he said: Abbad ibn al-Awwam narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Zubayr narrated to us, from his father, from Imran, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the same. Abu Jafar said: The meaning of 'there is no vow in anger' is: in anger for the sake of Allah, so its meaning goes back to the meaning of the hadith in the chapter before this chapter, except that we examined the isnad of this hadith and found it unsound as well."
"2470","2163 - As Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahhab ibn Ata narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Zubayr al-Hanzali informed us, from his father, from a man, from Imran, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned this hadith."
"2471","2164 - As Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Abd Allah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn al-Zubayr, from his father, from a man, from Imran, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned it. So we concluded that all that was reported in this chapter is defective. Then someone said: Another hadith has been reported on it,"
"2472","2165 - And he mentioned what Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi narrated to us, he said: Harb ibn Shaddad narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated to me, from Muhammad ibn Aban, from al-Qasim, from Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever vows to disobey Allah, let him not disobey Him."
"2473","2166 - And what Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Salamah al-Minqari narrated to us, he said: Aban ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Yahya narrated to us, then he mentioned the same with his isnad. Our answer to him in that, by the success-giving of Allah the Mighty and Majestic and His aid: this hadith is also of unsound isnad, because Muhammad ibn Aban in its isnad is unknown, and it adds to the hadith which al-Zuhri reported from Abu Salamah, whose unsoundness has already become clear, further confusion; for it became at one time from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir from Abu Salamah, and at another time from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir from Muhammad ibn Aban. And Allah we ask for success."
"2474","Chapter 349 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about his command to Abu Israil, when he vowed to stand in the sun and not to speak, what he commanded him in that. 2167 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Said ibn Hammad al-Harrani narrated to us, he said: Jarir ibn Hazim narrated to us, from Ayyub, he said: Ikrimah narrated to me, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was addressing the people on Friday when he saw a man of Quraysh of Banu Amir ibn Luayy called Abu Israil. He said: Is that not Abu Israil? They said: Yes. He said: What is the matter with him? They said: O Messenger of Allah, he has vowed to fast and to stand in the sun and not to speak. He said: Command him to complete his fast, to sit, to seek shade, and to speak."
"2475","2168 - Jafar ibn Muhammad ibn al-Hasan al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn al-Hajjaj al-Sami narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb ibn Khalid narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the same. Then someone said: In this hadith the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded Abu Israil regarding his vow to stand in the sun and not to speak to move away from the sun and to speak, without any expiation he commanded him along with that. So is this contrary to what you reported before of his (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanding whoever vows to disobey Allah the Mighty and Majestic not to disobey Him and to expiate for his oath? Our answer to him in that, by the success-giving of Allah the Mighty and Majestic and His aid: there is nothing in this hadith contrary to what is in the hadith he mentioned, for it is possible that he commanded him with expiation but it was shortened in transmission to us, just as it was shortened in most reports about the one who broke the fast in Ramadan by intercourse with his wife, regarding the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanding him to make up a day in place of the day in which that breaking of fast occurred, for which he commanded him with the expiation he commanded him in it, and it is obligatory upon him without disagreement. And it is possible that worship at that time did not include expiation along with abandoning disobedience in it, then the expiation mentioned in the hadith you cited was later placed in it. And when expiation becomes obligatory by the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanding it in some case, it must be adhered to and obligated upon whoever deserves its obligation until we know of its abrogation. And with Allah the Mighty and Majestic is success."
"2476","Chapter 350 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the dream: how many parts of the parts that are prophethood is it? 2169 - Al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Israil narrated to us, from Simak, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The righteous dream is one part of seventy parts of prophethood."
"2477","2170 - Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Numayr al-Hamdani narrated to us, from Ubayd Allah al-Umari, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the same."
"2478","2171 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Ubayd ibn Ishaq al-Attar narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Muawiyah narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Amr ibn Abd Allah al-Asamm, from Abd Allah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the same."
"2479","2172 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Ubayd Allah ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Shayban al-Nahwi narrated to us, from Firas, from Atiyyah, from Abu Said al-Khudri, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the same. Then someone said: You have reported these reports as you reported, in which is that the dream is one part of seventy parts of prophethood, while you report from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what contradicts them, that the dream is one part of forty-six parts of prophethood."
"2480","2173 - And he mentioned what Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Abi Arubah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Anas ibn Malik, from Ubadah ibn al-Samit, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The dream of the Muslim is one part of forty-six parts of prophethood."
"2481","2174 - And what Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Malik ibn Anas narrated to us, from Ishaq ibn Abd Allah ibn Abi Talhah, from Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The good dream of the righteous man is one part of forty-six parts of prophethood."
"2482","2175 - And what Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Bakr al-Sahmi narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Hassan narrated to us; and Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Hisham informed us, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the same."
"2483","2176 - And what Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: al-Khadir ibn Muhammad ibn Shuja narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Abu al-Zinad, from al-Araj, from Sulayman ibn Arib, he said: I heard Abu Hurayrah saying to Ibn Abbas: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The righteous dream of the servant is one part of forty-six parts of prophethood. Then Ibn Abbas said: Of fifty."
"2484","2177 - And what Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Said narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Amr, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The dream of the righteous man, which he sees or which is seen for him, is one part of forty-six parts of prophethood."
"2485","2178 - And what Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Mushir al-Ghassani narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hamzah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Ubaydah narrated to me, from Abu Ubayd Allah - Abu Jafar said: he is the scribe of Abu al-Darda - Abu Mushir said, and he is Muslim ibn Mishkam, that he narrated to him from Awf ibn Malik al-Ashjai, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: Dreams are three: among them is frightening from Satan to grieve those who believe; among them is what a man is concerned with in his wakefulness so he sees it in his sleep; and among them is one part of forty-six parts of prophethood. So I said: Did you hear it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: I heard it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). This questioner said: This is severe confusion: at one time they report that it is one part of seventy parts of prophethood, at another time they report that it is one part of forty-six parts of prophethood, and at another time they report from Ibn Abbas what he could not have said except as tawqif, that it is one part of fifty parts of prophethood. Our answer to him in that, by the success-giving of Allah the Mighty and Majestic and His aid: all the reports we reported on this bear what involves no contradiction, namely that the dream is a part of parts of prophethood made as glad tidings."
"2486","2179 - As Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abi Yazid, from his father, from Siba ibn Thabit, from Umm Kuraz al-Kabiyyah: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Prophethood has gone and the mubashshirat (glad tidings) remain."
"2487","2180 - As Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Abu Salih, from Ata ibn Yasar, from a shaykh of the people of Egypt, from Abu al-Darda (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: I asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about His saying, the Mighty and Majestic: {For them are glad tidings in the life of this world and in the Hereafter}. He said: The righteous dream which the Muslim sees or which is seen for him; {and in the Hereafter}, he said: Paradise. So it is possible that Allah the Mighty and Majestic had made it in the beginning one part of seventy parts of prophethood, so what is given to whoever sees it or for whom it is seen thereby is that part of prophethood as a favor from Him upon him and a gift from Him to him, then He increased him after that by making what He gives him thereby one part of fifty parts of prophethood, then He increased him after that by making what He gives him thereby one part of forty-six parts of prophethood. If someone says: Why was it not possible that the smaller is the abrogator of the larger? Our answer to him in that, by the success-giving of Allah the Mighty and Majestic and His aid: Allah the Mighty and Majestic does not strip from His servants a favor He favored them with except due to an event they newly do by which they deserve that from Him, as Allah the Mighty and Majestic said: {For the wrongdoing of those who became Jews We forbade them good things that had been lawful to them}, the verse; and as Allah the Mighty and Majestic said: {That is because Allah would never change a blessing He bestowed upon a people until they change what is in themselves}. So there was not from those upon whom He the Mighty and Majestic bestowed much of the parts of prophethood anything by which they deserve deprivation of that and return to few of its parts. And Allah we ask for success."
"2488","Chapter 351 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about whoever commits a sin in this world and is punished for it, and about whoever commits a sin in this world and Allah the Mighty and Majestic conceals it for him in this world and pardons him. 2181 - Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Yunus ibn Abi Ishaq, from his father, from Abu Juhayfah, from Ali (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever commits a sin in this world and is punished for it, Allah the Mighty and Majestic is too generous to double His punishment upon His servant; and whoever commits a sin in this world and Allah the Mighty and Majestic conceals it for him and pardons him, Allah is too generous to go back on something He has pardoned."
"2489","2182 - al-Husayn ibn Ghulayb narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Yusuf al-Azraq narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Sulayman, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu Juhayfah, from Ali ibn Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with him) that he said: Shall I not narrate to you a hadith which every Muslim must retain? We said: Will you not narrate it to us? He narrated it to us at the beginning of the day and we forgot it at the end of the day, so we returned to him and said: The hadith which you mentioned that every Muslim must retain - we have forgotten it, so repeat it. He said: There is no Muslim who commits a sin and Allah takes him for it in this world and then punishes him in the Hereafter except that Allah the Mighty and Majestic is too great and too generous to return to his punishment on the Day of Resurrection; and there is no Muslim servant who commits a sin and Allah the Mighty and Majestic pardons him except that Allah the Mighty and Majestic is too forbearing and too generous to go back on it on the Day of Resurrection. Then he recited: {And whatever misfortune befalls you is for what your hands earned, and He pardons much}. Abu Jafar said: In this hadith is what indicates that Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) did not say what is in it by deduction, but said it as tawqif, so it thereby joins the hadith before it. Then someone said: How is it permissible to attribute to Allah the Mighty and Majestic pardon of a sin in this world, then attribute to Him the Mighty and Majestic that His leaving punishment for it in the Hereafter is generosity from Him, when He has already pardoned it in this world, and it is not permissible that He pardons it in this world then punishes for it in the Hereafter? And if that is so, His leaving punishment for it in the Hereafter would not be generosity, for generosity is only the generous leaving what he is entitled to do. Our answer to him in that, by the success-giving of Allah the Mighty and Majestic and His aid: it is possible that servants have sins for which they deserve from Allah the Mighty and Majestic punishment in this world and punishment in the Hereafter together, like what He the Mighty and Majestic said in the verse of the muh­aribun: {The recompense of those who wage war against Allah and His Messenger and strive in the land with corruption is that they be killed or crucified or their hands and feet be cut off from opposite sides or they be exiled from the land; that is disgrace for them in this world, and for them in the Hereafter is a great punishment}. So that worldly punishment, when carried out upon the sinners, is not repeated upon them in the Hereafter, and there are upon them in the Hereafter other punishments besides it; and when Allah the Mighty and Majestic conceals those sins for them in this world and pardons them by leaving taking them with the worldly punishments upon them therein, He does not thereby drop from them the otherworldly punishments upon them therein, and their affairs are to Him the Mighty and Majestic: if He wills He punishes them for them, and if He wills He pardons them. And like that is what Ubadah ibn al-Samit (may Allah be pleased with him) reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"2490","2183 - As Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Idris, from Ubadah ibn al-Samit (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: We were with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in a gathering and he said to us: Pledge to me that you will not associate anything with Allah, and he recited the verse to them; whoever of you fulfills, his reward is upon Allah the Mighty and Majestic; whoever commits something of that and is punished for it, it is expiation for him; and whoever commits something of that and Allah the Mighty and Majestic conceals it for him, then he is to Allah the Mighty and Majestic: if He wills He forgives him, and if He wills He punishes him."
"2491","2184 - And as Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, from al-Thawri, from Khalid al-Hadhdha, from Abu Qilabah, from Abu al-Ashath al-Sanani, from Ubadah ibn al-Samit (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took from us something as he took from the women in the Quran: {They pledge to you that they will not associate anything with Allah, nor steal, nor commit adultery}, the verse. Whoever of you commits a hadd and his punishment is hastened for him, it is his expiation; and whoever has it deferred, his affair is to Allah the Mighty and Majestic: if He wills He forgives him, and if He wills He punishes him. Abu Jafar said: The punishment with which Allah the Mighty and Majestic punishes for that in the Hereafter, and the pardon of it, is according to what He the Mighty and Majestic wills to run their affairs upon, like what is in the hadith of Ali which we reported; and what He the Mighty and Majestic inflicts upon them in the Hereafter is other than what He inflicted upon them in this world, if He inflicted it upon them therein, and other than what He pardoned them of in this world, if He pardoned them of it in this world, whether of pardon or concealment or punishment from Him the Mighty and Majestic in that. And among what also enters this chapter is what was reported from Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her) from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"2492","2185 - As Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us. And as Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid Hisham ibn Abd al-Malik al-Tayalisi narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, he said: I heard Ishaq ibn Abd Allah ibn Abi Talhah, he said: Shaybah al-Khudri narrated to us that he witnessed Urwah ibn al-Zubayr narrating to Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz from Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: Three I testify to, and the fourth, if I testified I would hope not to sin: Allah the Mighty and Majestic does not make one who has a share in Islam like one who has no share; and the shares of Islam are fasting, prayer, and charity; and Allah the Mighty and Majestic does not take charge of a man in this world then entrust him in the Hereafter to another; and no man loves a people except he comes with them on the Day of Resurrection; and the fourth: Allah the Mighty and Majestic does not conceal for a servant in this world except He conceals for him in the Hereafter. Abu Jafar said: Abu Ubayd mentioned in his book on lineage, in the lineages of Banu Muharib ibn Khasafah, saying: among them Malik ibn Tarif ibn Khalaf ibn Muharib ibn Khasafah, and this Malik is al-Khadir, because Malik was dark-skinned, so his child was therefore called al-Khadir. Abu Jafar said: What is in this hadith in his saying - and the fourth: Allah does not conceal for a servant in this world except He conceals for him in the Hereafter - is what the servants must hold of good opinion of their Lord the Mighty and Majestic regarding what He undertakes of their affairs in the Hereafter, for He is the One worthy of piety and worthy of forgiveness, so what is hoped from Him regarding what He concealed for them in this world of what did not take them out of Islam is that He will not take them for it in the Hereafter. And in the hadith of Ubadah is a phrase that must be noted: his saying, whoever commits something of that and is punished for it, it is expiation for him - that is not upon whoever commits something of all that is in it, for in it is their pledging the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) upon what is in the verse taken upon the women, namely His saying the Mighty and Majestic: {They pledge to you that they will not associate anything with Allah, nor steal, nor commit adultery, nor kill their children, nor bring slander they fabricate between their hands and feet, nor disobey you in what is right}. So his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - what is in the hadith of Ubadah of expiation and of concealment which may be accompanied by pardon - refers only to what is besides shirk, for Allah the Mighty and Majestic said: {Allah does not forgive that partners be associated with Him, and He forgives less than that for whom He wills}. And Allah the Mighty and Majestic we ask for success."
"2493","Chapter 352 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his saying: The imam is a guarantor and the muadhdhin is entrusted. 2186 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), raising the hadith, he said: The imam is a guarantor and the muadhdhin is entrusted. O Allah, make the imams firm and forgive the muadhdhins."
"2494","2187 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Surayj ibn al-Numan al-Jawhari narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, from al-Amash, he said: Abu Salih narrated to us, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the same."
"2495","2188 - And Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Umayyah ibn Bistam narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn al-Qasim narrated to us, from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih, from al-Amash, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the same."
"2496","2189 - Ahmad ibn Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Rahim al-Barqi narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Jafar narrated to us, he said: Suhayl ibn Abi Salih narrated to me, from al-Amash, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the same."
"2497","2190 - Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Yazid al-Makki narrated to us, he said: Muhriz ibn Salamah narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Abi Hazim narrated to us, from Suhayl, from Sulayman al-Amash, then he mentioned the same with his isnad."
"2498","2191 - Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Sulayman, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the same."
"2499","2192 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Hafs ibn Ghiyath al-Nakhai narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, from Sulayman, he said: Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, then he mentioned the same. It was said to him: You mentioned it from Abu Salih. He said: Yes, so take it from him. Then someone said: This hadith is impugned, for some people mention that al-Amash did not hear it from Abu Salih, but took it from an unknown man from him."
"2500","2193 - And he mentioned what Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Shuja ibn al-Walid narrated to us, from Sulayman ibn Mihran, he said: I was narrated from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, and he mentioned the same. Our answer to him in that, by the success-giving of Allah the Mighty and Majestic and His aid: Shuja did report it from al-Amash as he mentioned, but Hushaym, who is above him, said in it, from al-Amash, he said: Abu Salih narrated to us; and Allah knows best the reality in that. And we found it from the hadith of Abu Ishaq from Abu Salih."
"2501","2194 - As Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Muawiyah narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The imam is entrusted; O Allah, guide the imams and forgive the muadhdhins. And we found it also from Abu Salih from Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her) by another route."
"2502","2195 - As Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Yazid al-Muqri narrated to us, he said: Haywah ibn Shurayh narrated to us, he said: Nafi ibn Sulayman informed us that Muhammad ibn Abi Salih informed him from his father, from Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her), she said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The imam is a guarantor and the muadhdhin is entrusted; so may Allah guide the imam and pardon the muadhdhin. Abu Jafar said: Thus it became sound for us to bring this chapter by these routes. Then we considered the meaning of his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): the muadhdhin is entrusted, and its meaning in our view - and Allah knows best - is that he is entrusted over the times in which he calls the adhan, so people act upon his adhan for their prayers and for their breaking fast from their fasting, and for other matters of their worship which his adhan indicates the practice in. And we considered his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): and the imam is a guarantor, and its meaning in our view - and Allah knows best - is that the prayer of those led is guaranteed by his prayer, in its soundness and its corruption and in his forgetfulness in it. Do you not see that if he led them without ablution, or while junub while they are pure, or with his awrah uncovered while they are covered, deliberately, there is no disagreement among the people of knowledge that his prayer is corrupt; and by analogy, as that is so in deliberate case, it is the same in forgetfulness, just as his ruling is the same regarding himself in that, in the corruption of his prayer in deliberate and forgetful cases, so their ruling is the same in their prayer behind him following him, in corruption in deliberate and forgetful cases. So just as in the deliberate case it corrupts their prayer, in the forgetful case it corrupts their prayer. And Allah we ask for success."
"2503","Chapter 353 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his saying: Whoever leads the people and completes the prayer and hits the time, it is for him and for them; and if he falls short of that in anything, it is upon him and not upon them. 2196 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub informed me, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Harmalah, from Abu Ali al-Hamdani - I say: he is Thumamah ibn Shufi - he said: I heard Uqbah ibn Amir al-Juhani saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whoever leads the people and hits the time and completes the prayer, it is for him and for them; and whoever falls short of that in anything, it is upon him and not upon them. Abu Jafar said: The people of knowledge of hadith say the correct in the isnad of this hadith is that it is from Yahya ibn Ayyub, from Harmalah ibn Imran, from Abu Ali al-Hamdani, for Abd al-Rahman ibn Harmalah is not known to have heard from Abu Ali al-Hamdani; and what Said ibn Kathir ibn Ufayr reported of this hadith from Yahya ibn Ayyub indicates what they said of that."
"2504","2197 - As al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Kathir ibn Ufayr narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to us, from Harmalah ibn Imran, from Abu Ali al-Hamdani, he said: I heard Uqbah ibn Amir saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying, then he mentioned the same exactly."
"2505","2198 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub informed me, from al-Ala ibn Kathir, from Dawud ibn Ayyub, from Said al-Maqburi, that Abu Shurayh al-Adawi said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The imam is a shield; if he completes, it is for you and for him, and if he falls short, the shortcoming is upon him and completeness is for you. Abu Jafar said: This Abu Shurayh is attributed by some to Adi, a branch of Khuzaah, and by some to Kab, also a branch of Khuzaah; his name, as al-Waqidi mentioned, is Khulayd ibn Amr, and as Ibn Abi Dawud mentioned from Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Numayr: Kab ibn Amr. Then both agreed that his death was in the year sixty-eight; al-Waqidi said in Madinah. Then someone said: You reported in the chapter before this chapter from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: the muadhdhin is entrusted, and the muadhdhin is the one to whom the iqamah belongs, not the imam; so how did you accept what you mentioned in this chapter of what you attributed to the imam, of what is for him and what is upon him? Our answer to him in that - by the success-giving of Allah the Mighty and Majestic and His aid - is that the adhan belongs to the muadhdhin as he mentioned, not to the imam, while the iqamah is contrary to what he mentioned: it belongs to the imam, not to the muadhdhin."
"2506","As Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Said al-Qattan narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Mansur, from Hilal ibn Yasaf, from Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami, from Ali (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The muadhdhin has most right to the adhan and the imam has most right to the iqamah. Abu Jafar said: So the iqamah for prayer belongs to the imam, not to the muadhdhin; we thus understood that seeking its time belongs to the imam, not to the muadhdhin. So the sin in falling short of it is upon him, not upon the muadhdhin, just as the sin in falling short of seeking the time of the adhan is upon the muadhdhin, not upon the imam. And in what we mentioned is clarification of what this questioner asked about. And Allah, glory and exaltation to Him, we ask for success."
"2507","Chapter 354 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about his answering one who said to him, when he said about the adhan what he said: You left us while we compete over the adhan - what he answered him with. 2199 - Ahmad ibn Abd al-Mumin al-Marwazi narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn al-Hasan ibn Shaqiq narrated to us, he said: Abu Hamzah narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The imam is a guarantor and the muadhdhin is entrusted; so may Allah guide the imams and forgive the muadhdhins. They said: O Messenger of Allah, you left us while we compete over the adhan. He said: Nay, after you there is a time in which your muadhdhins will be your lowliest. Abu Jafar said: This, in our view - and Allah knows best - is upon the adhan being a noble station which the nobles should have been its people; so he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed with what he informed, meaning they abandon it until those lower than them undertake it, so he becomes noble and his rank rises above their ranks. Like what was reported from Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him)."
"2508","Of what Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Kathir narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ismail, from Shubayl ibn Awf, he said: Umar said: Who are your muadhdhins today? They said: Our mawlas and our slaves. He said: That is a great deficiency upon you. And what was reported of what enters this chapter."
"2509","And it is what Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Bayan al-Bajali, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, he said: Umar said: If I could bear the adhan along with the caliphate I would call it. And this is like what is in the hadith of Abu Hurayrah of his saying: Draw near, O sons of Farrukh, for the Arabs have turned away - i.e. from knowledge; and we shall mention that later in this book of ours, Allah willing. And like that is what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the people of the Quran, of Allah the Mighty and Majestic raising them thereby, and abasing others by abandoning it."
"2510","As Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud and Abu Amir narrated to us, they said: Ibrahim ibn Sad narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, he said: Abu al-Tufayl Amir ibn Wathilah al-Laythi narrated to me that Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) employed Nafi ibn Abd al-Harith over Makkah, then met him at Usfan and said: Whom did you leave over the valley? He said: I left Ibn Abza over them. He said: And who is Ibn Abza? He said: A mawla of ours. He said: You left a mawla over them? He said: O Commander of the Believers, he is a reciter of the Book of Allah, learned in the obligations of Allah, a judge. Umar said: Allah the Mighty and Majestic raises by this Book peoples and abases thereby others."
"2511","And as Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Salih al-Wuhazi narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Yahya al-Kalbi narrated to us, he said: al-Zuhri narrated to us, he said: Amir ibn Wathilah narrated to me that Nafi ibn Abd al-Harith met Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) at Usfan, then he mentioned this hadith. And like that is what was reported from Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) of what he said only as tawqif."
"2512","And as Yazid narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Abu al-Tufayl, he said: Nafi ibn Abd al-Harith left Ibn Abza over Makkah, and he was of the mawlas; Umar said to him: Whom did you leave over Makkah? He said: I left Ibn Abza. He said: Do you leave a man of the mawlas? He said: I left no one more learned in the Book of Allah the Mighty and Majestic than him. He said: If you said that, Allah the Mighty and Majestic raises by the Quran men and abases thereby men, and I hope he is among those raised by the Quran. Abu Jafar said: So Allah the Mighty and Majestic raises by the Quran one who was not noble before that; likewise it is possible He raises by the adhan one who was not noble before that. And the meaning of his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): there will come a time in which your muadhdhins are your lowliest, is not upon the meaning that they are lowly in their lineages, nor lowly in other of their matters, but they are lowly compared to one higher than them in lineage, of those who should have preceded them to what they became of its people, and that he should be the one in charge of what he left to them; so when he leaves it to them he is lowered thereby and they are raised over him by undertaking it, though they became its people instead of him. And Allah we ask for success."
"2513","Chapter 355 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about his approving the judgment of Ali ibn Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with him) concerning the people who fell into the pit dug in Yemen, clinging to one another until their death resulted from that. 2200 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan Malik ibn Ismail al-Nahdi narrated to us, he said: Israil ibn Yunus narrated to us, from Simak ibn Harb, from Hanash, and he is Ibn al-Mutamir, from Ali (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent me to Yemen, and I found a clan of the Arab clans who had dug - or he said: had prepared - a pit for a lion, and caught it. While they were looking into it, a man fell and clung to another, then the other fell and clung to another, then the other clung to another, until they became four in it. The lion wounded all of them, then a man reached it and killed it, and all of them died of their wounds. The guardians of the last rose against the guardians of the first and took up arms to fight. Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) came to them at the subsiding of that and said: Do you want to fight while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is alive and I am beside you? If you fought you would kill more than what you differ over. I shall judge between you with a judgment: if you accept the judgment, well; otherwise some of you shall restrain from others until you come to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and he is the one who judges between you; whoever transgresses after that has no right. Gather from the tribes who attended the well a quarter of the blood-money, a third of the blood-money, half the blood-money, and the complete blood-money: for the first a quarter of the blood-money, for he perished with three above him; for the one next to him a third of the blood-money, for two perished above him; for the third half the blood-money, for one perished above him; and for the fourth the complete blood-money. They refused to accept, so they came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and met him at the Station of Ibrahim (peace be upon him) and told him the story. He said: I shall judge between you, and he wrapped himself in his cloak. A man of the people said: Ali has already judged between us. When they told him the story, he approved it."
"2514","2201 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Simak ibn Harb, from Hanash ibn al-Mutamir, he said: A pit was dug for a lion in Yemen, and the lion fell into it. In the morning people crowded pushing at its edge, and a man fell into it and clung to another, and the other clung to another, and the other clung to another, so four fell into it and all perished. The people did not know what to do. Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) came and said: If you wish I shall judge between you with a judgment that will be a barrier between you until you come to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: I place the blood-money upon those who attended the well: I make for the first who fell into the well a quarter of the blood-money, for the second a third of the blood-money, for the third half the blood-money, and for the fourth the complete blood-money. They turned away from that until they came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and informed him of the judgment of Ali (may Allah be pleased with him), and he approved the judgment. Abu Jafar said: We examined this hadith to understand the aspect in which Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) judged what he judged of what is mentioned in it, Allah willing. We found in the hadith of Rawh that those on the edge of the pit were pushing one another until they fell into it. And we found in the hadith of Fahd the falling of some upon others, for in it: for the first a quarter of the blood-money because three perished above him, for the one next to him a third because two perished above him, for the third half because one perished above him. So we understood by what is in the hadith of Rawh that those on the pit were offenders against the fallen, and it is possible the fallen were also entangled together. The first of them fell by dragging the one next to him, dragging the other two next to him, since some were entangled with others, so the dragging of the one who dragged the first of them was a dragging from him of the rest of them. The death of the first of them was from the push of those on the edge of the pit pushing him into the pit, and from the falling of three fallen men upon him by his dragging them upon himself, so he died by the four things: one of them the push whose doers among the people on the edge of the pit are unknown, so its ruling goes back to the ruling of the push of one man; and the weight of three men whom he dragged upon himself until they fell upon him. So a quarter of his blood-money was due to him for the push, and three quarters of his blood-money fell since he was the cause of the falling of the three men who fell upon him. And we found the second of the fallen dead from the push unknown as to its doers among the men on the edge of the pit, and from his dragging two men upon himself until he died from their weight upon him, and from his falling into the pit; so a third of his blood-money was due to him for the push upon its people, and what remained of his blood-money of what he was its cause was void. And we found the third likewise perished by the push unknown as to its people and by his dragging the fourth upon himself, so half his blood-money was due to him for the push, and half his blood-money was void because he was the cause of the perishing of what perished of it by his dragging whom he dragged upon himself. And we found the fourth perished from the push unknown as to its doers, not from other than it, so all his blood-money was due to him upon those upon whom it was due. If someone says: How did what you mentioned become obligatory upon those who pushed, while you know the push by which that falling occurred was only from someone present among those on the pit, not from all of them? It would have been proper, since that present one is unknown, to make the obligation in that void, since it is not known upon whom it is. Our answer to him in that, by the success-giving of Allah the Mighty and Majestic and His aid: the matter in that is not as he mentioned; rather the ruling in that goes back to a group who gathered and fought and dispersed from a slain one among them, not known who of them killed him, so his blood-money is upon all their aqilahs, as the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) placed the blood-money of the slain man of the Ansar found at Khaybar, not known who killed him, upon the Jews who were at Khaybar then, since Khaybar was their abode. Like that are these fighters who attended the place in which they fought, whose hands were upon it to the exclusion of others: whoever is struck therein slain of those unknown who killed him is upon all of them, upon their aqilahs. If he says: In the hadith of Fahd which you mentioned: the lion wounded all of them and they all died of their wounds, so in that is what indicates the ruling of their death is from the wounds the lion inflicted on them, not from other than it. Our answer to him in that - by the success-giving of Allah the Mighty and Majestic and His aid - is that the cause of the lion wounding them was the push by which their falling into the pit occurred, and the weight of some upon others, until their death resulted thereby by the lion wounding them. Like that is as a man who pushes a man into a well until he falls in it upon a stone and dies from his falling upon that stone, or there is a knife in it and he dies from his falling upon that knife: the ruling in the loss of his life is that the obligation in it is upon whoever was the cause of his death by what he died of what we mentioned, to the exclusion of other than it. And in this ruling is what refutes what al-Awzai used to say about one who kills himself by way of a mistake from him upon it, that his blood-money is upon his aqilah as it would be upon it had a man other than him killed him; and we have not found this saying from anyone of the people of knowledge besides him. And Allah the Exalted we ask for success."
"2515","Chapter 356 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his answering Sad ibn Abi Waqqas when he asked him: Who are the people most severe in trial? 2202 - Nassar ibn Harb al-Mismai al-Basri narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Asim, from Musab ibn Sad, from Sad, he said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, which people are most severe in trial? He said: The prophets, the blessings of Allah upon them, then the righteous, then the most excellent then the most excellent. A man is tried according to his religion - or he said: according to his religion - so if he is firm in religion his trial is severe, and if there is softness in his religion he is tried according to that. Trial does not leave the servant until he walks with no sin upon him."
"2516","2203 - al-Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us; and Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, they said: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from Asim ibn Abi al-Nujud, from Musab ibn Sad, from Sad, he said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, who are the people most severe in trial? He said: The prophets, the blessings of Allah upon them, then the most excellent then the most excellent. A man is tried according to his religion: if there is firmness in his religion his trial is increased, and if there is softness in his religion it is lightened for him. Trial does not cease with the servant until he walks upon the earth with no sin upon him."
"2517","2204 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Yaqub ibn Ishaq al-Hadrami narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah and Hammad ibn Zayd both narrated to us, from Asim ibn Bahdalah, from Musab ibn Sad, from his father, he said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, then he mentioned the same."
"2518","2205 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Rabi al-Zahrani narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, he said: Asim narrated to us, from Musab ibn Sad, from his father, then he mentioned similar, and said: Until he walks upon the earth with no sin upon him. Hammad said: Asim hamzated it."
"2519","2206 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: al-Hasan ibn Musa al-Ashyab narrated to us, he said: Shayban, and he is al-Nahwi, narrated to us, from Asim ibn Abi al-Nujud, then he mentioned the same with his isnad."
"2520","2207 - Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhammad ibn al-Mughirah al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: al-Minjar ibn al-Harith al-Tamimi al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: Sharik ibn Abd Allah al-Nakhai narrated to us, from Simak, from Musab ibn Sad, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: It was said: Which people are most severe in trial? He said: The prophets, the blessings of Allah upon them, then the most excellent then the most excellent; then people are tried according to their religions: if a man is good in religion his trial is severe, and if there is something in his religion he is tried according to that. Trial does not leave the servant until he walks upon the earth with no sin upon him. Abu Jafar said: We examined this hadith and found in it, in the answer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Sad (may Allah be pleased with him) about what he asked him of who are the people most severe in trial - the prophets, then the most excellent then the most excellent; a man is tried according to his religion: if there is firmness in his religion his trial is increased, and if there is softness in his religion it is lightened for him. We thus understood that that saying of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in describing religions with firmness and softness does not go back upon the prophets, the blessings of Allah upon them, for there is no softness in their religions; rather that refers only to others besides them of those mentioned with them. And in this hadith is that sins are removed from the Muslims besides them by the trial with which they are tried in this world; and that, in our view - and Allah knows best - is for their reckoning hope of reward in that and their patience upon it, so their sins are purged from them thereby, if they are people of sins. And the prophets, the blessings of Allah upon them, are in that unlike them, for they have no sins. And with Allah is success."
"2521","Chapter 357 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about what afflicted him of fever, that he had in it two rewards. 2208 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Qabisah ibn Uqbah narrated to us, from Sufyan, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim al-Taymi, from al-Harith ibn Suwayd, from Abd Allah, he said: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his illness while he was suffering severe fever, and I said: O Messenger of Allah, you suffer severe fever; you have two rewards. He said: Yes. No Muslim is afflicted with harm except his sins fall from him as the leaves of the tree fall. Abu Jafar said: In this hadith is that Abd Allah addressed the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that for the fever he suffers he has two rewards, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not deny that. That indicates reward was indeed written in the fever he suffered."
"2522","2209 - And Ahmad ibn Dawud ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Ubayd Allah ibn Muhammad al-Taymi narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muslim al-Qasmali informed us, he said: al-Amash narrated to us, from Ibrahim al-Taymi, from al-Harith ibn Suwayd, from Abd Allah ibn Masud, he said: I entered upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was feverish, and I touched him with my hand and said: O Messenger of Allah, you suffer severe fever. He said: Yes, I suffer severe fever as two men of you suffer. I said: Then you have two rewards. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No Muslim is afflicted with harm of illness or other than it except Allah removes - as if he meant his sins - as the tree sheds its leaves."
"2523","2210 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Hisham ibn Sad informed me, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Abu Said al-Khudri (may Allah be pleased with him) that he entered upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was feverish, with a velvet cloth upon him; he placed his hand upon him and felt its heat above the cloth. Abu Said said: How severe is the heat of your fever, O Messenger of Allah! The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: We are like that: trial is intensified upon us and reward is multiplied for us. Abu Jafar said: We examined these reports and found that since the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had no sins to be removed from him by what afflicted him in his body of fever, there was made for him in place of that of reward what was made for him therein of what is mentioned in these reports. And what is in the hadith of Abu Said - of the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in answer to him about what he asked him therein: we are like that, trial is intensified upon us and reward is multiplied for us - indicates he meant thereby himself and the rest of the prophets of Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, the blessings of Allah upon them, since they have no sins nor faults. And Allah, glory and exaltation to Him, we ask for success."
"2524","Chapter 358 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about what befalls those besides the prophets, the blessings of Allah upon them, in their bodies: are they rewarded for that or not? 2211 - Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Muslim ibn Ibrahim al-Azdi narrated to us, he said: Aban ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated to us, from Abu Qilabah, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Shaybah, from Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her) that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was struck by pain and tossed upon his bed. Aishah said to him: O Prophet of Allah, had one of us done this you would have blamed him. He said: The believers are treated severely, and no believer is struck by a calamity or pain except Allah raises him thereby a degree and removes from him thereby a sin."
"2525","2212 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, from Ali ibn al-Mubarak, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Qilabah, that Abd al-Rahman ibn Shaybah, keeper of the Kabah, narrated to him that Aishah informed him, then he mentioned the same. So in what we reported in this hadith is the informing of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that reward is written for whoever is struck by calamity or pain: Allah the Mighty and Majestic raises him thereby a degree, along with removing from him thereby a sin."
"2526","2213 - Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Bakr al-Sahmi narrated to us, he said: Sinan ibn Rabiah narrated to us, from Thabit al-Bunani, from Ubayd ibn Umayr, from Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No Muslim is tried with a trial in his body except Allah writes for him in his illness every righteous deed he used to do in his health."
"2527","2214 - Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Jabbar al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hassan narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: al-Awwam ibn Hawshab informed us, from Ibrahim ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Saksaki, from Abu Burdah ibn Abi Musa, from his father, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - not once nor twice - saying: Whoever used to do a deed and illness or travel busies him from it, there is written for him the righteous of what he used to do while healthy and resident. So a denier denied these reports and said: How is it permissible that reward be written for a man without his deed, by which he deserves that reward? Our answer to him in that, by the success-giving of Allah the Mighty and Majestic, is that reward is written for him for his good intention along with what befell him, his patience upon it, and his surrendering therein the affair to the One who tried him with it; so Allah thanks him for that and rewards him for it. And among what indicates that is what is in the two hadiths of Ibn Masud and Abu Said, of the answer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to them, or of his acceptance of whoever said to him of them that reward is multiplied for you - of what indicates that the multiplication for him is giving him upon what is with him the like of what He gives others upon what befalls them of it of reward, plus the like of it upon it. And this is what the Madinans and the Kufans all reported. He said: Ibn Masud is reported to have said what refutes that; and he mentioned what."
"2528","Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Jami, meaning Ibn Shaddad, from Umarah ibn Umayr, from Abu Mamar, he said: Abd Allah said: Pain is not written as reward - so that was harder - or more grievous - upon us; and when he narrated to us a hadith we would not ask him about its explanation until he clarified it. He said: But Allah expiates sins thereby. Our answer to him in that, by the success-giving of Allah the Mighty and Majestic and His aid: illnesses and pains are not written as reward as Ibn Masud, may Allah have mercy on him, said; rather sins are removed thereby and degrees are raised thereby, combining both matters together, not singling out one to the exclusion of the other. And it is possible Ibn Masud (may Allah be pleased with him) meant thereby the differing rulings of people in them: among them one who has sins engrossing his reward upon them, so his reward upon them and his wage therein is the removal of his sins, not other than it; and besides him, of those with no sins, like the prophets, the blessings of Allah upon them, or like others besides them whose reward exceeds the removal of his sins, there is written for him of reward what there are no sins of his for what is written for him to be expiation for. And it would have behooved this denier of what he denied of what is in these reports not to deny it, since he found all the Muslims consoling one another upon their calamities with their loved ones that Allah magnify their rewards for that - and that is of what they have no action in, but they have in it patience and reckoning hope of reward. Like that is for them in illnesses and pains."
"2529","And Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Yaqub ibn Ishaq al-Hadrami narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Umarah ibn Umayr, from Abu Mamar, from Amr ibn Shurahbil, he said: Abd Allah said: Pain is not written as reward, but sins are removed thereby; reward is for deed. Abu Jafar said: The speech in the hadith of Abd Allah before this has sufficed us from speech on this, except what is in this hadith of his saying: reward is in deed. Its aspect in our view - and Allah knows best - is that deed does not remove sins thereby, but reward is written thereby, whether its doer has sins or has no sins; and it is unlike illnesses and pains by which sins are removed, if there are sins there, and reward is written thereby, if there are no sins there. And Allah knows best, and with Allah is success."
"2530","Chapter 359 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the removal of sins. 2215 - Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Hassan informed us, from Wasil, freedman of Abu Uyaynah, from al-Walid ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Iyad ibn Ghutayf, he said; and Jarir ibn Hazim informed us, he said: Bashshar ibn Abi Sayf narrated to us, from al-Walid ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Iyad ibn Ghutayf, he said: We entered upon Abu Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrah (may Allah be pleased with him) with his wife Tuhayfah beside him and his face toward the wall. We said: How did Abu Ubaydah spend the night? She said: He spent the night with reward. He turned to us and said: I did not spend the night with reward. That grieved us and we fell silent. He said: Will you not ask me about what I said? We said: That would not please us, so we ask you about it. He said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whomever Allah tries with a trial in his body, it is a removal for him."
"2531","2216 - Ali narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan Muhammad ibn Mutarrif al-Laythi narrated to us, from Abu al-Husayn, from Abu Salih al-Ashari, from Abu Umamah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Fever is a blast of Hell; whatever of it strikes the believer is his share of the Fire."
"2532","2217 - Ali narrated to us, he said: Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Ismah ibn Salim al-Hunai - and he was truthful and intelligent - narrated to us, he said: al-Ashath ibn Jabir al-Hudai narrated to me, from Shahr ibn Hawshab, from Abu Rayhanah al-Ansari, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Fever is of the blast of Hell, and it is the share of the believer of the Fire."
"2533","2218 - Ali narrated to us, he said: al-Muqri narrated to us, from Said ibn Abi Ayyub; and al-Kaysani narrated to us, he said: al-Muqri narrated to us, from Said, from Sulayman ibn Abi Zaynab, from Yazid ibn Muhammad al-Qurashi, from Abu Said al-Khudri (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No believer is struck by worry or grief or ailment or fatigue or harm except it is expiated thereby from him."
"2534","2219 - Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Anas ibn Iyad al-Laythi narrated to me, from Sad ibn Ishaq, from Zaynab daughter of Kab, from Abu Said al-Khudri (may Allah be pleased with him) that a man said: O Messenger of Allah, have you seen these illnesses that strike our bodies - what do we have for them? He said: Expiations. Ubayy ibn Kab said: Even if little, O Messenger of Allah? He said: Even a thorn and what is beyond it. He said: So Ubayy ibn Kab supplicated upon himself that fever remain wrestling his body as long as he is kept in this world, not preventing him from hajj or umrah, nor jihad in the path of Allah, nor attending prayer in the mosque of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: After that, no taster tasted it except he found upon him heat like fire, until it wore out his body and left him like a stripped palm stalk."
"2535","2220 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Said al-Qattan narrated to us, he said: Sad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Zaynab, from Abu Said, that a man of the Muslims, then he mentioned the same, except he said: nor an obligatory prayer in congregation; and he did not say: until he became like a stripped palm stalk,"
"2536","2221 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Yunus and Malik informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Urwah, from Aishah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No calamity befalls the Muslim except it is expiated thereby from him, even the thorn he is pricked with."
"2537","2222 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim and Makki narrated to us, they said: Ibn Jurayj narrated to us - Abu Asim said: Abu al-Zubayr informed me, and Makki said: from Abu al-Zubayr - from Jabir, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: No believing man or believing woman, no Muslim man or Muslim woman falls ill with an illness except Allah removes thereby some of his sin."
"2538","2223 - Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abu Muawiyah al-Darir narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her), she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No believer is struck by a calamity and what is above it except Allah deducts thereby a sin from him."
"2539","2224 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Abi Mulaykah, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, from Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her), she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No Muslim is pricked with a thorn and what is above it except it is expiation for him."
"2540","2225 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Usamah ibn Zayd al-Laythi informed me, from Ibn Halhalah, from Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Ata al-Amiri, he said: I heard Abu Said al-Khudri (may Allah be pleased with him) saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: No believer is struck by worry or grief or ailment or fatigue or harm except it is expiated thereby from him. Abu Jafar said: The discussion on this has been sufficed by what preceded from us of discussion on what is before it of these chapters. And Allah we ask for success."
"2541","Chapter 360 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that illnesses write rewards or remove sins. 2226 - al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Khazim narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, from Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her), she said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: No Muslim is struck by a thorn and what is above it except he is raised thereby a degree or there is removed from him thereby a sin."
"2542","2227 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Abu Musab al-Zuhri narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Abi Hazim narrated to us, from Ibn al-Had, from Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad, from Amrah, from Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her) that she heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Nothing befalls the believer, even the thorn that strikes him, except Allah writes for him thereby a good deed or removes from him thereby a sin. So we considered what is in these two hadiths: is there in them contradiction to what preceded in these chapters of the reports we reported therein of this kind? We found it, by the praise of Allah and His blessing, not contrary to anything in them. That is because in them is what we understood thereby that the illnesses among these things mentioned with them in these two hadiths may befall one with no sin nor fault upon him, of the prophets, the blessings of Allah upon them, and of others besides them, so they are rewards for them; and may befall one with sins and wrongs, so they are a removal of their sins and faults from them. So what is in these two hadiths confirms that and strengthens it; and what is said in these two hadiths of the removal of sins is meant for one with sins, and what is in them of reward and raising in degrees is for one with no sins nor wrongs upon him of those it befalls. And Allah we ask for success."
"2543","Chapter 361 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the manner of sending blessings upon him. 2228 - Abu al-Qasim Hisham ibn Muhammad ibn Qurrah ibn Abi Khalifah al-Ruayni narrated to us, he said: Abu Jafar Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Salamah al-Tahawi al-Azdi narrated to us, he said: Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Bishr al-Abdi narrated to us, from Mujammi ibn Yahya, from Uthman ibn Mawhab, from Musa ibn Talhah, from his father, he said: We said: O Messenger of Allah, we have known greeting you, so how is the sending of blessings? He said: Say: O Allah, send blessings upon Muhammad and upon the family of Muhammad as You sent blessings upon Ibrahim and upon the family of Ibrahim; You are Praiseworthy, Glorious. And bless Muhammad and the family of Muhammad as You blessed Ibrahim and the family of Ibrahim."
"2544","2229 - Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Wahb narrated to us that Malik ibn Anas narrated to him, from Nuaym ibn Abd Allah al-Mujmir, that Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Zayd al-Ansari - and Abd Allah ibn Zayd is the one who was shown the call to prayer - informed him from Abu Masud al-Ansari that he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to us while we were in the gathering of Sad ibn Ubadah, and Bashir ibn Sad said to him: Allah the Mighty and Majestic has commanded us to send blessings upon you, O Messenger of Allah, so how do we send blessings upon you? He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) fell silent until we wished he had not asked him. Then he said: Say: O Allah, send blessings upon Muhammad and upon the family of Muhammad as You sent blessings upon the family of Ibrahim, and bless Muhammad and upon the family of Muhammad as You blessed the family of Ibrahim in the worlds; You are Praiseworthy, Glorious. And the greeting is as you have known."
"2545","2230 - Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Isa ibn Yunus narrated to me, from Khalid ibn Salamah, that Abd al-Hamid ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Zayd ibn al-Khattab entered upon Musa ibn Talhah and said: O Abu Isa, how did it reach you about sending blessings upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? Musa said: I asked Zayd ibn Kharijah about sending blessings upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and Zayd ibn Kharijah said: I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - meaning I said: How is the sending of blessings upon you? He said: Send blessings and strive, then say: O Allah, bless Muhammad and upon the family of Muhammad as You blessed Ibrahim; You are Praiseworthy, Glorious."
"2546","2231 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Qabisah ibn Uqbah narrated to us, from Sufyan, from al-Amash, from al-Hakam, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Kab ibn Ujrah, he said: When: {O you who believe, send blessings upon him and greet him with greeting} was revealed, a man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, this greeting upon you we have known, so how is the sending of blessings? He said: Say: O Allah, send blessings upon Muhammad and upon the family of Muhammad as You sent blessings upon the family of Ibrahim; You are Praiseworthy, Glorious. And bless Muhammad and upon the family of Muhammad as You blessed the family of Ibrahim; You are Praiseworthy, Glorious."
"2547","2232 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Qabisah narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Ibrahim ibn Muhajir, from Mujahid, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Kab ibn Ujrah; and from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Kab ibn Ujrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) similar to this."
"2548","2233 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Ubayd Allah ibn Musa al-Absi narrated to us, he said: Shayban - meaning al-Nahwi - narrated to us, from al-Amash, from al-Hakam, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Kab, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the same."
"2549","2234 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to me, he said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to me; and Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, they said: Shubah narrated to us, from al-Hakam, he said: I heard Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, he said: Kab ibn Ujrah met me and said: Shall I not gift you a gift? I said: Yes. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to us and we said: O Messenger of Allah, this greeting upon you we have known, so how is the sending of blessings? He said: Say: O Allah, send blessings upon Muhammad and upon the family of Muhammad as You sent blessings upon the family of Ibrahim; You are Praiseworthy, Glorious. And bless Muhammad and upon the family of Muhammad as You blessed the family of Ibrahim; You are Praiseworthy, Glorious."
"2550","2235 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Ubayd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Hafs al-Taymi narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahid, meaning Ibn Ziyad, narrated to us, he said: Abu Farwah narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Isa ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us that he heard Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla saying: Kab ibn Ujrah met me and said: Shall I gift you a gift I heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? I said: Yes, and gift it to me. He said: We asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) how is the sending of blessings upon you, people of the House, for Allah the Mighty and Majestic has taught us how we greet? He said: Say: O Allah, send blessings upon Muhammad and upon the family of Muhammad as You sent blessings upon Ibrahim and upon the family of Ibrahim; You are Praiseworthy, Glorious. O Allah, bless Muhammad and upon the family of Muhammad as You blessed Ibrahim and upon the family of Ibrahim; You are Praiseworthy, Glorious."
"2551","2236 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: al-Nadr ibn Abd al-Jabbar Abu al-Aswad al-Muradi narrated to us, he said; and Nafi, meaning Ibn Yazid, informed us, from Ibn al-Had, from Abd Allah - meaning Ibn Khabbab - narrated to him, from Abu Said al-Khudri (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: We said: O Messenger of Allah, this greeting upon you - so how do we send blessings upon you? He said: Say: O Allah, send blessings upon Muhammad, Your servant and Your Messenger, as You sent blessings upon the family of Ibrahim; and bless Muhammad and the family of Muhammad as You blessed Ibrahim."
"2552","2237 - Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhammad ibn al-Mughirah narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Main narrated to us, he said: Marwan ibn Muawiyah narrated to us, from Uthman ibn Hakim, from Khalid ibn Salamah, from Musa ibn Talhah, from Zayd ibn Kharijah, brother of Banu al-Harith ibn al-Khazraj, he said: We said: O Messenger of Allah, we have known how we greet you, so how do we send blessings upon you? He said: Send blessings upon me and say: O Allah, send blessings upon Muhammad and upon the family of Muhammad as You blessed Ibrahim and the family of Ibrahim; You are Praiseworthy, Glorious."
"2553","2238 - Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him from Abd Allah ibn Abi Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Hazm, from his father, from Amr ibn Sulaym al-Zuraqi, that Abu Humayd al-Saidi informed him that they said: O Messenger of Allah, how do we send blessings upon you? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Say: O Allah, send blessings upon Muhammad and upon his wives and his offspring, as You sent blessings upon the family of Ibrahim; and bless Muhammad and his wives and his offspring as You blessed the family of Ibrahim; You are Praiseworthy, Glorious. Abu Jafar said: We did not find in the hadith of anyone we mentioned in this chapter about mentioning sending blessings upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the sending of blessings upon his wives and his offspring except this hadith; its pivot is upon Abd Allah ibn Abi Bakr. So we sought whether we find for him a corroborator upon that."
"2554","2239 - So we found Ubayd ibn Rijal narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, he said: Mamar informed us, from Ibn Tawus, from Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Hazm, from a man of the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say: O Allah, send blessings upon Muhammad and upon the people of his house and upon his wives and his offspring, as You sent blessings upon Ibrahim; You are Praiseworthy, Glorious. And bless Muhammad and upon the people of his house and upon his wives and his offspring; You are Praiseworthy, Glorious. Ibn Tawus said: And my father used to say the like of that. So in this hadith is what indicates the agreement of Ibn Tawus with Abd Allah ibn Abi Bakr in taking this hadith from Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad, by including the wives of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his offspring in sending blessings upon him; and in this hadith is the addition of Ibn Tawus upon Abd Allah ibn Abi Bakr: and the people of his house. So we concluded that the addition of all that in the narration of Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad over others besides him of the narrators of this hadith by the routes we mentioned in this chapter from others besides him."
"2555","2240 - Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman and Fahd narrated to us, they said: al-Qanabi narrated to us, he said: Dawud ibn Qays narrated to us, from Nuaym ibn Abd Allah, from Abu Hurayrah (may the hadith continue)."
"2556","And Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Hajib ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Fudayk narrated to us, he said: Dawud ibn Qays narrated to us, from Nuaym ibn Abd Allah al-Mujmir, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: We said: O Messenger of Allah, how do we send blessings upon you? He said: Say: O Allah, send blessings upon Muhammad and upon the family of Muhammad, and bless Muhammad and upon the family of Muhammad, as You sent blessings and blessed Ibrahim and the family of Ibrahim; You are Praiseworthy, Glorious. And the greeting is as you have known. Abu Jafar said: What the people of knowledge held about the manner of sending blessings upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - of the people of Madinah, what is in the hadith of Abu Masud; and of the people of Kufah, what is in the hadith of Kab ibn Ujrah. We do not know anyone attached to anything of these reports; likewise the rest of the people of knowledge besides them, we do not know them attached to anything of these reports besides these two reports. And each group of them used what it held of them in prayer and in other than it, not upon their counting what is of that from them in their prayers among the obligations which are not valid except thereby, and of what if left in them the one praying must repeat them - except al-Shafii, for he held it is among the obligations in prayers which are not valid except thereby. And he held its place therein is after the tashahhud which the greeting follows; and he held in its manner what is in the hadith of Abu Masud which we reported in this chapter - Harmalah ibn Yahya mentioned that from him, and we did not find it from others of his companions from him. And it would have been incumbent upon him by his principle that the hadith of Abu Humayd in this be more worthy than it and than other than it of these reports, for the addition in it over what is in them, namely including his wives and his offspring and the people of his house in sending blessings upon him, as he held to the hadith of Ibn Abbas in the tashahhud for the addition in it, namely 'and the blessed ones', over what is in others of the reported reports on the tashahhud. And with Allah is success. And in some of these reports the aim is Ibrahim, and in some the aim is his family; and this, in our view, is of what has no contradiction nor disagreement in it, for mentioning the family in the usage of the Arabs includes those who are his family, as Allah the Mighty and Majestic said: {Admit the family of Pharaoh into the severest punishment} - not that Pharaoh is outside them; but since his family, by their following him upon what he was upon of opposing the command of Allah, deserved thereby, he, by his calling them to it and his leading them in it, deserved more intensely. And Allah we ask for success."
"2557","Chapter 362 - Explanation of the difficulty of the aspect in what we mentioned of the disagreement about sending blessings upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at the end of prayers: is it an obligation without which prayer is not valid, or is it of the commanded sunnahs in prayers which are valid even if it is not done therein? 2241 - Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Sulayman, and he is al-Amash, from Shaqiq, from Abd Allah ibn Masud (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: We used to say behind the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while in prayer, when we sat: Peace upon Allah the Mighty and Majestic and upon His servants; peace upon Jibril and Mikail; peace upon so-and-so and so-and-so. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah the Mighty and Majestic is Peace, so do not say thus; rather say: Greetings belong to Allah, and prayers and good things. Peace upon you, O Prophet, and the mercy of Allah and His blessings. Peace upon us and upon the righteous servants of Allah - for when he says it, it reaches every righteous servant in heaven and earth. I bear witness there is no god but Allah, and I bear witness Muhammad is His servant and His Messenger. Then let him choose the best speech, or what he loves of speech."
"2558","2242 - And what Bakr ibn Idris al-Azdi and Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Idris al-Basri narrated to us, they said: Abd Allah ibn Yazid al-Muqri narrated to us, he said: Haywah ibn Shurayh narrated to us, he said: Abu Hani informed us that Abu Ali narrated to him - Abu Jafar said: he is Amr ibn Malik al-Janbi - that he heard Fadalah ibn Ubayd that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - a man supplicating in his prayer without praising Allah the Mighty and Majestic nor sending blessings upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: This one hastened. Then he called him and said to him and to others: When one of you prays, let him begin with praise of his Lord the Mighty and Majestic and extolling Him, then send blessings upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then supplicate with what he wills. So in the hadith of Abd Allah ibn Masud from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) which we mentioned is that the one praying, after his tashahhud in his prayer, may choose of speech what he loves, or supplicate of speech with what he loves. And in that is what negates the saying of one who says he must send blessings upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And in the hadith of Fadalah is that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), after learning that the mentioned one praying had not sent blessings upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his prayer, did not command him to return to it; for had that been what his prayer would not suffice with, he would have commanded him to return to it, as he commanded in the hadith of Rifaah and Abu Hurayrah and Ibn Umar the one praying a deficient prayer to return to it."
"2559","2243 - As Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Salih al-Wuhazi narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to me, he said: Sharik ibn Abi Namir narrated to me, from Ali ibn Yahya, from his uncle Rifaah ibn Rafi, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was sitting in the mosque when a man entered and prayed while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) looked at him. Then he left and greeted the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: And upon you; go back and pray, for you have not prayed. He did that two or three times. The man said to him at the end of that: Show me and teach me, for I am but a human who is right and wrong. He said to him: Yes. He said to him: When you stand from your prayer... then he taught him what he taught him of what he does in his prayer, then said to him: When you have done that your prayer is complete, and what is diminished of that only diminishes of your prayer."
"2560","2244 - And as Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Abi Kathir al-Ansari narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Ali ibn Yahya ibn Khallad al-Zuraqi, from his father, from his grandfather Rifaah ibn Rafi, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) similar."
"2561","2245 - And as Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Hakam narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Rushdin narrated to us, from Haywah, from Ibn Ajlan, from Ali ibn Yahya ibn Khallad, from his father, from his uncle, he said: We were sitting with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when a man entered and prayed while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) watched him unknowingly. When he finished he came and greeted the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Go back and pray, for you have not prayed. He went back and prayed, then came and he said to him: Go back and pray, for you have not prayed. When it was the second or third time he said: By the One who sent you with the truth, I have striven, so teach me. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) taught him of what he does in his prayer."
"2562","2246 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Said narrated to us, from Ubayd Allah ibn Umar, he said: Said al-Maqburi narrated to me, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) similar to the hadith of Abu Dawud from al-Wuhazi which we reported in this chapter. And in what we mentioned is evidence and proof for one who does not make sending blessings upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at the end of prayers among the obligations without which prayer is not valid. If someone who holds its obligation in prayers says: I found Allah said in His Book: {O you who believe, send blessings upon him and greet him with greeting}, so I understood thereby it is among the things He obligated. It is said to him: Did He say: send blessings upon him in your prayers? He only said that absolutely, which they attain by saying it in their prayers and in other than them - like what He said in other than this verse, namely: {O you who believe, remember Allah with much remembrance, and glorify Him morning and evening}. And whoever left glorification in his prayer, his prayer is not thereby corrupted; like that, whoever left sending blessings in his prayer upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), his prayer is not thereby corrupted, though he left excellence and faith - by what he left of them he leaves his share and falls short of the rank he would have been of its people had he not left that. And it is also said to him: We saw you say that had he sent blessings upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his prayer in other than the tashahhud followed by the greeting, and did not send blessings upon him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) after the tashahhud followed by the greeting, that does not suffice him for his sending blessings upon him in his prayer. What proof have you for what you said of that? If he says: I only said it is after the last tashahhud of his prayer, for I found in the verse what indicates that, namely His saying the Mighty and Majestic: {and greet with greeting}. I thus understood it is adjacent to the greeting in prayer. It is said to him: Your opponent says to you: That greeting mentioned in this verse is not the greeting in prayer; rather it is submission to him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his command and prohibition, in prayer and in other than it, as He the Mighty and Majestic said: {By your Lord, they do not believe until they make you judge in what disputes between them, then find no distress in themselves of what you decreed, and submit fully}. So there is no difference between you and him in your interpretations. And in what we mentioned of this is sufficiency from other than it. And Allah the Mighty and Majestic we ask for success."
"2563","Chapter 363 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his saying: There is no charity upon the Muslim in his slave nor in his horse. 2247 - Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Wahb narrated to us that Malik narrated to him; and Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: al-Qanabi Abd Allah ibn Maslamah narrated to us, he said: Malik narrated to us, from Abd Allah ibn Dinar, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, from Irak ibn Malik, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no charity upon the Muslim in his slave nor in his horse."
"2564","2248 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Amir and Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, they said: Shubah narrated to us, from Abd Allah ibn Dinar, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, from Irak, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the same."
"2565","2249 - And Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Hudhayfah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abd Allah ibn Dinar, then he mentioned the same with his isnad."
"2566","2250 - And Muhammad ibn Isa ibn Fulayh narrated to me, he said: Abu al-Aswad al-Nadr ibn Abd al-Jabbar narrated to us, from Sulayman ibn Bilal ibn Fulayh, from Abd Allah ibn Dinar, then he mentioned the same with his isnad."
"2567","2251 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Usamah ibn Zayd al-Laythi informed me, from Makhul, from Irak, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the same."
"2568","2252 - And Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sabiq narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Tahman narrated to us, from Ayyub ibn Musa, from Makhul, from Irak, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: There is no charity upon horses and slaves."
"2569","2253 - And al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Khuthaym ibn Irak, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: There is no charity upon the Muslim in his horse nor in his slave. Then someone said: How did you leave these reports and place upon the Muslim in his slaves the charity of fitr, while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not except that in what you reported from him? Our answer to him in that, by the success-giving of Allah the Mighty and Majestic, is that this, though not among what the exception of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was mentioned in what we reported he said, his excepting it and obligating it was mentioned in other than it."
"2570","2254 - As Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Hakam narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Nafi ibn Yazid informed me, he said: Jafar ibn Rabiah informed me, from Irak, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no charity upon the Muslim in his slave nor in his horse except the charity of fitr in slaves."
"2571","2255 - Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Zayd al-Makki narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Mawhab narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Zakariyya ibn Abi Zaidah narrated to us, from Ubayd Allah ibn Umar, from Abu al-Zinad, from al-Araj, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: There is no zakat in horses and slaves, except that in slaves is the charity of fitr."
"2572","2256 - And as Jafar ibn Ahmad ibn al-Walid al-Aslami narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn al-Walid al-Kindi narrated to us, he said: Abu Yusuf informed us, from Ubayd Allah ibn Umar, from Usamah ibn Zayd, from Irak ibn Malik, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the same."
"2573","2257 - And as al-Hasan ibn Ghulayb narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahim ibn Sulayman al-Razi narrated to us, from Ubayd Allah ibn Umar, from Usamah ibn Zayd, from Irak ibn Malik, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the same. So we understood thereby that what preceded of our mention of the reports in this chapter of what their narrators shortened of what the narrators of the reports we reported after that in this chapter preserved - they are thereby worthier, and their addition upon them upon that is accepted and acted upon, for whoever preserves something is worthier than whoever falls short of it. This questioner said: Is that upon all slaves, of their Muslims and of their disbelievers? Our answer to him in that, by the success-giving of Allah the Mighty and Majestic and His aid, is that of our doctrine in that is that it is upon all slaves, their Muslims and their disbelievers, for the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not except therein a Muslim from a disbeliever, nor a disbeliever from a Muslim. And Abu Hurayrah of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) preceded us in that. As"
"2574","Yahya ibn Uthman and Abd al-Wahhab ibn Khalaf ibn Umar al-Kindi narrated to us, they said: Nuaym ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, he said: Ibn Luhayah informed us, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abi Jafar, from al-Araj, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: He used to pay zakat al-fitr for every person he maintains, young or old, free or slave, even if Christian: two mudds of wheat or a sa of dates. And of their followers Ata ibn Abi Rabah and Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz preceded us in it. As."
"2575","Yahya and Abd al-Wahhab narrated to us, they said: Nuaym ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, from Ata, he said: If you have Christian slaves not managed for trade, pay for them on the day of fitr."
"2576","Yahya and Abd al-Wahhab narrated to us, they said: Nuaym ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Ayyash narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn al-Muhajir narrated to us, from Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, he said: A man gives for his slave even if Christian, zakat al-fitr. Then someone said: In the hadith of Ibn Umar in which is mention of the obliging by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of zakat al-fitr upon every free and slave, male or female, of the Muslims - and we shall mention that with its isnads later in this book of ours, Allah willing - he said: In that is what negates non-Muslims being included in that. Our answer to him in that, by the success-giving of Allah the Mighty and Majestic and His aid, is that that does not negate the slaves upon other than the religion of Islam from the obligation of zakat al-fitr in them, for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only obligated it upon whoever pays it from his property as zakat for him and purification; so that is upon the capable Muslims, not upon others besides them of the slaves incapable of it, for the obligations of Allah the Mighty and Majestic only attach to those capable of them, not those incapable of them. And the slaves are incapable of this obligation, for Allah removed them from ownership of things by His saying: {Allah sets forth a parable: a slave owned, unable over anything}. So the obligation which he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) obligated in this hadith goes back to the owning finders, not to the incapable owned. And we know no disagreement among the people of knowledge that a slave freed before his master pays zakat al-fitr for him, then owns wealth after that, is not obligated to pay it for himself of what he owns, as he pays for himself the expiations of his oaths in which he broke them in his slavery without expiating them by fasting for them. That indicates what is obligatory upon him in himself is what he pays after his freeing from his wealth he earns after his freeing, and in that he is of those whose ruling is observed in his Islam and in his lack of Islam; and what of that he does not pay after his freeing is what was upon his master, not upon him, and observed in that is the religion of his master, not his religion. And as it was obligatory upon his master to pay zakat for him if for trade, as he pays zakat for his Muslim slave if for trade, and his disbelief does not prevent him of that; likewise the one he owned by his marrying her to him by his command - if owned by other than trade, his disbelief does not prevent that either: he also pays zakat al-fitr for him by his owning him, and his disbelief does not prevent him of that. Then another questioner of the people of anomaly said: It is obligatory upon him - meaning the slave - in himself, he pays it from his earnings. And this is a saying we know no one preceded him in; and he attached in that to the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Whoever sells a slave who has wealth. He said: I thus understood he said: possessor of wealth. Our answer to him in that - by the success-giving of Allah the Mighty and Majestic and His aid - is that there is not in what was mentioned of what necessitates what he held, that the slave is possessor of wealth; rather in the rest of the hadith is what negates that, namely his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): His wealth is for the seller unless the buyer stipulates. That indicates the reality of his wealth is for his owner, and its attribution to him - meaning the slave - is only like his (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) attributing the fruit of the sold palm to the palm by his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Whoever sells a palm with pollinated fruit - not upon the palm owning anything. And as Allah the Mighty and Majestic attributed the house of the spider to the spider by His saying: {And the weakest of houses is the house of the spider} - not for its owning it. And as the door of the house is attributed to the house and the horse-cloth to the horse - not that they own that. Had the slave owned his wealth, his master would not take it from him, as he may not take the dower of his wife which he owned by his marrying her to him by his command. And in what we mentioned is sufficiency. And Allah is the Praised upon that. And another questioner said: In what you reported to us from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about horses he negated zakat from them, while you obligate zakat in them if for trade. Our answer to him in that - by the success-giving of Allah the Mighty and Majestic and His aid - is that we found all the people of knowledge agreed upon removing them, if for trade, from that; and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only removed them from zakat if for other than trade. And their consensus is a proof like the exception had the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) excepted it for us in that hadith. And another questioner said about the hadith of Abu Hurayrah: Is there not in slaves zakat al-fitr - meaning mentioned therein of what we reported - while the people of knowledge differ about zakat al-fitr: does it become obligatory in trade slaves or not? Abu Hanifah and his companions and al-Thawri do not obligate zakat al-fitr in them; Malik and the rest of the people of Hijaz obligate zakat al-fitr in them, and the obligation of wealth zakat in them if managed in trades does not prevent that in their view. Our answer to him in that - by the success-giving of Allah the Mighty and Majestic and His aid - is that this is of what we found no mention in Book nor Sunnah; we only found the evidence for the saying in it from consensus, not from other than it. That is because we found pasturing livestock: no disagreement about the obligation of zakat in them if not for trade, and that if for trade the two zakats do not combine in them together; only one of them becomes obligatory in them and the other is negated, upon what the people of knowledge say in that. We thus understood that two zakats do not combine in one thing, and that when one of them becomes obligatory in it it negates the other from it. Likewise trade slaves: when some zakat becomes obligatory in them, it negates from them zakat al-fitr. And Allah we ask for success."
"2577","Chapter 364 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from Qays ibn Sad ibn Ubadah al-Ansari about the abrogation of zakat al-fitr, and about the abrogation of the obligation of fasting the day of Ashura. 2258 - Bakkar ibn Qutaybah and Ibrahim ibn Marzuq and Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, they said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, he said: I heard al-Hakam, he said: I heard al-Qasim ibn Mukhaymirah, from Amr ibn Shurahbil, from Qays ibn Sad ibn Ubadah, he said: We used to give the charity of fitr before zakat was revealed, and fast Ashura before Ramadan was revealed; when Ramadan was revealed and zakat was revealed we were neither commanded with it nor forbidden from it, and we used to do it."
"2578","2259 - Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, he said: al-Hakam informed us, then he mentioned the same with his isnad."
"2579","2260 - Bakkar narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from al-Hakam, then he mentioned the same with his isnad."
"2580","2261 - And Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Amir narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from al-Hakam, then he mentioned the same with his isnad."
"2581","2262 - And Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, from al-Qasim ibn Mukhaymirah, from Abu Ammar, from Qays ibn Sad with similar meaning."
"2582","2263 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us (transformation); and Bakkar narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir narrated to us, they said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Salamah, then he mentioned the same with his isnad. So we examined what is in this hadith of Qays of what the fasting of the day of Ashura was upon before the obligation of fasting the month of Ramadan, and found Abd Allah ibn Masud (may Allah be pleased with him) agreed with him upon it."
"2583","2264 - As Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Ubayd Allah ibn Musa al-Absi narrated to us, he said: Israil narrated to us, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, from Abd Allah - and al-Ashath ibn Qays entered upon him on the day of Ashura while he ate - and he said: O Abu Abd al-Rahman, today is the day of Ashura. He said: It used to be fasted before Ramadan was revealed; when Ramadan was revealed it was left. As for you - you are breaking fast - so come near and eat."
"2584","2265 - And as Sulayman ibn Shuayb al-Kaysani narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Khurasani narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from his father, from Umarah ibn Umayr, from Qays ibn al-Sakan, from Ibn Masud (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: A man came to him while he ate, and he said to him: Come. He said to him: I am fasting. Abd Allah said to him: We used to fast it, then it was left - meaning Ashura."
"2585","2266 - And as Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Abu Hamzah, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, he said: We were sitting with Abd Allah when al-Ashath ibn Qays came to him while he took lunch, and he said: Lunch, O Abu Muhammad! He said: Do you not know today is the day of Ashura? He said: Yes, by the One in whose Hand is my soul, I knew; and we were not commanded with its fasting except before Ramadan was revealed. When it was revealed we were neither commanded with it nor forbidden from it. And we found Aishah also agreed upon what agrees with him."
"2586","2267 - As al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: al-Shafii narrated to us, he said: Malik ibn Anas narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her) that she said: The day of Ashura was a day Quraysh fasted in the pre-Islamic ignorance, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) fasted it in the pre-Islamic ignorance. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to Madinah he fasted it and commanded its fasting. When Ramadan was obligated it became the obligation, and the day of Ashura was left: whoever wished fasted it and whoever wished left it."
"2587","2268 - And as al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Shuayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Irak ibn Malik, Irak ibn Malik informed him that Urwah informed him that Aishah informed him that Quraysh used to fast the day of Ashura in the pre-Islamic ignorance; then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded its fasting until Ramadan was obligated. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever wishes, let him fast it; and whoever wishes, let him break it."
"2588","2269 - And as Nasr ibn Marzuq and Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, they said: Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sad narrated to me, he said: Uqayl narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Urwah ibn al-Zubayr informed me that Aishah informed him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded fasting the day of Ashura before Ramadan was obligated. When Ramadan was obligated he said: Whoever wishes fasted Ashura and whoever wishes broke it. And we found Jabir ibn Samurah agreed with him upon it."
"2589","2270 - As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Shayban narrated to us, from al-Ashath, from Jafar ibn Abi Thawr, from Jabir ibn Samurah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to command fasting Ashura and urge us upon it and check upon us for it. When Ramadan was obligated he neither commanded us nor checked upon us for it. Abu Jafar said: Abd Allah ibn Masud and Aishah and Jabir ibn Samurah agreed in the fasting of the day of Ashura upon what we reported from them in it. And it was reported from Abd Allah ibn Abbas that it was fasted unlike that."
"2590","2271 - As Bakkar ibn Qutaybah and Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, they said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Abu Bishr, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, that he said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to Madinah he found the Jews fasting the day of Ashura, so he asked them. They said: This is the day in which Allah the Mighty and Majestic made Musa (peace be upon him) prevail over Pharaoh. He said: You are more worthy of Musa (peace be upon him) than them, so fast it. Abu Jafar said: In this is evidence they fasted it for thanks, not as obligation. And it is possible they fasted it for thanks, not upon what is in this hadith of Ibn Abbas; then its fasting was obligated upon them, so they fasted it as obligation, upon what is in the hadiths of Ibn Masud and Aishah and Jabir ibn Samurah. And it was reported in emphasizing the obligation of its fasting also of what indicates it was as obligation, not as thanks."
"2591","2272 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Salamah al-Khuzai, from his uncle, he said: We came early to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the morning of the day of Ashura, having already taken lunch. He said: Did you fast this day? We said: We have taken lunch. He said: Complete the rest of your day."
"2592","2273 - Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Qatadah, he said: I heard Abu al-Minhal narrating from his uncle - and he was of Aslam - that people came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), or some of them, on the day of Ashura. He said: Did you fast today? They said: No, and we have eaten. He said: Fast the rest of your day."
"2593","2274 - And what Malik ibn Abd Allah ibn Sayf al-Tujibi narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hamzah narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Maryam, that Qazaah narrated to him from Abu Said al-Khudri (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioned Ashura and magnified it, then said to those around him: Whoever of you has not eaten, let him fast his day; and whoever of you has eaten, let him fast the rest of his day."
"2594","2275 - And what Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Khalid al-Wahbi narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us from Abd Allah ibn Abi Bakr, from Habib ibn Hind ibn Asma, from his father, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent me to my people of Aslam and said: Say to them: Let them fast the day of Ashura. Whomever you find of them who ate from the beginning of his day, let him fast its end."
"2595","2276 - And what Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Asbahani narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, from Majzaah ibn Zahir, from his father, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said - meaning the day of Ashura: Whoever ate, let him complete the rest of his day; and whoever did not eat, let him fast, in the name of Allah. And al-Bukhari mentioned that this Zahir is Ibn al-Aswad of Aslam and that he pledged under the tree."
"2596","2277 - And as Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Abidah ibn Humayd narrated to us, from Husayn ibn Abd al-Rahman, from al-Shabi, from Muhammad ibn Sayfi, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said on the day of Ashura: Is there among you anyone who fasted today? We said: Among us one who fasted and among us one who did not fast. He said: Complete this day of yours. Abu Jafar said: He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not uncover them in this hadith whether they ate or not; that indicates his commanding them to fast the rest of their day equally covers whoever ate before that therein and whoever did not eat. Abu Jafar said: That indicates it was then like the month of Ramadan after it had been the obligation. Then someone said: We saw one upon whom the month of Ramadan entered while he did not know of its entering upon him, so he ate, then knew in that day of his that he is in Ramadan - he is commanded to abstain from what the fasting abstains from in its rest, and to make up a day in its place; while he was not commanded with that in the fasting of the day of Ashura at the time its fasting was obligatory. Our answer to him in that - by the success-giving of Allah, Majestic and Mighty, and His aid - is that that, in our view - and Allah knows best - is because the obligation caught them in the day of Ashura after they entered into it, and after it had been, after their entering into it, not obligated upon them. And what is in the hadith of Abu Said al-Khudri which we reported in this chapter indicates that, of the magnifying by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and of his commanding those around him therein with what he commanded them therein. So they were like one who attains puberty of the youths, and like one who embraces Islam of the Christians in a day of the month of Ramadan: they are commanded to fast its rest, though they ate before that, and are not commanded to make up a day in its place. And as for what is in the hadith of Qays and those who agreed with him of those we mentioned, upon what he agreed upon of what he mentioned therein of fasting the day of Ashura, and of what he mentioned therein of the charity of fitr - it was reported from Abd Allah ibn Umar what contradicts that."
"2597","2278 - As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Arim narrated to us; and as Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, they said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both), he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded the charity of fitr for every young and old, free and slave: a sa of barley or a sa of dates. He said: So the people equated it with two mudds of wheat."
"2598","2279 - And as Ali ibn Shaybah and Abu Umayyah narrated to us, they said: Qabisah ibn Uqbah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ubayd Allah, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the same."
"2599","2280 - And as Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Sallam al-Attar al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Ala ibn Hammad al-Narsi narrated to us, he said: Sallam ibn Abi Muti narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) obligated upon every male and female, free or owned, a sa of dates or a sa of barley - meaning the charity of fitr."
"2600","2281 - And as Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Ansari narrated to us, he said: al-Qanabi narrated to us, he said: Malik narrated to us; and as Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us that Malik informed him from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the same, and they added: of the Muslims. And they did not mention the equating in some of what is before it, of the people equating two mudds of wheat. So in this hadith is mention of the obliging by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of it; and in it is the equating by the people of it with two mudds of wheat - and that is only with the continuance of its obligation. So this was contrary to what Qays said in that, except that we examined what Qays said in it and found for it a possible aspect for what he said in it: the charity of fitr in the beginning, in its obligation, was like what is in the zakat of wealth upon it in its obligation after it was obligated therein, until it became in its obligation like the five prayers in belief in them and in the incumbency of disbelief upon whoever denies them. So the charity of fitr was likewise; then the zakat of wealth was obligated, so the obligation that was in it was returned to the zakat of wealth, and in its place was made for zakat al-fitr an obligation less than that, upon what is in the hadith of Ibn Umar - of what, had a denier denied it, he would not by his denying it be a disbeliever, as he would be by his denying the zakat of wealth a disbeliever. So this is a sound meaning by which what Qays said about the obligation of zakat al-fitr is discharged. And Allah the Mighty and Majestic we ask for success."
"2601","Chapter 365 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the rising of the star with whose rising blight is lifted or lightened: which star is it? 2282 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Salim narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Hasan narrated to us, he said: Abu Hanifah narrated to us, he said: Ata ibn Abi Rabah narrated to us, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When the star rises, blight is lifted from the people of every land. Abu Jafar said: We examined this hadith and did not find in it mention of that star, which star it is; so we sought it in other than it of the hadiths."
"2602","2283 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us (transformation); and al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us - Yunus said: Ibn Abi Dhib informed me, and al-Rabi said: Ibn Abi Dhib narrated to us - from Uthman ibn Abd Allah ibn Suraqah, from Abd Allah ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade selling fruits until blight goes. He said: I asked Ibn Umar: When is that? He said: The rising of al-Thurayya (the Pleiades)."
"2603","2284 - And we found al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: al-Shafii narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ismail narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Dhib, then he mentioned the same with his isnad. Abu Jafar said: Abd Allah ibn Umar is the maternal uncle of Uthman ibn Abd Allah ibn Suraqah."
"2604","2285 - And Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Dhib, then he mentioned the same with his isnad. Abu Jafar said: We thus understood it is al-Thurayya; and we understood thereby also that what is meant by the lifting of blight from it is the fruits of palms. Then we sought in other than this hadith also of the hadiths whether we find mention of the time of its rising of the night or not."
"2605","2286 - Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Isl ibn Sufyan narrated to us, from Ata, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: The star never rises in the morning with blight standing except it is lifted from them or lightened. So we understood thereby that it is upon its rising in the morning, a rising with which dawn comes; and we sought which month of the months of the year that is in, by the reckoning of the Egyptians, and found it Bashans; and we sought the day in which that is, in the rising of its dawn, of its days, and found it the nineteenth day of its days; and we sought what corresponds to that of the Syriac months by which the people of Iraq reckon that, and found it Ayyar; and we sought the day in which that is in its dawn, and it is the twelfth day of its days. And these two months are those in which is the bearing of palms - I mean by their bearing it its appearance therein, not other than that - and by the time we mentioned of them blight feared upon them before that is secured against. And Allah the Mighty and Majestic we ask for success. And we did not find this hadith of Isl with addition over what Affan narrated of it."
"2606","2287 - As Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: al-Mualla ibn Asad narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb narrated to us, from Isl, from Ata, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When al-Thurayya rises in the morning, blight is lifted from the people of the land. So in this hadith is what the hadith of Suraqah indicated to us and what is in the hadith of Affan which we reported from Wuhayb. And Allah we ask for success."
"2607","Chapter 366 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his saying: Every son of Adam is eaten by dust except the tailbone. 2288 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us that Malik informed him from Abu al-Zinad, from al-Araj, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Every son of Adam is eaten by the earth except the tailbone; from it he was created and upon it he is composed."
"2608","2289 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Safwan ibn Isa narrated to us, from Ibn Ajlan, then he mentioned the same with his isnad."
"2609","2290 - Harun ibn Kamil narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Muhammad ibn Ajlan narrated to me, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Hurmuz al-Araj, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the same."
"2610","2291 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi al-Zinad narrated to me, from his father, then he mentioned the same with his isnad, except he said: And in it he is composed."
"2611","2292 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Salih al-Wuhazi narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi al-Zinad narrated to us, then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it."
"2612","2293 - Abu Umayyah and Muhammad ibn Ali narrated to us, they said: Said ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Mansur ibn Abi al-Aswad narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Every son of Adam decays except the tailbone, and in it creation is reconstituted."
"2613","2294 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, he said: al-Amash narrated to us, he said: I heard Abu Salih narrating, saying: I heard Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Everything of man decays except his tailbone, and in it creation is reconstituted on the Day of Resurrection, then Allah sends down water upon it and they sprout as vegetation sprouts. Then an objector said: Observation refutes what is in this hadith, for we find the dead man when his grave is opened and nothing is found in it, for it has perished by the earth consuming it, and we find him burned so fire consumes him until nothing remains of him. So our answer to him in that, by the grace of Allah Almighty and His help, is that what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is as narrated from him, no other is permissible, since those who transmitted it from him are precise and trustworthy in it; and whoever is ignorant of that and rejects it out of ignorance is ignorant of the subtlety of the power of Allah Almighty. For since it is of the subtlety of His power, Almighty, that He returns the compounded bones in the living to dust then returns them as they were before, as He Almighty said: And He it is who originates creation then repeats it, and as He Almighty said: And he strikes for Us a similitude and forgets his creation; he says: Who gives life to the bones when they are rotten? So He Almighty said: Say: He gives them life who produced them the first time, and He is Knower of every creation. And since that is as we mentioned regarding the subtlety of His power, it is not objectionable therein that the tailbones of the sons of Adam should remain uneaten by the earth. And just as He protected His servant and Prophet and intimate friend Ibrahim (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from being eaten by the fire which eats whatever things it meets, by His inspiring it to that, preserving that from them until He manifests it at the time He wills to manifest it, and even if that is hidden from our eyes it is not hidden from Him; just as He Almighty related to us of His servant Luqman, of his saying to his son: O my son, if it be but the weight of a grain of mustard seed, though it be in a rock or in the heavens or in the earth, Allah will bring it forth; indeed Allah is Subtle, Aware. And this subtlety is not objectionable regarding the tailbones of the sons of Adam, what has been narrated in this hadith, nor impossible therein; and Allah Almighty we ask for guidance."
"2614","Chapter 367 - Explanation of the difficulty in what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), of his saying: If faith were at al-Thurayya (the Pleiades), and of his saying: If religion were at al-Thurayya, men from the sons of Persia would attain it. 2295 - Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Najih, from his father, from Qays ibn Sad ibn Ubadah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If faith were at al-Thurayya, men from the people of Persia would attain it."
"2615","2296 - Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz al-Darawardi narrated to us, he said: I heard Thawr ibn Zayd mentioning from Abu al-Ghayth, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: When this verse was revealed: And others of them who have not yet joined them, the people spoke to him about it, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) turned to Salman and said: If religion were at al-Thurayya, men from among these would attain it."
"2616","2297 - Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Bilal informed me, from Thawr ibn Zayd, from Salim Abu al-Ghayth, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: We were sitting with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when Surah al-Jumuah was revealed: And others of them who have not yet joined them. A man said: Who are these, O Messenger of Allah? He did not answer him until he asked him three times, and among us was Salman al-Farisi, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) placed his hand upon Salman and said: If faith were at al-Thurayya, men from among these would attain it."
"2617","2298 - Yusuf narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz al-Darawardi narrated to us, he said: Shuayb, of the descendants of Umayyah ibn Zayd of the Ansar, narrated to me, he said: I heard Abu Hurayrah saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: By Him in whose Hand is my soul, if religion were at al-Thurayya, men from Persia would attain it - or he said: from the non-Arabs - Abd al-Aziz doubted. And there has been narrated from Abu Hurayrah regarding knowledge the like of this also, in a hadith in which there is something from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and something from Abu Hurayrah, of what is possible in our view that what is in it of the mention of knowledge is from the speech of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and possible that it is from the speech of Abu Hurayrah; so if it be from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) then it is like these two hadiths, and if it be from the speech of Abu Hurayrah, then Abu Hurayrah did not say that by opinion, but said it by taking it from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), or by taking it from one who took it from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"2618","2299 - And it is what Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Shayban informed us, from al-Amash, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Woe to the Arabs from an evil that has drawn near; successful is he who restrains his hand. Draw near, O sons of Farrukh, to remembrance; for the Arabs have indeed turned away, by Allah. By Allah, indeed among you are men who, if knowledge were at al-Thurayya, would attain it. And we have found from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him."
"2619","2300 - As Bakkar narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, he said: Awf al-Arabi narrated to us, he said: Shahr ibn Hawshab narrated to us, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If knowledge were at al-Thurayya, men from the sons of Persia would attain it. So we examined these reports to ascertain what is intended by what is in them, Allah willing, and we found that upon the way of similitude, as a man says to his companion: You are to me like al-Thurayya, i.e. in distance, or like his saying in its opposite, of nearness: You are to me as the back of the heart, and you are to me before my eyes, and you are to me as my forearm is to my upper arm, and the like of such examples; and since there is no faith nor religion nor knowledge at al-Thurayya, that was said by way of similitude, as was said regarding the rest of things. And it is possible that that was not said by way of similitude, but was said upon that if it were there it would necessarily be reached, for those things are only sought for the servants faith in them and their taking them and their knowledge of them; and of that is the saying of Allah Almighty: And I did not create jinn and men except to worship Me. So that was upon that if those things were placed there, and they in themselves were only intended for what we mentioned, Allah Almighty would make for whoever seeks them a means to reach them by His subtle wisdom, and those mentioned of the sons of Persia were the most eager in seeking them and hastening to them and holding fast to them; and Allah Almighty we ask for guidance."
"2620","Chapter 368 - Explanation of the difficulty in what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), of his command to cut off the Makhzumi woman who used to borrow jewelry and deny it. 2301 - Ubayd ibn Rijal narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, he said: Mamar narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: There was a Makhzumi woman who used to borrow goods and deny them, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered her hand to be cut off, so her family came to Usamah ibn Zayd and spoke to him, so Usamah spoke to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Usamah, I do not see you but speaking to me concerning one of the hudud of Allah Almighty. He said: Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood to preach and said: Those before you were destroyed only because when the noble among them stole they left him, and when the weak among them stole they cut him off. By Him in whose Hand is my soul, if Fatimah daughter of Muhammad had stolen I would have cut off her hand. So he cut off the hand of the Makhzumi woman."
"2621","2302 - Ubayd narrated to us, he said: Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, he said: Mamar informed us, from Ayyub, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: There was a Makhzumi woman who used to borrow goods and deny them, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered her hand to be cut off. Ubayd said to us: Ahmad said to us: This is disputed; it is only from Nafi, from Safiyyah, and from al-Qasim, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her."
"2622","2303 - And Musab ibn Ibrahim ibn Hamzah al-Zubayri narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, he said: al-Darawardi narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abdillah ibn Muslim narrated to us, from his uncle Ibn Shihab, from Urwah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, regarding the affair of the woman who borrowed jewelry, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) cut off her hand, she for whom Usamah ibn Zayd interceded to him."
"2623","2304 - And Musab narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, he said: al-Darawardi narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abdillah ibn Muslim narrated to us, from his uncle, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: That woman married a man of Banu Hashim and she was with him of good conduct; she would come to me and I would convey her need to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Then an objector said: You have narrated this hadith from these sound chains in your view, so how was it permissible for you to abandon them and abandon acting upon what is in them and oppose them? So our answer to him in that, by the grace of Allah Almighty and His help, is that these hadiths, as to the soundness of their transmission and the straightness of their isnads, are as he mentioned, but they fall short in mentioning the cause for which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) cut off the hand of the woman mentioned therein, of what we have found mentioned in others, and it is her theft; so the cutting by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of her was for that, not for other than it, and she was mentioned by other than it since it was a trait of her traits she was known by, and the cutting of her hand was for other than it."
"2624","2305 - As Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, that Urwah ibn al-Zubayr informed him, from Aishah, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that a woman stole at the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the campaign of the Conquest, so she was brought to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and Usamah ibn Zayd spoke to him concerning her, and when he spoke to him concerning her the face of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) changed color, and he said: Do you intercede concerning one of the hudud of Allah?! So Usamah said: Seek forgiveness for me, O Messenger of Allah. Then when it was evening the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood and praised Allah as He deserves, then he mentioned the rest of the hadith upon the like of what is in the hadith of Ubayd which we mentioned in this chapter."
"2625","2306 - And Yunus narrated to us, he said: Shuayb ibn al-Layth ibn Sad narrated to us, from his father, from Ibn Shihab, from Urwah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that Quraysh were concerned by the affair of the Makhzumi woman who stole, so they said: Who will speak to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning her? They said: Who dare to but Usamah ibn Zayd? Then he mentioned the like of its meaning. So we understood thereby that the cutting by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of that woman was only for her theft, not for other than that of what we mentioned in these hadiths; and Allah, glory be to Him, we ask for guidance, and may Allah bless our master Muhammad and his family and companions and grant them peace."
"2626","Chapter 369 - Explanation of the difficulty in what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding the two men who had emigrated to him, one of whom was martyred, and the other lived a year after him then died, yet excelled his martyred companion before him. 2307 - Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Tammam narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Ayyub ibn Sulayman ibn Isa ibn Musa ibn Talhah ibn Ubaydillah narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to me, from my grandfather, from Musa ibn Talhah, from his father."
"2627","And Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: I heard Yazid ibn Harun, he said: Muhammad ibn Amr informed us, from Abu Salamah, from Talhah ibn Ubaydillah, may Allah be pleased with him, that two men of Bali - and it is a clan of Qudaah - one of them was killed in the path of Allah Almighty, and the other was delayed a year after him, then died. Talhah said: I saw in a dream Paradise opened, and I saw the later of the two men enter Paradise before the first, so I was amazed; then in the morning I mentioned that, and it reached the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Did he not fast Ramadan after him, and pray after him a year of a thousand rakahs, and such and such rakahs for the prayer of his year."
"2628","2308 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Amir narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Amr narrated to us, from Abu Salamah, he said: Two men of Bali embraced Islam at the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned the like of it."
"2629","2309 - al-Rabi ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Ibn Luhayah and Yahya ibn Ayyub and Haywah ibn Shurayh informed us, from Yazid ibn Abdillah ibn al-Had, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Talhah ibn Ubaydillah, that two men of Bali came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and their Islam was together, and one of them was more strenuous than the other; so the strenuous of them raided and was martyred, and the other remained a year after him then died. Talhah said: While I was at the gate of Paradise, there they were; a caller came out of Paradise and admitted the one of them who died later, then came out and admitted the one who was martyred, then returned to me and said: Return, for permission has not been given to you. In the morning Talhah narrated it to the people and they were amazed at that, and it reached the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and they told him the hadith, so he said: At which of that do you marvel? They said: O Messenger of Allah, this one was the more strenuous of the two men, then was martyred in the path of Allah Almighty, and this other entered Paradise before him? He said: Did he not remain a year after him? They said: Yes. He said: And did he reach the month of Ramadan and fast it? They said: Yes. He said: And did he pray such and such prostrations in the year? They said: Yes. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Then what is between them is farther than what is between heaven and earth."
"2630","2310 - Yazid ibn Sinan and Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah and Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, they said: Abdillah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Ibn al-Had narrated to me, then they mentioned with his isnad the like of it."
"2631","2311 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Amr ibn Murrah, from Amr ibn Maymun, from Abdillah ibn Rabiah, from Ubayd ibn Khalid, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made brotherhood between two men; one of them was killed in the path of Allah, then the other died and they prayed over him. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What did you say? They said: We asked Allah Almighty to forgive him and have mercy on him and join him to his companion. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Then where is his prayer after his prayer, and his deed after his deed, and his fasting after his fasting? What is between them is farther than what is between heaven and earth."
"2632","2312 - Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Suwayd ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Abdillah, meaning Ibn al-Mubarak, narrated to us, he said: Shubah ibn al-Hajjaj informed us, from Amr ibn Murrah, he said: I heard Amr ibn Maymun narrating from Abdillah ibn Rabiah al-Sulami, and he was of the companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), from Ubayd ibn Khalid al-Sulami, then he mentioned the like of it. Abu Jafar said: And this Abdillah ibn Rabiah mentioned in this isnad is the grandfather of Mansur ibn al-Mutamir, and in this hadith is that he has companionship; and Ibn al-Mubarak was opposed in that, as al-Bukhari mentioned, and mentioned that he was not followed upon it."
"2633","2313 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Amr narrated to us, from Zayd, from Amr ibn Murrah, from Amr ibn Maymun al-Awdi, from Abdillah ibn Rabiah al-Sulami, from Ubayd ibn Khalid al-Bahzi, a man of the companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made brotherhood between two men of his companions; one of them was killed and the other lived after him as Allah Almighty willed, then died; so the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) began supplicating for him, and the utmost of their supplication for him was that he join his brother who was killed before him. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Which of them do you say is better? They said: The one who was killed before, O Messenger of Allah, in the path of Allah Almighty. He said: Do you not count for the prayer of this one and his fasting after him and his charity and his deed a merit? What is between them is farther than what is between heaven and earth; the one who died later excelled the one who died before. Abu Jafar said: Then a questioner asked about the meaning by which the later deceased of these two men deserved precedence over his companion martyred before him, though his companion has what has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding one above him in rank."
"2634","2314 - Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Shurayh narrated to me, from Abd al-Karim ibn al-Harith, from Abu Ubaydah ibn Uqbah, from Shurahbil ibn al-Simt, from Salman al-Khayr, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said: Whoever guards the frontier a day in the path of Allah, he has the reward of fasting a month and standing it in prayer; and whoever dies guarding the frontier, the like of that reward continues for him, provision is continued for him, and he is secured from the triers. Abu Jafar said about the reports of ribat virtue following, reconciling them with the two emigrants, and the answer is developed in the next entries."
"2635","2315 - And of what Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: al-Layth informed me, from Ayyub ibn Musa al-Qurashi, from Makhul, from Shurahbil, from Salman, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"2636","2316 - And of what Muhammad ibn Abdillah ibn Abd al-Hakam narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Abu Hani al-Khawlani informed me, from Amr ibn Malik, from Fadalah ibn Ubayd al-Ansari, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Every dead one has his deed sealed except the one guarding the frontier in the path of Allah, for his deed grows for him until the Day of Resurrection and he is secured from the triers of the grave. He said: So in these reports is what is in them of the merit of one who dies guarding the frontier, and whose deed grows for him until the Day of Resurrection; and one killed guarding is above one who dies guarding in rank, and that is not for one who dies not guarding; for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) has informed that his deed is cut off by his death, in the hadith of Abu Hurayrah, meaning what we mentioned earlier in this book of ours from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): that whoever dies, his deed is cut off by his death except from three: from knowledge he spread, from ongoing charity, and from a righteous child who supplicates for him. So our answer to him in that, by the grace of Allah Almighty and His help, is that what he argued with us is not contrary to what he argued with us therein of what we narrated in this chapter; and that is that what the dead one is given in his ribat is cut off from him as the deed of others of the dead is cut off from them, though his deed grows for him until the Day of Resurrection, for it is that deed itself, no other deed joining it. And the two emigrant men mentioned in the reports we narrated emigrated to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) together, so were equal in that, and stayed with him offering themselves in whatever he employed them, of jihad and of other things by which nearness to Allah Almighty is sought; and the accepted of them was employed in jihad until killed in it, and that employment was, Allah knows best, only by the employing of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of him therein; and perhaps his companion was with him in that and equaled him therein, and the other added the martyrdom for the like of which he had offered himself, so he was in the meaning of the martyr, though the martyr by his merit in what befell him of killing, yet he had offered himself for that; then he lived after him a year in his emigration to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and for that of merit is the spending of his wealth, so he was distinct thereby over his companion, and in that he was praying the prayers of that period, and fasting the month of Ramadan which passed over him in it, and for that of merit is what it has; so there was nothing in that necessarily to be denied, his surpassing his companion in rank and in reward therefor, and in deserving precedence over him to Paradise. And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) has indeed said regarding one below his like."
"2637","2317 - What Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Shurayh informed me, from Sahl ibn Abi Umamah ibn Sahl, from his father, from Sahl ibn Hunayf, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever asks Allah Almighty for martyrdom truthfully from his heart, Allah Almighty conveys him to the stations of the martyrs, even if he dies in his bed. Abu Jafar said: And the states of the man we mentioned, in his emigration to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his remaining with him to be employed in what he employs him, and his performing righteous deeds, and his offering himself for the causes of martyrdom, is above that; and Allah we ask for guidance."
"2638","Chapter 361 - Explanation of the difficulty in what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding one who catches a rakah of prayer, that he has caught the prayer, and its merit. 2318 - al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Aswad al-Nadr ibn Abd al-Jabbar al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Nafi ibn Yazid narrated to us, from Ibn al-Had, from Abd al-Wahhab ibn Abi Bakr, from Ibn Shihab, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever catches a rakah of prayer has caught the prayer and its merit. Abu Jafar said: We examined this hadith and found no one narrated it from Ibn Shihab with catching the prayer and its merit other than Abd al-Wahhab ibn Abi Bakr, and he is acceptable in narration; and we found al-Layth ibn Sad narrated it from Ibn al-Had, from Ibn Shihab, without mention of Abd al-Wahhab in it, and without mention of catching the merit of prayer."
"2639","2319 - As Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah and Fahd narrated to us, they said: Abdillah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to us, from Ibn al-Had, from Ibn Shihab, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whoever catches a rakah of prayer has caught the prayer. So in that was what obliged us to examine it, so we examined it and found the catcher of prayer catching its merit; so what al-Layth narrated thereof sufficed us from what Nafi added thereto therein. Then we examined it from the narration of other than Abd al-Wahhab and other than Ibn al-Had, from Ibn Shihab, how it is."
"2640","2320 - We found Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Malik narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever catches a rakah of prayer has caught the prayer."
"2641","2321 - And we found Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Qutaybah ibn Said informed us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, raising it, he said: Whoever catches a rakah of prayer has caught it. Abu Jafar said: So it agreed with what al-Layth narrated thereof also, and opposed what Nafi narrated; and we understood that that catching is only of the merit of prayer, not the catching of prayer itself, for if it were catching of itself, the making up of its remainder would not be obligatory upon him. And since that is so, we examined what many people of knowledge say regarding the catcher of this measure of prayer, that he is thereby a catcher of it as to the obligation of its prescription upon him, and as to making up what he missed of it upon the like of what its catchers prayed; and they make one who catches less than that of it contrary to that, until the Hijazis among them said regarding the menstruating woman who becomes pure from her menstruation while there remains of the time of the prayer in whose time she became pure a measure of a rakah of it: that its making up is obligatory upon her; and regarding the boy when he reaches puberty in such time of it, and regarding the Christian when he embraces Islam in such time of it: that they both make up that prayer; and that these three we mentioned, if that were of them while there remains of the time of that prayer less than a rakah, they are contrary to that and making it up is not obligatory upon them. And they said the like of that regarding Friday prayer: whoever catches of it a rakah makes up another, and whoever catches of it less than a rakah prays four; and they argue in that with the hadith we narrated at the beginning of this chapter. And we found of the proof against them, for their opponents therein of the Iraqis, of those who say regarding menstruating women when they become pure in the time of prayer, while there remains of its time a measure in which they wash and enter it with a takbirah, and it is the least little of it, that making up that prayer is obligatory upon them; and they say the like of that regarding boys when they reach puberty and Christians when they embrace Islam; and they say regarding one who enters in the tashahhud in Friday prayer: that he is thereby of its people and that he makes up what remains upon him of Friday prayer, and they made him therein like the catcher of a rakah of it, that there has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding catching little of prayer the like of what has been narrated from him in the reports we mentioned regarding catching a rakah of it."
"2642","2322 - As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Yaqub ibn Ishaq al-Hadrami narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Yala ibn Ata, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, he said: We visited a man of the companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of the Ansar while he was ill, so he said: Who is in the house? It was said: Your family and your child and your sitters in the mosque. He said: Seat me. So his son propped him to his chest, then said: I shall narrate to you today a hadith I have not narrated since I heard it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) seeking reward, and I do not narrate it to you today except seeking reward. I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: When the Muslim servant performs ablution and perfects ablution, then heads to the mosque, he does not lift his right foot but a good deed is written for him thereby, and does not put down the left but a sin is removed from him thereby, until he reaches the mosque; so let him draw near or go far. If he catches prayer in congregation with the people, what preceded of his sin is forgiven him; and if he catches some of it and some precedes him, then makes up what he missed and perfects its bowing and prostration, he is likewise; and if he comes while the people are sitting, he is likewise. So in this hadith, regarding catching the least little of prayer, is the like of what is in the first reports regarding catching a rakah of it; and since what is narrated regarding catching a rakah of it, its meaning is the meaning of catching merit, that indicated to their opponents that one who catches that of prayer is thereby of its people like the catchers of what is more than that of it; what we narrated in this hadith indicated to them that the catcher of its least is in the ruling of the catcher of that of it; and Allah knows best. And of those who held this saying of the Iraqis were Abu Hanifah and Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, except that Muhammad opposed Abu Hanifah and Abu Yusuf regarding Friday, saying therein as the Hijazis said therein; and this we mentioned is the face of fairness in this chapter. If an objector says: What you narrated at the beginning of this chapter may have been after what you narrated at its end, so abrogating it, it is said to him: And it is possible that this hadith we narrated at its end abrogates it. And since that is so, the two proofs are equivalent, except that those of the other saying therein have, in carrying the other hadith upon addition to what is in the first hadith, that Allah Almighty, when He favors His servants with a blessing He bestows upon them, of reward for a deed they do for Him, does not abrogate it by cutting that reward from them, nor diminishes them thereof except by sins from them by which they deserve that; and of that is His Almighty saying: For wrongdoing on the part of those who became Jews We forbade them good things which had been lawful to them, the verse. And what is in the other hadith of reward additional to what is in the first hadith we narrated at the beginning of this chapter is a blessing from Allah upon His servants, and a favor He favored them with, so it is impossible that it abrogate that, and that it repel it from them except by sins from them by which they deserve that; and that was not of them, by the praise of Allah and His blessing. So there is established, by what we mentioned, the remaining of the ruling of what is in the other hadith and its non-abrogation, and established that inference by what is in it upon what is obligatory, of the disagreement we mentioned regarding what we mentioned the disagreement of people of knowledge therein first, over inference upon that by what is in the first hadith; besides that had we been left with analogy, what would be obligatory in our view regarding the menstruating woman we mentioned, and regarding the boy and the Christian we mentioned, is that making up the prayer we mentioned is not obligatory upon them except by catching of the time in which they became of the people of prayer its measure in full, just as nothing of fasting is obligatory upon them except what they catch its time in full. And Zufar used to hold this saying, except that what indicated contrary to it of what we narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) isworthier in our view than it; and Allah Almighty we ask for guidance."
"2643","Chapter 372 - Explanation of the difficulty in what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), of his saying: Tiyarah (evil omen) is upon whoever takes omens. 2323 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Muawiyah narrated to us, from Utbah ibn Humayd, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Abi Bakr narrated to me that he heard Anas saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No tiyarah, and tiyarah is upon whoever takes omens; and if it be in anything, then in woman and house and horse. Then an objector said: In this hadith is contradictory speech, for in it is: no tiyarah, and that is its negation, and in it is: whoever takes omens, it is upon himself, and that is its affirmation. So our answer to him, by the grace of Allah and His help, is that there is no contradiction in it as he thought; and His saying: no tiyarah is upon its negation, and His saying after that: whoever takes omens it is upon himself is not that thereby what he took omens by is upon himself in its reality, but he confuses it upon himself; for tiyarah is shirk, as he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said in what we narrated earlier in this book of ours, that tiyarah is shirk, and there is none of us but, but Allah removes it by reliance; that whoever has tiyarah of him has entered into this meaning, and what attached to him by his entering into it is upon himself, not upon another; and Allah, glory be to Him, we ask for guidance."
"2644","Chapter 373 - Explanation of the difficulty in what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), of his saying: There shall be hanat and hanat, so whoever seeks to divide the ummah of Muhammad while it is united, strike him with the sword whoever he be. 2324 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Samad ibn Abd al-Warith narrated to us, from Shubah, from Ziyad ibn Ilaqah, from Arfajah, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: There shall be hanat and hanat; so whoever seeks to divide the ummah of Muhammad while it is united, strike him with the sword whoever he be."
"2645","2325 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sulayman, meaning Luwayn, narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Abdillah ibn al-Mukhtar and Layth ibn Abi Sulaym and al-Mufaddal ibn Faddalah, from Ziyad ibn Ilaqah, from Arfajah, raising the hadith to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: There shall be hanat and hanat; so whomever you see walking to the ummah of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while it is united to divide them, kill him whoever he be."
"2646","2326 - Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Yahya al-Marwazi narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Uthman narrated to us, from Abu Hamzah, from Ziyad ibn Ilaqah, from Arfajah ibn Shurayh, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There shall be hanat and hanat; so whoever seeks to divide the ummah of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while it is united, strike him with the sword whoever he be."
"2647","2327 - And Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Yahya, meaning al-Sufi, narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Mardanabah narrated to us - and he is Kufi - from Ziyad ibn Ilaqah, from Arfajah ibn Shurayh al-Ashjai, he said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) upon the pulpit preaching to the people, and he said: After me there shall be hanat and hanat; so whomever you see parting the congregation, or seeking to divide the affair of the ummah of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) whoever he be, kill him; for the hand of Allah Almighty is with the congregation, and Satan is with whoever parts the congregation, tossing him."
"2648","2328 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Ayyash narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Yazid narrated to me, from Zayd ibn Abi Unaysah, from Ziyad ibn Ilaqah, from Arfajah ibn Sharahil, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whoever seeks to divide the ummah of Muhammad while its affair is united, kill him whoever he be."
"2649","2329 - Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to me, he said: Muhammad ibn Qudamah informed me, he said: Jarir, meaning Ibn Abd al-Hamid, narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Ata ibn al-Saib, from Ziyad ibn Ilaqah, from Usamah ibn Sharik, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whatever man goes out dividing my ummah, strike his neck. Abu Jafar said: Then an objector said: What is the meaning of what is in these reports? So our answer to him in that, by the grace of Allah Almighty, is that al-hanah is a metonymy for a hateful thing, and al-hanat is its plural; and he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed that after him there shall be hateful matters, metonymizing of them; then he clarified some of them by his saying: So whoever seeks to divide the ummah of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while it is united, strike him with the sword whoever he be. So he uncovered for them thereby a hanah of those hanat, and commanded them what to do when occurring upon it, with whoever occurs of his ummah upon it, and withheld from other than it that they refer it after its uncovering to them to what they do thereat of what he taught them or of what he teaches them in the future of the rulings of Allah in that; and Allah we ask for guidance."
"2650","Chapter 374 - Explanation of the difficulty in what is narrated regarding the shihab (flaming meteors) sent upon the devils listening to the news of the lowest heaven at the mission of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): was any of that before his mission or not? 2330 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Abu Bishr, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not recite to the jinn nor see them. He set out to the market of Ukaz, and the devils had been barred from the news of heaven, and the shihab were sent upon them; so the devils returned to their people and they said: What is with you? They said: We have been barred from the news of heaven, and the shihab were sent upon us. They said: Nothing barred you from the news of heaven except something that happened. Go to the easts of the earth and its wests, and see what this is that barred you from the news of heaven. So they set out striking the easts of the earth and its wests, seeking what barred them from the news of heaven. Those groups turned back toward Tihamah to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was at Nakhlah heading to the market of Ukaz, praying Fajr with his companions; when they heard the Quran they listened to it and said: This, by Allah, is what barred you from the news of heaven. That is when they returned to their people and said: O our people, we have heard a wondrous Quran guiding to rectitude, so we believed in it and shall never associate anyone with our Lord. So Allah Almighty revealed to His Prophet: Say: It has been revealed to me that a group listened, and only the saying of the jinn was revealed to him. Abu Jafar said: So in this hadith is what indicated that the shihab sent upon the devils then, preventing them from the news of heaven, were of what they did not know before that."
"2651","2331 - Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Israil narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: The jinn used to ascend to heaven listening to revelation; when they heard the word they added nine to it; as for the word, it would be true, and as for what they added, it would be false. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was sent, they were barred from their seats, so they mentioned that to Iblis; and the stars were not pelted with before that. Iblis said to them: This is only for a matter that happened in the earth. So he sent his troops and they found the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) standing praying between two mountains - he said: I think he said: at the heights of Makkah - al-Firyabi doubted - so they came to him and informed him, and he said: This is the event that happened in the earth. Abu Jafar said: So in this also is what confirmed what we mentioned, of the saying of Ibn Abbas therein: and it was not pelted with before that. Then an objector said: And you narrate from Ibn Abbas what contradicts what you narrated from him in these two hadiths, of what he mentioned from men of the Ansar of the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"2652","2332 - He mentioned what Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Bakr informed us, he said: al-Awzai informed me, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Ali ibn Husayn informed me that Abdillah ibn Abbas said: Men of the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of the Ansar informed me that while they were sitting one night with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), a star was pelted and it lit up, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to them: What did you say in Jahiliyyah when the like of this was pelted? They said: Allah Almighty and His Messenger know best; we used to say: Tonight a great man was born, and tonight a great man died. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It is not pelted for the death of anyone nor his life, but our Lord, blessed be His name, when He decrees a matter, the bearers of the Throne glorify, then the people of heaven next to them glorify, until glorification reaches the people of the lowest heaven. Those next to the bearers of the Throne say to the bearers of the Throne: What did your Lord say? So they inform them; then the people of the heavens ask one another until the news reaches this lowest heaven; then the jinn snatch hearing and cast it to their allies, and they are pelted therewith; so what they bring upon its face is true, but they add therein and increase."
"2653","2333 - And of what Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Yazid informed me, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Ali ibn al-Husayn informed me that Ibn Abbas said: Men of the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of the Ansar informed me, then he mentioned the like of it, except that he did not mention: and they are pelted."
"2654","2334 - And of what Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Kathir ibn Ubayd narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Harb, from al-Zubaydi, from al-Zuhri, then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it. He said: So in this hadith is the informing of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that it was pelted in Jahiliyyah. So our answer to him in that, by the grace of Allah Almighty and His help, is that what they were pelted with in Jahiliyyah may have been in a particular of times, then after the mission of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was in all times; and what indicates that is the saying of Allah Almighty in His informing of the jinn by their saying: And we used to sit therein in seats for hearing, meaning before they saw the shihab they saw after the mission of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): But whoever listens now finds for him a ready shihab, i.e. that he cannot do the like of what he could before that, of listening, with the shihab that occurred of what prevents that. And of that is His Almighty saying: We adorned the lowest heaven with an adornment of stars, to His saying: And are pelted from every side, repelled, and theirs is a lasting torment, i.e. that they are repelled, barred from that, and al-wasib is lasting, i.e. that it is lasting, uncut. And of that is His Almighty saying: And We adorned the lowest heaven with lamps and made them missiles for the devils, and prepared for them the torment of the Blaze. And that all is after the mission of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); and in that is what indicated that what was of that kind before his mission (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not cut them off from returning to what they were pelted for; and what occurred of that after the mission of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is contrary to that. And what Allah Almighty related of the jinn, of saying: But we found it filled with stern guards and shihab, confirms that, i.e. that the matter with which it was guarded is not of what was before that in anything, and that we have been barred from what we reached before that of that kind. Then an objector said: There has been narrated from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), what indicates contrary to this."
"2655","2335 - He mentioned what Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Muhammad ibn Amr al-Yafii informed me, from Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Shihab, from Yahya ibn Urwah, from his father, from Aishah, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: People asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about soothsayers, so he said: They are nothing. They said: O Messenger of Allah, they inform us of the thing sometimes and it is true. He said: That is the word of the jinn; the jinni memorizes it and pours it into the ear of his ally like the clucking of the hen, then they add therein more than a hundred lies."
"2656","2336 - And of what Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad ibn al-Hasan ibn Abi al-Hasan ibn Zubalah al-Madani narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Main narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Yusuf narrated to us, from Mamar, from al-Zuhri, then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it, except that he did not say therein: like the clucking of the hen. So our answer to him in that, by the grace of Allah Almighty and His help, is that this may be that they asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and he answered them with what he answered them of what is in this hadith, before what is mentioned in the hadith of Ibn Abbas from men of the Ansar; then came what is in this hadith of Ibn Abbas, so that abrogated it. So it is clear, by the praise of Allah and His blessing, that there is no contradiction in any of these reports we mentioned in this chapter; and Allah Almighty we ask for guidance."
"2657","Chapter 375 - Explanation of the difficulty in what came regarding the cause in which was revealed: Those whom they call seek to their Lord the means, the verse, of what is attributed to Abdillah ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, of what we know with certainty he did not say by opinion, but said it by instruction. 2336M - Ahmad ibn Dawud ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Hisham al-Sadusi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, from Abu Mamar, from Abdillah, he said: A group of men used to worship a group of jinn; so the jinn embraced Islam while the men remained upon their worship; so they are those of whom Allah Almighty said: Those whom they call seek to their Lord the means, which of them is nearest."
"2658","2336M - Dawud ibn Ibrahim ibn Dawud al-Farisi Abu Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Ala ibn Hammad al-Narsi narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray narrated to us, he said: Said narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Abdillah ibn Mabad al-Zimmani, from Abdillah ibn Utbah ibn Masud, from his uncle Abdillah ibn Masud, he said: It was revealed regarding a group of Arabs who used to worship a group of jinn; so the jinn embraced Islam while the group of Arabs did not feel that, meaning His Almighty saying: Say: Call those you claimed besides Him, they possess no removal of harm from you nor transfer; those whom they call seek to their Lord the means, which of them is nearest, and hope His mercy and fear His torment; indeed the torment of your Lord is to beware of."
"2659","So a denier denied these two hadiths and said: By this verse is only intended. He mentioned what Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Isa ibn Maymun, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid: They seek to their Lord the means. Isa and Uzayr (peace be upon them) and the angels. And this denier said: Those we know were worshipped besides Allah Almighty, not others of the jinn. So our answer to him in that, by the grace of Allah Almighty and His help, is that what Ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, said in that is worthier than what Mujahid said therein, by his place from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and the jinn; and we have found Allah Almighty informed us in His Book that some men used to worship them, by His Almighty saying: And the day He gathers them all, then says to the angels: Was it you these used to worship? They say: Glory be to You; You are our ally besides them; rather they used to worship the jinn; most of them are believers in them. And we know of no one of the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding the interpretation of the verse for which we brought this speech, other than what we narrated therein from Ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, in the first two hadiths; and it is not proper to oppose the like of that to the saying of Mujahid, especially since Ibn Masud informed in one of his two hadiths of its revelation concerning those human groups who used to worship the jinn groups; and Allah we ask for guidance."
"2660","Chapter 376 - Explanation of the difficulty in what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), of his saying: Whoever fasts Ramadan then follows it with six of Shawwal, it is as if he fasted the year. 2337 - Muhammad ibn Abdillah ibn Abd al-Hakam narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, he said: Ibn Luhayah narrated to us, he said: Abd Rabbihi ibn Said narrated to us, from Sad ibn Said, from Umar ibn Thabit, from Abu Ayyub, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever fasts Ramadan and six after it, that is the fasting of the year, as Ibn Abd al-Hakam supposes."
"2661","2338 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Amr, from Sad ibn Said, from Umar ibn Thabit, from Abu Ayyub al-Ansari, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever fasts Ramadan and six of Shawwal has fasted the year."
"2662","2339 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Habban ibn Hilal narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Amr, from Umar ibn Thabit - and he did not mention Sad - from Abu Ayyub, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"2663","2340 - Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Abdillah ibn al-Hakam narrated to us, from Muhammad, meaning Ghundar, he said: Shubah narrated to us, he said: I heard Warqa, from Sad ibn Said, from Umar ibn Thabit, from Abu Ayyub, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Whoever fasts Ramadan and six of Shawwal, it is as if he fasted the age. Abu Jafar said: This hadith was of what was not strong in our hearts, because of what Sad ibn Said is upon in narration with people of hadith, and of their turning away from him, until we found those we mentioned, of people of majesty in narration and firmness therein, taking it from him; so we mentioned his hadith for that. Except that Muhammad ibn Amr narrated it once from him, and once from his shaykh who narrated it from him, and he is Umar ibn Thabit; and of those who narrated it also is Qurrah ibn Abd al-Rahman, and perhaps his age is as his age."
"2664","2341 - As al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Qurrah ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Maafiri informed me, from Sad ibn Said al-Ansari, he narrated to him from Umar ibn Thabit al-Mazini, from Abu Ayyub al-Ansari, that he narrated to them in the year of sacrifice that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever fasts Ramadan and six of Shawwal, it is as if he fasted the year. And of those who narrated it from him also is Sufyan ibn Uyaynah."
"2665","2342 - As Ahmad ibn Abdillah al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: al-Humaydi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, he said: Sad ibn Said narrated to me, from Umar ibn Thabit, from Abu Ayyub, he said: Whoever fasts Ramadan then follows it with six of Shawwal, it is as if he fasted the age. al-Humaydi said: I said to Sufyan, or it was said to him: They raise it, he said: Be silent, I have known that. And we found this hadith also narrated from Umar ibn Thabit by Safwan ibn Sulaym and Zayd ibn Aslam."
"2666","2343 - As Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad narrated to us, he said: Safwan ibn Sulaym and Zayd ibn Aslam informed me, from Umar ibn Thabit, from Abu Ayyub al-Ansari, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever fasts Ramadan and follows it with six of Shawwal, it is as if he fasted the age."
"2667","2344 - Ahmad ibn Abdillah al-Barqi narrated to us, he said: al-Humaydi narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad al-Darawardi narrated to us, from Safwan ibn Sulaym and Sad ibn Said, from Umar ibn Thabit, from Abu Ayyub al-Ansari, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. And we found of those who narrated it also from Sad ibn Said, Hafs ibn Ghiyath."
"2668","2345 - As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Habban ibn Hilal narrated to us, he said: Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, he said: Sad ibn Said narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Thabit narrated to me, from Abu Ayyub al-Ansari, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. And we found of those who narrated it from Umar ibn Thabit also Yahya ibn Said al-Ansari."
"2669","2346 - As Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Ammar narrated to us, from Sadaqah, he said: Utbah narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Bakr narrated to me, he said: Yahya ibn Said narrated to me, from Umar ibn Thabit, he said: We raided with Abu Ayyub al-Ansari, so he fasted Ramadan and we fasted; then when we broke fast he stood among the people and said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whoever fasts Ramadan and fasts six days of Shawwal, it is as the fasting of the age. And we found of those who narrated it also from this Umar, Abd Rabbihi ibn Said al-Ansari."
"2670","2347 - As Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abdillah ibn al-Hakam informed us, he said: Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Muqri narrated to us, he said: Shubah ibn al-Hajjaj narrated to us, from Abd Rabbihi ibn Said, from Umar ibn Thabit, from Abu Ayyub al-Ansari - and he did not raise it - that he said: Whoever fasts the month of Ramadan then follows it with six days of Shawwal, it is as if he fasted the year. And we found this hadith also narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) by Thawban his freedman and Jabir ibn Abdillah al-Ansari."
"2671","2348 - As Sulayman ibn Shuayb al-Kaysani narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hassan narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hamzah narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn al-Harith al-Dhimari narrated to me, from Abu Asma al-Rahabi, from Thawban, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The fasting of Ramadan is ten months, and the fasting of six days two months; that is the fasting of a year, meaning Ramadan and six after it."
"2672","2349 - Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Mahmud ibn Khalid informed me, he said: Muhammad ibn Shuayb ibn Shabur narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn al-Harith informed us, he said: Abu Asma al-Rahabi narrated to me, from Thawban, freedman of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Allah made the good deed ten months, and six days after the breaking of fast the completion of the year."
"2673","2350 - And as al-Rabi al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Ibn Luhayah and Bakr ibn Mudar and Said ibn Abi Ayyub informed me, from Amr ibn Jabir al-Hadrami, he said: I heard Jabir ibn Abdillah saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whoever fasts Ramadan and six of Shawwal, it is as if he fasted the whole year."
"2674","2351 - And as Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hassan narrated to us, he said: Ibn Luhayah and Bakr ibn Mudar both narrated to us, from Amr ibn Jabir al-Hadrami, from Jabir ibn Abdillah, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. Then an objector said: How is it permissible for you to accept the like of this from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), of what is in it that fasting other than Ramadan equals the fasting of Ramadan, while there is no disagreement that no fast is better than the fast of Ramadan? So our answer to him in that, by the grace of Allah Almighty and His help, is that the merit of the fast of Ramadan is as mentioned, but Allah Almighty may give for the performance of His obligations of reward what He bestows upon His servants; of that is what we narrated earlier in this book of ours, of the hadith of Said ibn al-Musayyib, from the Ansari whom he did not name, of the companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), of his saying: When the servant performs ablution and perfects ablution, then heads to the mosque, he does not lift his right foot but a good deed is written for him thereby, and does not put down the left but a sin is removed from him thereby; if he catches prayer in congregation with the people, what preceded of his sin is forgiven him. And since that is so, it is not objectionable that He Almighty expiate for one who fasts Ramadan with faith and seeking reward what was of him before that of sins."
"2675","2352 - As al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Usamah ibn Zayd al-Laythi informed me, he said: I heard Umar ibn Ishaq, freedman of Zaidah, he said: I heard my father saying: Abu Hurayrah met Kab al-Ahbar and said: How do you find Ramadan in the Book of Allah? Kab said: Rather how did you hear your companion saying therein? He said: I heard him saying: Whoever fasts Ramadan with faith and seeking reward, what preceded of his sin is forgiven him. Kab said: And I, by Him in whose Hand is my soul, indeed find it in the Book of Allah Almighty as a remission; Allah Almighty remits thereby sins."
"2676","2353 - And as al-Rabi narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Yazid informed me, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf informed me that Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying of Ramadan: Whoever stands it with faith and seeking reward, what preceded of his sin is forgiven him."
"2677","2354 - And as al-Rabi narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Malik informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Abu Salamah and Humayd, sons of Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. Thus Malik and Yunus ibn Yazid narrated this hadith from al-Zuhri; as for Ibn Uyaynah, he narrated it from al-Zuhri contrary to that."
"2678","2355 - As al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: al-Shafii narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever fasts Ramadan with faith and seeking reward, what preceded of his sin is forgiven him. And it is possible that the reality of the hadith is upon fasting and standing together; so we looked whether we find what indicates that."
"2679","2356 - We found Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Jafar narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Amr, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever fasts the month of Ramadan and stands it with faith and seeking reward, what preceded of his sin is forgiven him; and whoever stands Laylat al-Qadr with faith and seeking reward, what preceded of his sin is forgiven him. So that indicated that the reality of the hadith is upon them together, since Ramadan is obligatory in its fasting, and recommended in its standing."
"2680","2357 - We found Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Iyad narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Alqamah, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever fasts Ramadan and stands it with faith and seeking reward, what preceded of his sin is forgiven him."
"2681","2358 - al-Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Amr informed us, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, then he mentioned the like of it. And Allah Almighty expiates for him with that what is of him in the remainder of the ten months of his year; then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) urged people after that to fast six days of Shawwal, so it is as tenfold its likes, as He Almighty said: Whoever brings a good deed has ten likes of it; so that, with what He Almighty bestowed by the fast of the month of Ramadan, is an expiation for the whole year; and Allah Almighty we ask for guidance."
"2682","Chapter 377 - Explanation of the difficulty in what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding which of his nine wives he left, whom he died leaving, he did not apportion for: who of them is she? 2359 - Abu al-Qasim Hisham ibn Muhammad ibn Qurrah ibn Abi Khalifah al-Ruayni narrated to us, he said: Abu Jafar Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Salamah al-Azdi narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: I attended the funeral of Maymunah, may Allah be pleased with her, with Ibn Abbas, and he said: This is the wife of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so-and-so, so do not shake her, and be gentle with her; for there were nine with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he used to apportion for eight, and did not apportion for one; and she for whom he did not apportion is Safiyyah. Abu Jafar said: The meaning by which he did not apportion for Safiyyah had been obscure to me, until I asked about it more than one of those asked about its like, and I found nothing with them therein, until I myself ascertained that Ibn Jurayj erred regarding the woman for whom the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not apportion of his wives; for he mentioned that she was Safiyyah, and she was not Safiyyah, but she was Sawdah."
"2683","2360 - As Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Abi Maryam narrated to me, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Dinar narrated to me, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, from Abdillah ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died leaving nine women he visited except Sawdah, for she gave her day and night to Aishah, may Allah be pleased with them all. So I ascertained thereby that the woman for whom there was no apportioning was Sawdah, and that that was only of him by her goodwill and her transferring it to Aishah; and that was worthier to carry the abandonment by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of apportioning for her upon, since it was of his sunnah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) justice between his wives and fulfilling their rights of himself, and his warning his ummah against contrary to that, of inclining to some of their wives to the exclusion of some."
"2684","2361 - As Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us, he said: Hammam ibn Yahya narrated to us, from Qatadah, from al-Nadr ibn Anas, from Bashir ibn Nahik, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever has two wives inclining with one of them against the other comes on the Day of Resurrection with one of his sides inclining. And the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is the worthiest of people to abandon what he forbids; and in what we mentioned is what indicated the correctness of what we narrated regarding this wife for whom he did not apportion (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who she is, and what the cause is for which he did not apportion for her; and that is as in the hadith of Amr ibn Dinar, from Ata, not as in the hadith of Ibn Jurayj, from Ata; and Allah we ask for guidance. And there has been narrated from Aishah regarding the gift of Sawdah to her of her day, and that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to apportion for her by her day and by the day Sawdah gave her."
"2685","2362 - As Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Muawiyah narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Urwah narrated to us, from Urwah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that Sawdah daughter of Zamah gave her day to Aishah, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to apportion for Aishah by her day and the day of Sawdah, to its end; and Allah we ask for guidance."
"2686","Chapter 378 - Explanation of the difficulty in what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), of the bequest regarding the Copts of Egypt, and his informing therein that they have dhimmah (protection) and kinship. 2363 - Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Harmalah ibn Imran narrated to me, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Shimasah al-Mahri, he said: I heard Abu Dharr saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You shall conquer a land in which the qirat is mentioned, so enjoin good regarding its people; for they have dhimmah and kinship. If you see two brothers fighting over a brick site, leave it. So he passed by Rabiah and Abd al-Rahman, sons of Shurahbil ibn Hasanah, fighting over a brick site, and left it. Abu Jafar said: In this hadith was his informing that they have kinship. So we sought what is narrated from him regarding that kinship, what it is."
"2687","2364 - We found Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Sabbah narrated to us, he said: al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, from al-Awzai, from al-Zuhri, from a son of Kab ibn Malik, from his father, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If you enter Egypt, enjoin good regarding the Copts of Egypt; for they have dhimmah and kinship."
"2688","2365 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim also narrated to us, he said: al-Walid ibn Shuja ibn al-Walid narrated to us, he said: al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to me, then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it."
"2689","2366 - And Ishaq ibn Ibrahim also narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Muslim ibn Warah narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Musa ibn Ayan narrated to me, he said: My father narrated to us, from Ishaq ibn Rashid, from al-Zuhri, from Abdillah ibn Kab, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), similar, and he added therein, meaning that the mother of Ismail (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was of them. So we understood thereby that that kinship he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioned is from Hajar, mother of Ismail (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Then an objector said: What is the meaning of his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding the dhimmah he mentioned for them, while they then are people of war with no dhimmah for them? So our answer to him in that, by the grace of Allah Almighty and His help, is that the dhimmah he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intended therein is the right for them by their kinship; so that was a covenant for them obliging its observance for them, like what has been said regarding the saying of Allah Almighty: They observe toward a believer neither pact nor dhimmah. He said: al-dhimmah here is of al-tadhammum (claiming protection)."
"2690","As Wallad al-Nahwi narrated to us, he said: al-Masadiri narrated to us, from Abu Ubaydah Mamar ibn al-Muthanna al-Taymi, regarding the saying of Allah Almighty: They observe toward a believer neither pact nor dhimmah. He said: al-dhimmah here is of al-tadhammum. So like that is what we mentioned regarding the meaning of his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): for they have dhimmah; and Allah, glory be to Him, we ask for guidance."
"2691","Chapter 379 - Explanation of the difficulty in what is narrated from Ibn Abbas, that he took only from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), in explanation of the difficulty of the saying of Allah Almighty: Indeed among your wives and children are enemies to you, so beware of them. Allah Almighty said: O you who believe, indeed among your wives and children are enemies to you, so beware of them. Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abi Bukayr al-Kirmani narrated to us, from Israil ibn Yunus, from Simak ibn Harb, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, regarding the saying of Allah Almighty: O you who believe, indeed among your wives and children are enemies to you, so beware of them. He said: These are a people of the people of Makkah who embraced Islam, but their wives and children refused to let them emigrate; then when they came to Madinah and saw people had understood religion, they intended to punish them, so this verse was revealed: And if you pardon and overlook and forgive, then Allah is Forgiving, Merciful."
"2692","Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, from Israil, then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it. Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Israil narrated to us, then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it. Abu Jafar said: So by this hadith is clear the face which Allah Almighty informed in the verse we recited, of the meaning by which some of their wives and children were enemies to them; and that their preventing them was from emigration to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), until they be as others who preceded them in emigration, until they thereby attained understanding in the religion of Allah; then He commanded them pardon and overlooking of them and forgiveness for them, when they intended punishing them for that, since they were punishments by which they would attain nothing; and in that is what indicated that He intended of the ummah of His Prophet not to obey wife nor child in barring from obedience to Allah, and informed them that whoever of them attempts that is an enemy to them; and Allah Almighty we ask for guidance."
"2693","Chapter 380 - Explanation of the difficulty in what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding overlooking the slips of people of rank, except in a hadd of the hudud of Allah Almighty. 2367 - al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Nafi al-Madini, freedman of the Umaris, narrated to us, he said: I heard Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr ibn Amr ibn Hazm saying: Amrah daughter of Abd al-Rahman said: Aishah said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Overlook the slips of people of rank. He said: And Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr judged thereby regarding a man of the family of Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, who wounded a man and struck him, so he released him and said: You are of people of rank."
"2694","2368 - Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Amr ibn al-Harith narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Nafi, freedman of the Umaris, narrated to us, then he mentioned the like of it, except that he did not mention therein what was of Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr in releasing the Umari, and in his saying to him what he said to him."
"2695","2369 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Nafi narrated to us, he said: I heard Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr ibn Hazm saying: Amrah said: Aishah said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Overlook the lapses of people of rank. Abu Jafar said: We examined these reports and found them all returning to Abu Bakr ibn Nafi; so it was possible that this Abu Bakr is Abu Bakr ibn Nafi, freedman of Abdillah ibn Umar, of whom Malik ibn Anas narrated; so if so, he is a man acceptable in narration; so we looked into that."
"2696","2370 - We found Muhammad ibn Sulayman al-Baghandi narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Abd al-Wahhab al-Hajabi narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Nafi, freedman of Zayd ibn al-Khattab, narrated to us, he said: I heard Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr ibn Amr ibn Hazm saying: Amrah said: Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Overlook the lapses of people of rank. Abu Jafar said: We understood thereby that he is other than the Abu Bakr ibn Nafi of whom Malik narrated, and that in reality he is the freedman of Zayd ibn al-Khattab, not the freedman of Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with them both."
"2697","2371 - Then we found Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Maslamah ibn Qanab narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Nafi al-Madini narrated to us, from Abu al-Rijal Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Amrah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Overlook the slips of people of rank. So in this hadith was, in place of Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr in what we narrated before it, Abu al-Rijal; and this Yahya was opposed therein by Abu Amir and Said ibn Mansur and Asad ibn Musa and Abdillah ibn Abd al-Wahhab al-Hajabi, who mentioned that it is from Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr; and four are worthier of retention than one. Then we looked: was anything narrated therein from other than this face?"
"2698","2372 - We found Fahd and Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, they said: Said ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: al-Attaf ibn Khalid al-Makhzumi informed me, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr ibn Hazm informed me, from his father, from Amrah daughter of Abd al-Rahman, from Aishah, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Overlook the slips of people of rank. Abu Jafar said: This hadith came by way of Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakr, of the narration of al-Attaf of it from him; and we did not hear of this Abd al-Rahman mention in other than this hadith. Then we looked: was this hadith narrated from other than these faces?"
"2699","2373 - We found Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhammad ibn al-Mughirah narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi al-Rijal narrated to us - Abu Jafar said: and he is Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi al-Rijal, and he is praiseworthy in his narration - from Ibn Abi Dhib, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Abdillah ibn Abdillah ibn Umar ibn al-Khattab, he said: A freedman of mine called Sallam al-Barbari, whom I wounded, claimed against me to Ibn Hazm; so he came to me and said: Did you wound him? I said: Yes. He said: I heard from my maternal aunt Amrah saying: Aishah said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Overlook the slips of people of rank. So he freed his way and did not punish him. Abu Jafar said: We looked: was Ibn Abi al-Rijal opposed, from Ibn Abi Dhib, in the isnad of this hadith or not?"
"2700","2374 - We found Yunus narrated to us, he said: Man ibn Isa al-Qazzaz narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Dhib, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Abdillah, from Abu Bakr ibn Hazm, from Amrah daughter of Abd al-Rahman, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Overlook the slips of people of rank. So we ascertained that Man ibn Isa opposed Ibn Abi al-Rijal in the isnad of this hadith, from Ibn Abi Dhib; so he narrated it from him cut off, halted upon Amrah. Then we looked: was it narrated from other than the way of Ibn Abi Dhib, from the shaykh whom Ibn Abi Dhib narrated it from?"
"2701","2375 - We found Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Hatim informed us, he said: Suwayd ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Abdillah, meaning Ibn al-Mubarak, informed us, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Abdillah ibn Umar, from Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn Hazm, from his father, from Amrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he mentioned it. So we ascertained thereby the cutting off by Ibn al-Mubarak of it, and his agreeing therein with Man ibn Isa, and his opposing therein Ibn Abi al-Rijal. Then we looked: was this hadith narrated from other than these faces?"
"2702","2376 - We found Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala and Muhammad ibn Abdillah ibn Abd al-Hakam both narrated to us, they said: Muhammad ibn Ismail ibn Abi Fudayk narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Yazid, from Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr ibn Hazm, from his father, from Amrah daughter of Abd al-Rahman, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that she said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Overlook the slips of people of rank, except a hadd of the hudud of Allah Almighty. Then we sought to ascertain who this Abd al-Malik ibn Zayd is. We found him Abd al-Malik ibn Zayd ibn Said ibn Zayd ibn Amr ibn Nufayl; thus Duhaym mentioned him, from Ibn Abi Fudayk, in other than this hadith. Then we looked: did other than Ibn Abi Fudayk narrate this hadith from this Abd al-Malik?"
"2703","2377 - We found Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Ali narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Malik ibn Zayd al-Madani narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr, from his father, from Amrah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Overlook the slips of people of rank, except the hudud. So we ascertained the narration of Ibn Abi Fudayk and Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi of this hadith from this Abd al-Malik ibn Zayd; so it became from two just men of people of hadith from him, and this hadith became strong in our hearts, and we needed to ascertain its meaning. We found the early people of knowledge made those intended by overlooking those lapses the imams, and made them commanded to overlook them from people of rank. Then we looked into people of rank."
"2704","2378 - We found al-Hasan ibn Abdillah ibn Mansur al-Balisi Abu Ali narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abd al-Aziz ibn Umar ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf narrated to us, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Abdillah ibn Abdillah ibn Umar, from his father, from his grandfather, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Overlook the punishment of people of manliness, and he is the righteous one. So we understood thereby that people of rank in the reports whose narration preceded are the righteous, no others. Then we sought what people of knowledge said regarding those intended by that command, and we found of them who say: they are the imams to whom is the establishment of punishments for sins, and that they should comply with that in whoever commits them, except what of that is of the hudud of Allah. And of those who said that of them are Abu Hanifah, may Allah have mercy on him, and Abu Yusuf and Muhammad ibn al-Hasan. As Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, from his father, from Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, from Abu Yusuf, from Abu Hanifah, and he did not relate therein disagreement. And there has been narrated from al-Shafii, may Allah have mercy on him, what indicates that he held this doctrine also, as al-Rabi related to us from him, by hearing or license from him to us, in what he mentioned in the siyar of al-Waqidi. And of them are who rejected this hadith, of them Malik ibn Anas, may Allah have mercy on him, as Ashhab ibn Abd al-Aziz mentioned of his denial of this hadith, and his negating it from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Then we examined the meaning of this hadith and found it possible that those intended by the command to overlook the lapses of those described therein are those to whom belong claims for punishments for the due disciplines by those lapses, from people of rank; since they were not their character nor habit, but were a slip for them, so it was better of them to pardon them therefor, and to leave their rights therein from them, as they may pardon all their rights other than them, except the imams to whom those rights do not belong, so they are not commanded to overlook them. And this meaning was strengthened by the saying of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Your bloods and wealth and honors are inviolable to you."
"2705","2379 - As al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Hatim ibn Ismail narrated to us, he said: Jafar ibn Muhammad ibn Ali narrated to us, from his father, from Jabir ibn Abdillah, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), thereby."
"2706","2380 - And as Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Hawdhah ibn Khalifah Abu al-Ashhab al-Bakrawi narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Awn narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakrah, from Abu Bakrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), thereby also."
"2707","2381 - And Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Muhammad al-Muaddib narrated to us, he said: Husayn ibn Azib narrated to us, from Shabib ibn Gharqadah, from Sulayman ibn Amr ibn al-Ahwas, from Amr ibn al-Ahwas, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), thereby also, except that he did not say therein: and your wealth. He said: And what was obligatory of rights in inviolable wealth, and in inviolable blood, of punishments, pardoning them is to their people to whom they were due, not to the imams who establish them for them; so like that, rights in honors are only overlooking them, and pardoning them is to their people whom the imams take them for, not to the imams who take them for them. Then an objector said: What is the meaning of his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): except a hadd of the hudud of Allah Almighty, or: except the hudud? So our answer to him in that, by the grace of Allah and His help, is that what is commanded to be overlooked and pardoned of whoever it was of, of what we mentioned of slips and lapses, is only from one with whom is manliness or rank, those whom what was of them of lapses and slips did not remove from what they were upon before that, of manliness and ranks which are righteousness; so they deserved thereby overlooking for them, and pardon of them. As for one who commits what necessitates a hadd, either slander of a chaste woman, or other than that of things necessitating hudud, he has thereby left the meaning for which overlooking the lapses of its people is commanded, and has thereby become a fasiq committing major sins for whose committers the threat of Allah Almighty preceded, with punishments therefor, and the attaching of fisq to them therefor, and the dropping of justice in testimonies of them therefor; and whoever becomes so, Allah Almighty imposed upon the imams tazir therein, and upon those of rights obligatory for them therein the establishment of their punishments upon them, that it be a deterrent for them and for others from committing the like of that and returning to it, and for establishing proof for what necessitates tafsiq of whoever must be made fasiq of them, until no testimony is accepted for them after that against anyone of the servants of Allah Almighty, as Allah Almighty judged therein; and Allah we ask for guidance."
"2708","Chapter 381 - Explanation of the difficulty in what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), of his saying to Safwan ibn Umayyah, when he gave as charity his cloak to its thief from him, after the command of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to cut him: Why not before you brought him to me. 2382 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abu Kurayb narrated to us, he said: Hashim ibn Abd al-Wahid narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abd al-Aziz, from Ashath, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: Safwan ibn Umayyah came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with a man who stole his cloak from under his head while he slept, and that man did not deny it; so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered him to be cut. Safwan said: For this he is cut? He said: Why did you not say this before you brought him to me. He said: If a denier denies our arguing with this hadith because of Ashath ibn Sawwar, it is said to him: Ashath is not abandoned in hadith, and none of the imams of hadith in his time held back from him until they narrated from him, of them Shubah and al-Thawri; and there narrated from him one more eminent than this class, and he is Abu Ishaq al-Sabii. And al-Bukhari mentioned from Abu Bakr ibn Abi al-Aswad, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi, he said: Sufyan said: Ashath is firmer with me than Mujalid; and this is an eminent rank."
"2709","2383 - And Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Malik ibn Anas narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, from Safwan ibn Abdillah ibn Safwan, that Safwan ibn Umayyah was told: Whoever does not emigrate perishes; so Safwan ibn Umayyah came to Madinah and slept in the mosque pillowing his cloak; then a thief came and took his cloak from under his head; so Safwan seized the thief and brought him to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered his hand cut. Safwan said: I did not intend this; it is charity upon him. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Why not before you brought him to me. He said: Thus Ibn Wahb and most people narrated this hadith from Malik; and Shababah ibn Sawwar narrated it from him contrary to this isnad."
"2710","2384 - As Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn Jafar narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Shababah ibn Sawwar narrated to us, he said: Malik ibn Anas narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab, from Abdillah ibn Safwan, from his father Safwan ibn Umayyah: he was told: Whoever does not emigrate perishes; so he called his mount and rode it until he came to Madinah; then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asked him and he said: I was told: Whoever does not emigrate perishes; so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Emigration is gone; go to the plain of Makkah. So Safwan slept in the mosque, then he mentioned this hadith as Ibn Wahb mentioned it from Malik. And Abu Alqamah al-Farwi agreed with Shababah upon this isnad in this hadith. And since the isnad of this hadith is as we mentioned, it is possible that al-Zuhri heard it from Abdillah ibn Safwan, from his father, and heard it from Safwan ibn Abdillah, so narrated it once thus and once thus, as he does in his hadiths from others of whom he narrates. If an objector says: Is it feasible in his age to meet Abdillah ibn Safwan? It is said to him: Yes, that is not objectionable; for Abdillah ibn Safwan was killed with Abdillah ibn al-Zubayr on the day he was killed, of year seventy-three; and al-Zuhri then was fourteen years old, for his birth was in the year Husayn ibn Ali, may Allah be pleased with them both, was killed, and it is year sixty-one. Then an objector said: It is possible that Abdillah ibn Safwan is the son of Abdillah ibn Safwan. It is said to him: We know of no son of Safwan ibn Abdillah ibn Safwan from whom anything of knowledge was taken; but Abdillah ibn Safwan ibn Umayyah."
"2711","2385 - And Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Qatadah and Qays and Habib al-Muallim and Humayd and Umarah, from Ata, from Safwan ibn Umayyah; and Hammad, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Tawus, that Safwan ibn Umayyah was sleeping in the mosque with a khamisah under his head; then a thief came and snatched it from under his head; so he seized him and raised him to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who ordered his cutting. He said: O Messenger of Allah, do not cut him. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Why did you not leave him before you brought him to us. So we looked into this hadith: is it hearing verbatim from Safwan or not?"
"2712","2386 - We found Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to me, he said: Muhammad ibn Jafar narrated to us, from Said, and he is Ibn Abi Arubah, from Qatadah, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, from Tariq ibn al-Muraqqa, from Safwan ibn Umayyah, then he mentioned this hadith. So we ascertained thereby that Ata did not take it from Safwan, and that he took it from this Tariq, from Safwan, though we do not know this Tariq."
"2713","2387 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr, from Tawus, he said: It was said to Safwan ibn Umayyah: There is no religion for whoever does not emigrate. He said: By Allah, I shall not reach anything until I emigrate to Madinah. So he came to Madinah and lodged with al-Abbas; while he was sleeping in the mosque with a khamisah of his under his head, then he mentioned this hadith. So we looked: did Tawus take it from Safwan by hearing?"
"2714","2388 - We found Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Dawud al-Missisi narrated to us, he said: Mualla ibn Asad narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb narrated to us, from Abdillah ibn Tawus, from his father, from Safwan ibn Umayyah, he said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, this one stole a khamisah of mine, belonging to a man with him, then he mentioned this hadith. Then we looked into the age of Tawus, what is possible that he took this hadith from Safwan by hearing from him. We found the death of Safwan was in Makkah when people left for al-Jamal; and we found the death of Tawus was in Makkah year one hundred six, and his age then some seventy years; so we understood thereby that it is not possible that he took it from Safwan by hearing."
"2715","2389 - And Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Talhah al-Qannad narrated to us, he said: Asbat ibn Nasr al-Hamdani narrated to us, from Simak, from Humayd, nephew of Safwan ibn Umayyah, from Safwan ibn Umayyah, he said: I was sleeping in the mosque upon a khamisah of mine worth thirty dirhams; then a man came and snatched it from me; so the man was seized and brought to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who ordered his hand cut. I came to him and said: Do you cut him for thirty dirhams? I sell it to him and defer its price to him. He said: Why not before you brought him to me. And this Humayd was of those not known; and we found in this chapter nothing other than what we mentioned therein, of what is in their isnads what we mentioned therein. Except that we found people of knowledge argued with this hadith, so we ascertained thereby its soundness with them, as we ascertained the soundness with them of the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): No bequest for an heir; and as we ascertained the soundness with them of his saying: When the two traders differ in price, while the commodity stands, they both swear and undo the sale; though all that does not stand from the side of isnad. So they sufficed by its soundness with them from seeking isnad for it; so like that, the hadith of Safwan we mentioned, when they all argued with it they sufficed thereby from isnad for it. Then we examined the saying of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) therein to Safwan: Why not before you brought him to me, since people of knowledge differ in this question. A party of them say: He is cut, and no heed to what was of the owner of theft, of giving it as charity upon the thief; of them Malik and al-Shafii and many of the Hijazis other than them; and it is one of the sayings of Abu Yusuf therein. And they differ therein if it were before he is brought to the imam: the Hijazis we mentioned say: He is cut, and Ibn Abi Layla agrees with them upon that; and Abu Yusuf says: He is not cut. And a party of them say: He is not cut in any of that, with the occurrence of his ownership upon the theft before he is taken with it to the imam and after he is taken with it to him; and of them Abu Hanifah and Muhammad ibn al-Hasan. And the saying of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Safwan: Why not before you brought him to me, is of what indicated that giving as charity of the stolen thing before he is taken with it to the imam, its ruling is contrary to the ruling of giving as charity thereby upon him after he is taken with it to the imam; and were it not that that is so, the saying of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Safwan: Why not before you brought him to me, would have no meaning. And we found people of knowledge do not differ regarding the thief, when he confesses theft with the imam and mentions to him its measure and his stealing it from its hirz and his removing it from that hirz, from a man absent from him with no kinship between him and him, that he is cut therein, though the owner of theft does not dispute him therein. And they differ when there is claimed against him theft of a garment in his hand he claims for himself, denying he stole it. So objectors say: There is no dispute therein between him and whoever claims that against him until the claimant against him is the owner of the garment, or one standing in his place therein; and of those who say that are Abu Hanifah and his companions and al-Shafii. And a party of them say: Whoever of people disputes him therein is a disputant to him therein; of them Ibn Abi Layla and Malik. And the saying in our view therein is the first saying, for it is not permissible to judge theft for an absent one; and for that when it is not judged thereby for him, it was in ruling for him in whose hand it is; so it is voided to cut therein for that. And when its owner disputes him therein, or one standing his place therein, and establishes proof against him of his ownership of it and his stealing it from him, it is judged for him thereby, and judged with cutting upon its thief from him; and the imam is sufficed thereafter from him, for proof has been established with him of the obligatoriness of cutting upon its thief, as its establishment with him by his confession of stealing it. So he needs thereafter no disputing of him to him therein. And his giving it to its thief, and his charity therewith upon him, and his ownership for him from where he owned it, does not lift cutting upon him therein, as Abu Yusuf said therein; and Allah Almighty we ask for guidance."
"2716","Chapter 382 - Explanation of the difficulty in what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), of his taking upon his companions in his pledging them that some of them not slander others. 2390 - Ismail ibn Yahya al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: al-Shafii narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahhab ibn Abd al-Majid al-Thaqafi informed us, from Khalid al-Hadhdha, from Abu Qilabah, from Abu al-Ashath, from Ubadah ibn al-Samit, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took upon us as he took upon women: That you associate nothing with Allah, nor steal, nor fornicate, nor kill your children, nor shall some of you slander others, nor disobey me in a good I command you; so whoever of you commits one of them and his punishment is hastened, it is his expiation; and whoever has his punishment delayed, his affair is to Allah Almighty: if He wills He punishes him, and if He wills He forgives him. So we examined the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this hadith: nor shall some of you slander others, to ascertain what is intended thereby, Allah willing. We found al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: al-Shafii, may Allah have mercy on him, said: Whoever lies against his brother has slandered him. And we found Abu Qurrah Muhammad ibn Humayd narrated to us, he said: I heard Said ibn Kathir ibn Ufayr saying: al-adahah is the sorceress. He said: And he recited to us therein: al-adaha al-adihah. So in what we mentioned from al-Muzani, from al-Shafii, was that what is intended thereby is lying; and in what we mentioned from Abu Qurrah, from Ibn Ufayr, was that what is intended thereby is sorcery. Then we found therein what is higher than these two sayings."
"2717","2391 - And it is what Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Umar al-Zahrani and Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi narrated to us - and the wording is Bishr's - they said: Shubah narrated to us, he said: Abu Ishaq, meaning al-Sabii, narrated to us, from Abu al-Ahwas, he said: Abdillah, meaning Ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Shall I not inform you what al-adhu is? He said: It is talebearing, gossip between people."
"2718","2392 - And we found Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Ubaydillah al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Amr narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Abi Unaysah, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abdillah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Shall I not inform you of al-adhu? Al-adhu is talebearing that parts between people."
"2719","And we found Yazid narrated to us, he said: Habban ibn Hilal narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muslim al-Qasmali narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim al-Hajari narrated to us, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abdillah, he said: We used to say in Jahiliyyah: al-idah is sorcery, and al-idah among you today is gossip. It was said: And he said: Enough of a man in lying that he narrate all he hears."
"2720","2393 - And we found Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala, he said: Abdillah ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Abdillah ibn Luhayah and Amr ibn al-Harith informed me, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Sinan ibn Sad, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do you know what al-adhu is? They said: Allah Almighty and His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) know best. He said: It is the نقل of speech from some people to some, to corrupt between them. And we found Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz licensed to us what was mentioned to us that he heard from Abu Ubayd, regarding the hadith of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when he said: Shall I not inform you what al-adhu is? They said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah. He said: It is talebearing. Abu Ubayd said: And so it is with us. The poet said: al-adahah. It is said: al-idah and al-adhu and al-adah of al-adihah. So we ascertained thereby that the return of what is intended in the hadith of Ubadah is to what we mentioned in these narrations. As for the people of Arabic other than whom we mentioned of them in these narrations, of them al-Khalil ibn Ahmad, they used to say: I slandered so-and-so, adhan, and al-idah is forgery and calumny and false speech. He said: And it is said: He cast him with al-adihah, i.e. with forgery; and al-idah is the thorn tree. So what is in these hadiths we narrated in this chapter, upon this doctrine, meaning of the hadith of Abdillah and of the hadith of Anas, is only al-adhu, not al-idah; and al-idah is cutting. And Allah knows best the reality of the matter therein; and Allah we ask for guidance."
"2721","Chapter 383 - Explanation of the difficulty in what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding what is obligatory for one who dies owing fasts: is it fasting or feeding on his behalf? 2394 - Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, he said: Sulayman, meaning al-Amash, narrated to us, from Muslim al-Batin, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that a woman rode the sea and vowed to fast a month, then died before fasting; so her sister came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and asked him, so he commanded her to fast on her behalf."
"2722","2395 - And Imran ibn Musa al-Tai narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Jafar ibn Abi Wahshiyyah, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that a woman rode the sea and vowed, if Allah Almighty saves her from it, to fast a month; then she died before fasting, so her maternal aunt or some relative asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he commanded that fasting be done on her behalf."
"2723","2396 - And Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Abu Bishr, he said: Said ibn Jubayr narrated to us, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that a woman rode the sea and vowed, if Allah Almighty saves her, to fast a month; so Allah Almighty saved her, then she died before fasting, so a relative came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded her to fast on her behalf."
"2724","2397 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Asbagh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Wahb narrated to me, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith narrated to me, from Ubaydullah ibn Abi Jafar, from Muhammad ibn Jafar ibn al-Zubayr, from Urwah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever dies owing fasts, his guardian fasts on his behalf."
"2725","2398 - al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Ibn Luhayah narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Abi Jafar narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Jafar, from Urwah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"2726","2399 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub informed us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Abi Jafar narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Jafar, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. Then an objector said: This is a sunnah narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from these acceptable faces, so whence was it permissible for you to abandon it and say contrary to it? So our answer to him in that, by the grace of Allah and His help, is that our abandoning it was because we know not that it is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except from the two quarters we narrated it from, and it is from the quarter of Ibn Abbas and Aishah, may Allah be pleased with them both; then we found Ibn Abbas and Aishah after the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) abandoned that and said its opposite, while they are trusted upon what they narrated, just in what they said. So we understood thereby that they did not abandon what they heard from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) therein except to what is worthier, of what they heard from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) therein."
"2727","And what is narrated from them contrary to that. Of what Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Sawwar ibn Abdillah al-Anbari narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray narrated to us, he said: al-Hajjaj al-Ahwal narrated to us. Abu Jafar said: And he is al-Hajjaj ibn al-Hajjaj al-Bahili; Yazid and Ibrahim ibn Tahman narrated from him, and he is acceptable in narration with its people. He said: Ayyub ibn Musa narrated to us, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: No one prays on behalf of anyone, nor fasts anyone on behalf of anyone; but he feeds on his behalf, in place of every day a mudd of wheat."
"2728","And of what al-Rabi al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith informed me that Bukayr narrated to him that Kurayb, freedman of Ibn Abbas, narrated to him that Ibn Abbas said: The old man ransoms when he cannot fast. So Ibn Abbas made what the old man resorts to at his inability to fast the ransom thereof, not the fasting of another on his behalf."
"2729","And of what al-Hasan ibn Abd al-Ala al-Sanani wrote to me, narrating to me from Abd al-Razzaq ibn Hammam, from Mamar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Thawban, he said: Ibn Abbas was asked about a man who died owing fasting of the month of Ramadan, and a vow of another month, so he said: Sixty poor are fed on his behalf."
"2730","And of what Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: Ubaydah ibn Humayd narrated to us, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Rufay, from Amrah daughter of Abd al-Rahman, she said: I asked Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, saying to her: My mother died owing Ramadan; is it proper that I make it up on her behalf? She said: No, but give charity on her behalf, in place of every day upon a poor one, better than your fasting on her behalf."
"2731","And of what Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, from Umarah ibn Umayr, he said: A freedwoman of Ibn Abi Usayfir died owing fasting of a month, so Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, said: Feed on her behalf."
"2732","And of what Yahya ibn Uthman narrated to us, he said: Nuaym ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Jarir ibn Abd al-Hamid narrated to us, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Rufay, from Amrah, she said: My mother died owing fasting of Ramadan, so I asked Aishah about that, and she said: Make it up on her behalf, then said: Rather give charity, in place of every day upon a poor one half a sa."
"2733","And of what Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Muammal ibn Ismail and Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, they said: al-Thawri narrated to us, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, from Umarah ibn Umayr, from a freedwoman of the family of Banu Usayfir, she said: I asked - meaning Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her - about a woman who died owing fasting of a month, so she said: Feed on her behalf - and the wording is Rawh's. So the saying of Ibn Abbas and Aishah herein was proof that they said what they said in what we narrated from them in these reports, and the ruling with them in what they said therein is what they said therein; and it is not permissible that that be of them except after the establishment of the abrogation of what they heard from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) therein; and were it not that, their justice would fall, and in the fall of their justice would be the fall of their narration; and far be it from Allah Almighty that they be so. But they are upon their justice, and upon that they did not abandon what they heard from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except to what they heard from him of what they said after him. And they in our view therein are like what Muhammad ibn Sirin said."
"2734","Of what Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Jarir ibn Hazim informed me, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, regarding al-mutah - meaning the mutah of Hajj - he said: They, meaning the companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), attended it, and they were forbidden it. So in their doctrine is nothing suspect, nor in their opinion anything deficient; and Allah we ask for guidance."
"2735","Chapter 384 - Explanation of the difficulty in what is narrated from Ibn Abbas and from Salamah ibn al-Akwa, may Allah be pleased with them both, of what we know with certainty they said only by taking it from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), in explanation of the difficulty of the saying of Allah Almighty: And upon those who can bear it, a ransom of feeding a poor one. Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Zakariyya ibn Ishaq narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Dinar narrated to us, from Ata, that he heard Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, saying: And upon those who are burdened with it, a ransom of feeding a poor one. Ibn Abbas said: It is not abrogated; it is the old man and the old woman who cannot fast, so they feed in place of every day a poor one."
"2736","Abu Shurayh Muhammad ibn Zakariyya ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Mansur, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abbas, that he used to recite this verse: And upon those who are burdened with it. He said: It is the old one; half a sa is fed on his behalf every day."
"2737","Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Mukhawwal ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Israil ibn Yunus informed us, from Salim, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, regarding the saying of Allah Almighty: And upon those who can bear it. He said: Those who undertake it with hardship and cannot but with effort: the pregnant, the old, the sick, and the one with chronic sneezing."
"2738","Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Muadh ibn Hisham narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Azrah, from Said ibn Jubayr, that Ibn Abbas had a slave girl nursing, so she strove, so he said to her: Break fast, for you are in the position of those who can bear it. So what we narrated from Ibn Abbas in this chapter indicated that it is differed from him in yutawwaqunahu and yutiqunahu; and that Ata and Mujahid narrated from him yutawwaqunahu, and that Said ibn Jubayr narrated from him yutiqunahu; and in all what we narrated from him of that is the return of the substitute from fasting to feeding, not to fasting."
"2739","Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhammad ibn al-Mughirah narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Bakr ibn Mudar narrated to us, from Amr ibn al-Harith, from Bukayr ibn Abdillah ibn al-Ashajj, from Yazid, freedman of Salamah ibn al-Akwa, from Salamah ibn al-Akwa, that he said: When this verse was revealed: And upon those who can bear it, a ransom of feeding a poor one, whoever wished to break fast and ransom did, until the one after it was revealed and abrogated it. Abu Jafar said: meaning the saying of Allah Almighty: So whoever of you witnesses the month, let him fast it; and whoever is ill or on a journey, then a number of other days. Abu Jafar said: So Allah Almighty returned the substitute for fasting to ransom by feeding, when the ruling was upon what is in the first verse, not to other than it of fasting on behalf of one upon whom it was obligatory; then Allah Almighty abrogated that by what is in the second verse, and there remained what is in the first verse of what one unable to fast does, and it is ransom by feeding, nothing else on his behalf. And it is possible that in the reports we narrated in the chapter before this chapter, of fasting on behalf of the dead, it was before the revelation of this verse mentioned in the two hadiths of Ibn Abbas and Salamah we mentioned; then the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) employed feeding therein, not fasting in its place; of them Anas ibn Malik and Qays ibn al-Saib."
"2740","As Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Muadh ibn Hisham narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, that he was too weak for fasting a year before his death, so he broke fast and fed on behalf of every day a poor one."
"2741","2400 - And as Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Surayj ibn al-Numan al-Jawhari narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Muslim al-Taifi narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, from Qays ibn al-Saib, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was a partner to me, and the best partner, neither disputing nor dissembling; and Qays had grown old, so he used to feed on behalf of man in the month of Ramadan when he grew old, two mudds every day; so feed on my behalf a sa. He said: And in what we mentioned of this is what indicated the employment of feeding for fasting, not the fasting of other than one upon whom it was obligatory on behalf of one upon whom it was obligatory; and Allah we ask for guidance."
"2742","Chapter 385 - Explanation of the difficulty in what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), in what he answered one who asked him about the inheritance of a man of al-Azd in his hand, when he mentioned to him that he found no Azdi. 2401 - Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhammad al-Muharibi narrated to us, from Jibril ibn Ahmar, from Abdillah ibn Buraydah, from his father, that a man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, with me is the inheritance of a man of al-Azd, and I find no Azdi to pay it to. He said: Go seek an Azdi a year, or he said: a hawl. So he went, then returned in the second year and said: O Messenger of Allah, by Allah I found no Azdi to pay it to. He said: Go look for the first Khuzaai and pay it to him."
"2743","2402 - Yahya ibn Uthman narrated to us, he said: Nuaym ibn Hammad narrated to us -H-. He said: And Muhammad ibn Sinan al-Shayzari narrated to us, he said: Isa ibn Sulayman al-Shayzari narrated to us, he said: Abbad ibn al-Awwam narrated to us - Yahya said: from Jibril ibn Ahmar Abu Bakr, and Muhammad said: from Jibril ibn Ahmar - then both agreed and said: from Abdillah ibn Buraydah, from his father, then he mentioned the like of it, except that he said: Go pay it to the first Khuzaai you meet; then when he turned he said: Bring him to me. He said: So he returned. He said: Go pay it to the eldest of Khuzah. Abu Jafar said: And the meaning of eldest of Khuzah in our view - and Allah knows best - is the eldest in lineage; and of it: al-wala for al-kubr."
"2744","2403 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan Malik ibn Ismail narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Muhammad al-Ansari narrated to us, he said: Jibril ibn Ahmar informed us, from Abdillah ibn Buraydah, from his father, he said: A man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: With me is the inheritance of a man of al-Azd; I find no Azdi to pay it to. He said: Wait with it a hawl. He said: So he did, then came to him and he said: Go pay it to the eldest of Khuzah. Abu Jafar said: We examined this hadith and found what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded therein the one who asked him, of seeking an Azdi a hawl, he commanded therein like what he commanded regarding the found object, and seeking its owner a hawl, then it is spent in what it must be spent in after the hawl. So He made like that what he commanded the asker in the hadith we narrated, of seeking an Azdi a hawl, and of returning that inheritance, if he does not find him until the hawl passes, to the eldest of Khuzah, for they are of al-Azd; they only split off from them when they left Yemen and came to Makkah; and they are Banu Mazin ibn al-Aswad ibn al-Ghawth ibn Nabt ibn Malik ibn Zayd ibn Kahlan ibn Saba ibn Yashjub ibn Yarub ibn Qahtan. So they allied in Makkah whom they allied there, so became thereby allies of Banu Hashim. Then an objector said: How is it permissible that what is in this hadith be as mentioned therein, of the failure of the one with whom that inheritance was to find an Azdi deserving it until he seeks it of Khuzah, while the Ansar are with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and they are of al-Azd, and they are nearer to that deceased than Khuzah? For Khuzah, when it split off, was named thereby, and it is of a particular belly of al-Azd; and others of al-Azd besides it are not of that belly. So it was attributed to what it was attributed to and thereby distinguished from al-Azd, while the rest of the bellies of al-Azd remained upon what they were upon before that, of attribution to al-Azd; as the thighs of Quraysh distinguished from Quraysh by what they are of the thighs of Quraysh, so it is said: the Hashimis for the Hashimis, and the Abshamis for Abd Shams, until it is said in the bellies of Quraysh likewise, while Quraysh gathers them all. So our answer to him in that, by the grace of Allah and His help, is that this may have been in Makkah before the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) emigrated from it to Madinah, and before the Islam of the Ansar; and of what brings near that that is so in hearts is that the one who narrated this hadith from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is Buraydah ibn al-Husayb, and he is a man of Aslam, and Aslam is of Khuzah; and the Islam of Aslam and the Islam of Khuzah was while the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was in Makkah. So what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded the one who asked him, and the answer of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to him with what he answered him therein, while there were then no Ansar, nor anyone then nearer to al-Azd than them to that deceased except Khuzah; and in that is what indicated that that was of one who had embraced Islam, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) returned his inheritance to the nearest of the Muslims of Khuzah. And Sharik ibn Abdillah al-Nakhai narrated from Jibril ibn Ahmar, opposing therein Musa ibn Muhammad al-Ansari and Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhammad al-Muharibi and Abbad ibn al-Awwam."
"2745","2404 - As Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala and Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, they said: Amr ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Sharik ibn Abdillah informed us, he said: Jibril ibn Ahmar narrated to us, from Ibn Buraydah, from his father, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was brought the inheritance of a man of Khuzah, so he said: Seek for him an heir; so they sought and did not find; so he said: Seek for him kin; so they sought and did not find; so he said: Seek for him a womb-relative; so they sought and did not find; so he said: Pay his wealth to the eldest of Khuzah. Abu Jafar said: With Yunus, of Amr ibn Khalid, were only two hadiths: this hadith and another."
"2746","2405 - And as Fahd narrated to us, Fahd narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Said ibn al-Asbahani narrated to us, he said: Sharik informed us, he said: Jibril ibn Ahmar narrated to us, from Ibn Buraydah, from his father, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was brought the inheritance of a man of Khuzah, then he mentioned the like of it. Abu Jafar said: What others than Sharik narrated of this hadith thereof is worthier in our view than what Sharik narrated thereof, by their number, and because three are worthier of retention than one; and because of the impossibility of some of what is in the hadith of Sharik, of what is mentioned therein of the saying of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Seek for him a womb-relative - and this is not permissible among Arabs, for Arabs do not give inheritance by wombs, but give inheritance by asabah, except where Allah Almighty gave inheritance to those of stated shares of them, and sisters by father and mother or by father with daughters; for when their asabah of their thighs are not found, they are found of the thighs next to their thighs, as is done regarding them in the blood-moneys of their offenses: their thighs bear the compensations of offenses; if their number falls short of bearing them, that is returned to those next to them of thighs. But inheritance by wombs contrary to what we mentioned is among other than Arabs, of non-Arabs who do not return to shub nor to tribes, but return to lands, to nothing else, as has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of what its narrators carried upon that."
"2747","2406 - As Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: I heard Yazid ibn Harun -H-. And as Ali ibn Shaybah and Abu Umayyah both narrated to us, they said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, then all together said: al-Jurayri informed us, from Abu al-Ala ibn al-Shikhkhir, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Suhar al-Abdi, from his father, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: The Hour shall not stand until tribes are swallowed, until it is said: Who remains of Banu so-and-so? So I knew he means Arabs, for non-Arabs are only attributed to their villages."
"2748","And there has been narrated regarding the saying of Allah Almighty: And We made you shub and tribes that you know one another, what Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Qays ibn al-Rabi narrated to us, from Abu al-Husayn, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both: Allah Almighty says: shub and tribes. He said: al-shub are the agglomerations, and al-qabail are the thighs by which they know one another."
"2749","And of what Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Israil narrated to us, he said: Abu Yahya narrated to us, from Mujahid, regarding His Almighty saying: shub and tribes. He said: al-shub is the distant lineage, and al-qabail is below that."
"2750","And of what Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Hudhayfah narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Abu Husayn, from Said ibn Jubayr, regarding His Almighty saying: And We made you shub and tribes that you know one another. He said: al-shub is like Tamim and Bakr, and al-qabail are the thighs."
"2751","And of what Wallad al-Nahwi narrated to us, he said: al-Masadiri narrated to us, from Abu Ubaydah Mamar ibn al-Muthanna: shub and tribes; it is said: Of which shib are you? So he says: Of Mudar, of Rabiah; and al-qabail below that. Ibn Ahmar said: Abu Jafar said: And Arabs return to al-shub and to tribes and to thighs, and by them they inherit; and non-Arabs do not return to that, but their lands gather them, nothing else. And so Abu Yusuf used to say regarding inheritance by wombs that are not asabah: it is only among non-Arabs, not among Arabs. So thereby is impossible what is in the hadith of Sharik, of what he attributed to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), of seeking a womb-relative to pay to him the inheritance of the Azdi whom Sharik therein attributed to Khuzah; and Allah we ask for guidance."
"2752","Chapter 386 - Explanation of the difficulty in what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), of his saying: Let none of you prevent his neighbor from planting a timber in his wall. 2407 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Said ibn al-Asbahani narrated to us, he said: Husayn ibn Ali al-Jufi narrated to us, from Zaidah ibn Qudamah, from Simak ibn Harb, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Let none of you prevent his neighbor from placing a timber upon his wall."
"2753","2408 - al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Qays ibn al-Rabi narrated to us, from Simak, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever builds, let him support his trunks upon the wall of his neighbor."
"2754","2409 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim al-Nabil narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Hisham ibn Yahya, that Ikrimah ibn Salamah ibn Abi Rabiah informed him that two brothers of Banu al-Mughirah, one of them freed if the other places a timber in his wall; so they met Mujammi ibn Yazid and people of the Ansar of the companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: We bear witness that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Let none of you prevent his brother from placing a timber in his wall. So he said to his brother: You know you are judged for against me, so place the pillars behind the wall, and place your timber in them. Amr ibn Dinar said: I saw those pillars."
"2755","2410 - Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Makki ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, and he mentioned with his isnad the like of it."
"2756","2411 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik told him, from Ibn Shihab, from al-Araj, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: None of you should prevent his neighbour from planting a beam in his wall."
"2757","2412 - Yunus narrated it to us another time, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Malik and Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, and he mentioned the same with its chain, and he added: Then Abu Hurayrah says: What is wrong with me that I see you turning away from it; by Allah, I will certainly hurl it between your shoulders."
"2758","2413 - Abu Umayyah narrated it to us, he said: Khalid ibn Makhlad al-Qatawani narrated to us, he said: Malik narrated to me, from Abu al-Zinad, from al-Araj, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same, except that he said: that he fixes a beam in his wall, in place of what Yunus said: a beam in his wall."
"2759","2414 - Al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: Al-Shafii narrated to us, he said: Malik narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab, from al-Araj, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same, and he said: a beam in his wall, as Abu Umayyah said."
"2760","2415 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, from his father, he said: I heard al-Zubayr ibn al-Khirrit narrating from Ikrimah, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A man has no right to prevent his neighbour from placing a beam in his wall."
"2761","2416 - Abd al-Aziz ibn Muawiyah al-Attabi narrated to us, he said: Muslim ibn Ibrahim al-Azdi narrated to us, he said: Hisham al-Dastuwai narrated to us, he said: Mamar narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: None of you should prevent his neighbour from placing a beam in his wall."
"2762","2417 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Al-Muqaddami Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abi Hafsah narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you asks his neighbour to place a beam in his wall, let him not prevent him. Abu Jafar said: So this hadith was about the neighbour asking his neighbour to place a beam upon his wall, and more than one narrator agreed with Muhammad ibn Abi Hafsah in that from al-Zuhri, among them Uqayl ibn Khalid."
"2763","2418 - As Muhammad ibn Uzayz narrated to us, he said: Salamah ibn Rawh narrated to us, from Uqayl, from Ibn Shihab, that Said ibn al-Musayyib informed him that Abu Hurayrah informed him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever is asked by his neighbour to place a beam in his wall, let him not prevent him. Abu Hurayrah said: What is wrong with me that I see you turning away from it; by Allah, I will certainly hurl it between your shoulders. And among them is Sufyan ibn Uyaynah."
"2764","2419 - Al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: Al-Shafii narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab, from Abd al-Rahman al-Araj, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, I heard him saying: then he mentioned the same. And among them is Sulayman ibn Kathir."
"2765","2420 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Sulayman Sadawayh narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Kathir narrated to us, he said: I heard Ibn Shihab narrating from Abd al-Rahman al-Araj, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same, except that he did not say: by Allah, I will hurl it between your shoulders. He said: So these hadiths were about the request from a neighbour to his neighbour, and in them is what indicates that the neighbour has no right to place a beam upon his neighbours wall except after asking him for that and waiting for what comes from him in response to that, and in that is what indicates that this request is when the neighbour needs it from his neighbour, and that the permission for that may be understood as a matter of choice, not obligation, like His saying, mighty and majestic: And those who seek a contract for emancipation from among those whom your right hands possess, then make a contract with them if you know good in them. And all the people of knowledge agree without differing that this is by way of recommendation and encouragement to good, not obligation nor compulsion. Likewise in our view, and Allah knows best, is the saying of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): When one of you is asked permission by his neighbour to fix a beam in his wall, let him not prevent him. It is also by way of encouragement and recommendation, not compulsion. And likewise his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): When one of you is asked permission by his wife to go to the mosque. That is not by way of obligation according to all the people of knowledge, but it is by way of encouragement and recommendation, and according to what the husbands see therein of benefit and attainment of good, without there entering upon them through it from their wives what is not proper. And it has been reported from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, also contrary to what we have reported from him."
"2766","2421 - As al-Rabi al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Ikrimah, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade a man to prevent his neighbour from placing a beam upon his wall."
"2767","2422 - And as al-Rabi narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Qays ibn al-Rabi narrated to us, from Mansur ibn Dinar, from Abu Ikrimah al-Makhzumi, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It is not lawful for a Muslim man to prevent his neighbour from placing his beams upon his wall. Then Abu Hurayrah says: I will certainly strike with it between your eyes even if you dislike it. He said: And what is in these two hadiths is not contrary in our view to what we reported before it in this chapter, and Allah knows best. As for what is in the first of the two, it is about preventing what causes no harm. And as for the second of the two, it is like what has been reported from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Charity is not lawful for one who is strong and able to earn. By that he did not mean that it becomes unlawful for him like a forbidden thing when he needs it, as it is unlawful for those who are free from need of it, but rather it is not lawful for him in all its aspects, as what suffices him is lawful for one unable to earn by his strength. Likewise is his saying: It is not lawful for a Muslim man to prevent his neighbour. It is also upon that, because he may be able to permit him that, then it returns after that to there being no harm upon him in what he permitted him, just as there would be no harm upon him in it had he not permitted him it. And likewise is what has been reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"2768","2423 - From what Ibn Abi Imran and Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, they said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Salih al-Azdi narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Muhayyah Yahya ibn Yala al-Aslami narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Anas, he said: A youth from among us was martyred on the day of Uhud, and his mother began wiping the dust from his face and saying: Glad tidings, congratulations with Paradise. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: And what makes you know? Perhaps he used to speak about what did not concern him and withhold what would not harm him. And from Allah, mighty and majestic, we ask guidance."
"2769","Chapter 387 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported about the reason by which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) abolished what the polytheists were upon of their forbidding umrah in the time in which they used to forbid it of the year. 2424 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Mualla ibn Asad al-Ammi narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb ibn Khalid narrated to us, from Abd Allah ibn Tawus, from his father, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: They used to consider umrah in the months of hajj among the most wicked of wickedness, and they used to call Muharram Safar, and they used to say: When the camels back is healed, and the trace is effaced, and Safar enters, umrah becomes lawful for whoever performs umrah. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) arrived in Makkah on the morning of the fourth of Dhu al-Hijjah while they were chanting the talbiyah for hajj, so he commanded them to make it an umrah."
"2770","2425 - Jafar ibn Muhammad ibn al-Hasan al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn al-Hajjaj al-Sami narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb narrated to us, then he mentioned the same with its chain. So in this hadith is that what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did, of commanding the people to abandon the hajj for which they had entered ihram and to enter ihram in its place for umrah, was to nullify what the Arabs were upon of forbidding umrah in the months of hajj. And this hadith has been reported through another channel besides this channel, with an addition to what is in this hadith about the time in which they used to forbid umrah, and the reason by which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) nullified what they were upon of what is mentioned in this hadith became clear."
"2771","2426 - As Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Zakariyya ibn Abi Zaidah narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Abd Allah ibn Tawus, from his father, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: By Allah, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not make Aishah perform umrah in Dhu al-Hijjah except to cut off thereby the practice of Jahiliyyah, for this clan of Quraysh and those who followed their religion used to say: When the camels fur grows back, and the back is healed, and Safar enters, then umrah has become lawful for whoever performs umrah. So they used to forbid it until Dhu al-Hijjah and Muharram had passed, as the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made her perform umrah only to cut off that practice of theirs."
"2772","2427 - As Jafar ibn Muhammad al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Al-Hasan ibn Sahl al-Hannat, who was nicknamed Kharbush, narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abi Zaidah narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj and Ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Abd Allah ibn Tawus, from his father, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, then he mentioned the same, except that he said at its end: except to nullify that saying of theirs. So Yusuf ibn Adi and al-Hasan ibn Sahl differed in the chain of this hadith: Yusuf said therein: from Ibn Jurayj, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, while al-Hasan said therein: from Ibn Jurayj and Ibn Ishaq. And in this hadith is that they used to forbid umrah in Muharram, which is not one of the months of hajj, just as they used to forbid it in the months of hajj before it. That in our view, and Allah knows best, is an error of Muhammad ibn Ishaq, because what is well known among the people about the Arabs forbidding umrah is that it was only in the months of hajj, not in anything besides them. Likewise it is expressly stated in the hadith of Wuhayb which we have also reported therein, that they used to call Muharram Safar. So in that is what indicates that by their saying: and Safar enters, they meant the entering of Muharram which they used to call Safar, not meaning thereby the Safar that follows Muharram. And this hadith has been reported from Abd al-Razzaq from Mamar and Ibn Jurayj."
"2773","2428 - Jafar al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Azim al-Anbari narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, he said: Mamar and Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, and he did not mention Ibn Abbas therein. He said: They arrived with hajj purely, unmixed with anything, meaning the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and they used to consider umrah in the months of hajj the most wicked of wickedness, and what pleased them of the affair of Islam was what had been in Jahiliyyah, and they used to say: When the back is healed, and the fur grows back, and Safar ends, umrah becomes lawful for whoever performs umrah. So in this hadith is that they intended by forbidding umrah only the months of hajj specifically, and in that is the agreement of Mamar and Ibn Jurayj with what Wuhayb reported in that, and their disagreement with Ibn Ishaq in what he reported therein, except that therein is: and Safar ends, and that in our view, and Allah knows best, is an error; rather it is: and Safar enters, by that meaning the entering of Muharram which they used to call Safar, and Allah knows best. And in the hadith of Muhammad ibn Ishaq is that what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intended in nullifying what they were upon in Jahiliyyah of what we mentioned was his making Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, perform umrah in Dhu al-Hijjah. And this in our view is impossible, because the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded the people before that to dissolve their ihram for hajj and to enter ihram in its place for umrah, and among them was Aishah."
"2774","2429 - As Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Abd Allah ibn Abi Salamah narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, from his father, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: We went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioning nothing but hajj. When we came to Sarif I menstruated, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came in to me while I was weeping, and he said: What makes you weep? I said: I wish I had not performed hajj this year, or had not gone out this year. He said: Perhaps you have menstruated? I said: Yes. He said: This is a matter Allah has decreed for the daughters of Adam, so do what the pilgrim does except that you do not circumambulate the House. She said: When we came to Makkah, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to his Companions: Make it an umrah. So the people dissolved their ihram except those who had the sacrificial animal with them, and the sacrificial animal was with him and with Abu Bakr and Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, and those of means. Then they entered ihram for hajj. When it was the day of sacrifice I became pure, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent me and I performed the pouring forth. Then beef was brought, and I said: What is this? They said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sacrificed cattle on behalf of his wives. Then when it was the night of al-Hasbah I said: O Messenger of Allah, the people return with a hajj and an umrah, and I return with a hajj? So he commanded Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakr, and he mounted me behind him, and I remember that I would doze and the back of the saddle would strike my face, until we came to al-Tanim, and I entered ihram for umrah in place of the peoples umrah which they had performed. Abu Jafar said: So in this hadith from Aishah is that they went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), she being among them, mentioning nothing but hajj, and that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded the people to make it an umrah except whoever had the sacrificial animal with him."
"2775","2430 - As al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: We went out seeing it as nothing but hajj. When he came to Makkah he circumambulated and did not dissolve his ihram, and he had the sacrificial animal with him. Those with him of his wives and his Companions circumambulated, and those of them who did not have the sacrificial animal with them dissolved their ihram. He said: And she menstruated. She said: So we completed our rites of our hajj. When it was the night of al-Hasbah, the night of departure, I said: O Messenger of Allah, do your Companions return with a hajj and an umrah while I return with a hajj? He said: Had you not circumambulated the House on the nights we arrived? She said: I said: No. He said: Go with your brother to al-Tanim and enter ihram for umrah, then your appointed place is such and such a place. So in this hadith is what indicates that she had left her umrah which had taken the place of her hajj by abandoning tawaf for it, until she became occupied with what she became occupied with of the affair of her hajj. And Urwah ibn al-Zubayr reported this hadith from Aishah and clarified therein a meaning other than this meaning, which was the reason for her leaving umrah."
"2776","2431 - As Abu Bakrah Bakkar ibn Qutaybah and Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, they said: Uthman ibn al-Haytham ibn al-Jahm narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj informed me, he said: Hisham ibn Urwah narrated to me, from Urwah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that she said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded us to enter ihram for hajj, and whoever wished could enter ihram for umrah. She said: So I was among those who entered ihram for umrah, then I menstruated, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came in to me and commanded me to undo my hair and comb it and abandon my umrah. And Urwah was agreed with in what he reported of that from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, by Ibn Abi Mulaykah and Ikrimah the freedman of Ibn Abbas, for they both reported from her the same."
"2777","2432 - As Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Zaidah narrated to us, from Nafi ibn Umar al-Jumahi, from Ibn Abi Mulaykah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, then he mentioned the same."
"2778","2433 - And as Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Zaidah narrated to us, from Israil, from Zayd ibn al-Hasan, from Ikrimah, from Aishah, then he mentioned the same. So in these hadiths is that she left her umrah only by the permission of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), by undoing her hair and combing it and abandoning it. And these hadiths are more worthy than the hadith of al-Qasim, because therein has been clarified what was not clarified in the hadith of al-Qasim. And in that is what indicates that the Prophets (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) nullification of what the polytheists were upon of what we mentioned was only by his dissolving hajj and their entering ihram for umrah, not by Aishahs umrah for which she entered ihram on the night of al-Hasbah, because that umrah was only a making up of an umrah she had been in like the rest of the people were in their umrahs into which they were, and they had left hajj for them, and Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, left that umrah which is like their umrahs due to the menstruation that came upon her before her tawaf for her umrah. So it was not proper for her with that to continue in it after her entering ihram for the hajj for which she entered ihram as the rest of the people entered ihram for the like of it, because had she done that she would have been standing at Arafah for her hajj while its release after that from her hajj would be with an umrah for which she had not circumambulated. And what Suraqah ibn Malik ibn Jushum addressed to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about that umrah for which the people entered ihram by the command of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to them in place of the hajj for which they had entered ihram and which they dissolved for it, indicates what we mentioned of that."
"2779","2434 - As al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Hatim narrated to us, he said: Jafar ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from his father, from Jabir, in his hadith about hajj, he said: So he entered ihram, meaning the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with tawhid, and the people entered ihram with this with which they enter ihram, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not reject anything of them. Jabir said: We saw it as nothing but hajj; we did not know umrah, until when we were at the last tawaf upon al-Marwah he said: Had I faced of my affair what I have now turned my back to, I would not have driven the sacrificial animal and would have made it an umrah. So whoever does not have a sacrificial animal with him, let him dissolve his ihram and make it an umrah. So the people dissolved their ihram and shortened except the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and whoever had the sacrificial animal with him. Then Suraqah ibn Malik ibn Jushum stood and said: O Messenger of Allah, is this umrah of ours for this year of ours or forever? He said: So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) interlaced his fingers in the latter and said: Umrah has entered like this into hajj."
"2780","2435 - And as Muhammad ibn Humayd ibn Hisham al-Ruayni narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Ayan narrated to us, from Khusayf, from Ata, from Jabir, he said: When we arrived with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in Makkah in the Farewell Pilgrimage, he asked the people: With what did you enter ihram? Some people said: We chanted the talbiyah for hajj. Others said: We arrived as mutamattis. Others said: We chanted the talbiyah with your talbiyah, O Messenger of Allah. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to them: Whoever arrived and did not drive a sacrificial animal, let him dissolve his ihram, for had I faced of my affair what I have now turned my back to, I would not have driven the sacrificial animal until I was in a state of release. Then Suraqah ibn Malik ibn Jushum said: O Messenger of Allah, is this umrah of ours for our year or forever? He said: No, rather forever and ever. And this phrase which is in this hadith from the saying of Jabir: and others said: we arrived as mutamattis, is far-fetched in the hearts, because the mutamattis only begin their ihram with umrah then follow it with hajj, yet they did not know umrah in the months of hajj at that time, so how could they perform the tamattu which is only with umrah? And this in our view is an error of Khusayf. As for others besides him among the companions of Ata, they reported it from Ata, from Jabir, contrary to that, among them Qays ibn Sad."
"2781","2436 - As Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Qays ibn Sad, from Ata, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) arrived for four nights gone of Dhu al-Hijjah. When they circumambulated the House and between al-Safa and al-Marwah, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Make it an umrah. When it was the day of al-Tarwiyah they chanted the talbiyah. When it was the day of sacrifice they arrived and circumambulated the House, and did not go between al-Safa and al-Marwah. So how is it permissible that he commanded them all to dissolve into umrah while some of them were in umrah? And likewise others besides Jabir reported this hadith, that they arrived in Makkah chanting the talbiyah for hajj specifically, among them Ibn Umar."
"2782","2437 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, he said: Humayd narrated to us, from Bakr ibn Abd Allah, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his Companions arrived in Makkah chanting the talbiyah for hajj. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever wishes may make it an umrah, except whoever has the sacrificial animal with him. And among them is Abu Said al-Khudri."
"2783","2438 - As Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray narrated to us, he said: Dawud narrated to us, from Abu Nadrah, from Abu Said, he said: We went out from Madinah crying out with hajj loudly. When we arrived we circumambulated, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Make it an umrah, except whoever has the sacrificial animal with him. And among them is Asma daughter of Abu Bakr."
"2784","2439 - Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Al-Khasib ibn Nasih narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb narrated to us, from Mansur ibn Abd al-Rahman, from his mother, from Asma daughter of Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with them both, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his Companions arrived chanting the talbiyah for hajj, and al-Zubayr had the sacrificial animal with him. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to his Companions: Whoever does not have the sacrificial animal with him, let him dissolve his ihram. And in what we mentioned of this is proof of what we described, except that there has also been reported from Anas ibn Malik in that what enters into the meaning we rejected from the hadith of Khusayf."
"2785","2440 - As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Habban ibn Hilal narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb narrated to us, he said: Ayyub narrated to us, from Abu Qilabah, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed Zuhr in Madinah four rakahs, and prayed Asr at Dhu al-Hulayfah two rakahs, and spent the night there until morning. When he prayed Fajr he mounted his riding animal, and when it rose with him he glorified and magnified Allah, until when it stood straight with him upon al-Bayda he combined both of them. When we arrived in Makkah the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded them to dissolve their ihram. When it was the day of al-Tarwiyah they entered ihram for hajj. Abu Jafar said: So that too is far-fetched in the hearts, that they combined hajj and umrah while they did not know umrah in the months of hajj and counted it among the most wicked of wickedness. And how is it permissible that they be commanded to dissolve from the ihram they were in, in which was an umrah, into an umrah? And Ibn Umar had rejected this against Anas ibn Malik, and informed that their ihram was only for hajj with no umrah with it."
"2786","2441 - As Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Abd Allah ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Muawiyah narrated to us, he said: Humayd narrated to us, he said: And Bakr ibn Abd Allah narrated to me, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he chanted the talbiyah for umrah and hajj. He said: Here I am for umrah and hajj. So Ibn Abd Allah al-Muzani mentioned the saying of Anas to Ibn Umar, and he said: Anas erred; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only chanted the talbiyah for hajj, and we chanted it with him. When we arrived in Makkah he said: Whoever does not have a sacrificial animal with him, let him dissolve his ihram. Bakr said: So I returned to Anas and informed him of the saying of Ibn Umar, and he kept mentioning that until he died."
"2787","2442 - And as Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: I heard Yazid ibn Harun, he said: Humayd informed us, and he mentioned the same with its chain. And he added: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) arrived he said: Whoever does not have a sacrificial animal with him, let him dissolve his ihram. And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had a sacrificial animal with him, so he did not dissolve his ihram. Abu Jafar said: And in what we reported of these reports is what indicates that what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) nullified of what they were upon in Jahiliyyah of forbidding umrah in the months of hajj was only by his dissolving hajj and commanding his Companions to do so, and their entering ihram for umrah, not by his commanding Aishah to perform umrah after hajj in Dhu al-Hijjah. And from Allah we ask guidance. And we have mentioned in this chapter the hadith of Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to the people: Whoever wishes may enter ihram for hajj, and whoever wishes may enter ihram for umrah. So that in our view, and Allah knows best, was upon a saying from him to them after they had dissolved the hajj for which they had entered ihram and arrived in Makkah upon it. So he said to them: Whoever wishes may enter ihram for umrah so as to be thereby a mutamatti, and whoever wishes may enter ihram for hajj with no umrah with it, because the proof had been established by their dissolving from hajj before that, so it was understood from them that that was only for a reason intended thereby, the permitting of umrah for them at that time, because it had been forbidden to them, and because it is not proper to bring umrah into hajj, while it is proper to bring hajj into umrah. So he commanded them to leave hajj by that, so that entering ihram for umrah would be broadened for them for whoever wished to enter ihram for it, and beginning a hajj anew for whoever wished to enter ihram for it with no umrah with it, so he would return with a hajj with no umrah with it."
"2788","Chapter 388 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: None is flogged more than ten lashes except in a hadd of the hadds of Allah, mighty and majestic, and on the obligation of confining to that, and in what was reported from him of what necessitates contrary to that, and which of the two is more worthy. 2443 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, he said: Al-Layth narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, he said: Bukayr ibn al-Ashajj narrated to me; and al-Rabi al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Shuayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, he said: Al-Layth narrated to us, from Yazid, from Bukayr ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Ashajj, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Jabir ibn Abd Allah, from Abu Burdah ibn Niyar, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say: None is flogged more than ten lashes except in a hadd of the hadds of Allah. Abu Jafar said: So al-Layth did not mention in this hadith from Yazid, between Abd al-Rahman ibn Jabir and Abu Burdah, anyone, while others besides him mentioned between them his father Jabir."
"2789","2444 - As Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Wahb ibn Abi Karimah informed me, he said: Muhammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Abu Abd al-Rahim, he said: Zayd ibn Abi Unaysah narrated to me, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Bakr ibn Abd Allah, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, he said: While I was with Sulayman, Abd al-Rahman ibn Jabir came to him and narrated to Sulayman, then Sulayman turned to them and said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Jabir narrated to me that his father narrated to him that he heard Abu Burdah al-Ansari saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: There is no flogging above ten lashes except in a hadd of the hadds of Allah, mighty and majestic. And Zayd was agreed with in what he reported of that, an addition to what al-Layth reported therein, by Usamah ibn Zayd al-Laythi and Amr ibn al-Harith al-Ansari, for they both reported it from Bukayr likewise."
"2790","2445 - As Salih ibn Hakim al-Nasri al-Tammar Abu Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Abu Yala Muhammad ibn al-Salt al-Tawwazi narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Abi Hazim narrated to us, from Usamah ibn Zayd, from Bukayr ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Ashajj, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Jabir, from his father, from Abu Burdah ibn Niyar, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It is not lawful for a man to flog more than ten lashes except in a hadd of the hadds of Allah, mighty and majestic."
"2791","2446 - And as Ahmad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: My uncle Abd Allah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith al-Ansari narrated to me, from Bukayr ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Ashajj, he said: I was with Sulayman ibn Yasar when Abd al-Rahman ibn Jabir came to him and narrated to Sulayman ibn Yasar, then Sulayman ibn Yasar turned to us and said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Jabir narrated to me that his father narrated to him that he heard Abu Burdah ibn Niyar saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: None is flogged more than ten lashes except in a hadd of the hadds of Allah, mighty and majestic. Then an objector said: This hadith has been abandoned by all the people of knowledge, because they did not differ about tazir, that the imam may exceed with it ten lashes; they only differ about what he may not exceed after that therein. One group among them says: He does not exceed with it thirty-nine lashes, and among those who said that are Abu Hanifah and Muhammad ibn al-Hasan and al-Shafii. And one group among them says: He does not exceed seventy-five lashes, and among those who said that of them is Ibn Abi Layla. And one group among them says: He does not exceed seventy-nine lashes, and among those who said that of them is Abu Yusuf once. And one group among them says: He exceeds with it to what he sees fit, even if that exceeds most of the hadds which Allah, mighty and majestic, laid down for His servants, according to the gravity of the offence; and among those who said that of them are Malik ibn Anas and Abu Yusuf once, and he said another time the saying we mentioned from him, and said another time the saying of Abu Hanifah. And in that is what indicates their abandonment of this hadith, so whence was it permissible for them to abandon it? So our answer to him therein, by the guidance of Allah, mighty and majestic, and His aid, is that these whom we mentioned of the jurists whom we named, even if they opposed what is in the hadith and abandoned it, others besides them of the jurists of the lands held by it, namely al-Layth ibn Sad: he held by it once and abandoned it another time, and in the saying in which he held by it therein he said one differentiates within the ten according to the gravity of the offence: if it was grave he makes it severe within the ten, and if it was light he makes it light therein. Then this objector said: Do the others have a proof in their opposing this hadith? So our answer to him therein, by the guidance of Allah and His aid, is that the proof for them in the latitude of their opposing it is what has been reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about his flogging for wine."
"2792","2447 - As Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Yahya al-Qattan narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Abi Arubah narrated to us, from al-Danaj, from Husayn ibn Mundhir al-Raqashi Abu Sasan, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) flogged forty for wine, and Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, forty, and Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, completed it eighty; and each is a sunnah."
"2793","2448 - And Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn al-Mukhtar al-Ansari narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah al-Danaj narrated to us, he said: Husayn ibn al-Mundhir al-Raqashi narrated to us, he said: I witnessed Uthman ibn Affan, may Allah be pleased with him, when al-Walid ibn Uqbah was brought to him, and he had led the people of Kufah in Fajr four rakahs and said: Shall I add for you? He said: So Humran and another man testified against him; one of them testified that he saw him drinking it, and the other testified that he saw him vomiting it. So Uthman said: He did not vomit it until he drank it. Then Uthman said to Ali, may Allah be pleased with them both: Carry out the hadd upon him. So Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, said to his son al-Hasan: Carry out the hadd upon him, and al-Hasan said: Entrust its heat to whoever undertook its coolness. So Ali said to Abd Allah ibn Jafar: Carry out the hadd upon him. So he took the whip and began flogging him while Ali counted until he reached forty, then he said: Stop. Then he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) imposed forty, and Umar flogged eighty; and each is a sunnah. Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, said: And this is more beloved to me. So in this hadith is that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) flogged forty for wine. So it is possible that this was because it was the hadd for wine, and it is possible that this was not because it was a hadd therein, nor because the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intended thereby a known flogging. So we looked into that."
"2794","So we found Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Al-Khasib ibn Nasih narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muslim narrated to us, from Mutarrif, from Umayr ibn Said al-Nakhai, he said: Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Whoever drinks wine and we flog him and he dies, we pay his blood-money, because it is something we devised."
"2795","2449 - And we found Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Said ibn al-Asbahani narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, from Abu Hasin, from Umayr ibn Said, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: I never imposed a hadd upon anyone and he died in it and I found anything in myself except wine, for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not lay down anything therein. So we came to know thereby that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had not flogged the drinker of wine, upon what is in the hadith of Husayn from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, forty with an intent from him toward the forty, but with an intent from him toward a flogging with no fixed number therein."
"2796","And that is also indicated by what has been reported from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, through another channel besides this channel. As Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ata ibn Abi Marwan, from his father, he said: Ali was brought al-Najashi after he had drunk wine in Ramadan, so he struck him eighty, then ordered him to prison, then brought him out the next day and struck him twenty, then said: I only flogged you these twenty for your breaking the fast in Ramadan and your daring against Allah, mighty and majestic. He said: So that indicated, from Alis exceeding the forty to what is above it for wine, that what was from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of flogging therein was not a seeking from him of a known number. And in that is what indicates that it was not a hadd but was a tazir. And that is also indicated by what others besides Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, reported from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in that. Among them is Abd al-Rahman ibn Azhar."
"2797","2450 - As Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Usamah ibn Zayd narrated to us, he said: Ibn Shihab narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Azhar al-Zuhri narrated to me, he said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of Hunayn passing through the people asking about the dwelling of Khalid ibn al-Walid, then a drunk man was brought, and he commanded those who were with him and they struck him with what was in their hands, then he threw dust upon him. Then Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, was brought a drunk man, and he aimed at what had been of their striking in the presence of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and struck him forty. Then Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, was brought a drunk man and struck him forty. Do you not see that Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, only struck after the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forty therein by aiming at the striking of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) which had been in the like of it, not because that striking was intended toward a known number. And among them is Abu Said al-Khudri."
"2798","2451 - As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Abu al-Tayyah, from Abu al-Waddak, from Abu Said, he said: I will not drink the nabidh of the jar after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was brought one intoxicated, and he said: O Messenger of Allah, I did not drink wine; I only drank nabidh of dates and raisins in a gourd. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded concerning him, and he was pushed with hands and struck with sandals."
"2799","2452 - And as Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Matar narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Al-Masudi narrated to us, from Zayd al-Ammi, from Abu al-Siddiq or Abu Nadrah, from Abu Said al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) struck for wine with two sandals forty times, then Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, made for each sandal a lash. And among them is Abu Hurayrah."
"2800","2453 - As Yunus narrated to us, he said: Anas ibn Iyad narrated to us, from Yazid ibn al-Had, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was brought a drinker, and he said: Strike him. So some of them struck him with his hand and with his garment and his sandal. And among them is Uqbah ibn al-Harith."
"2801","2454 - As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Affan narrated to us; and as Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us; and as Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Al-Mualla ibn Asad narrated to us, they said: Wuhayb narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Ibn Abi Mulaykah, from Uqbah ibn al-Harith, he said: Al-Nuayman was brought to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while drunk, and it distressed the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) severely. So he commanded those who were in the house to strike him, and they struck him with sandals and palm stalks upon his heels, and I was among those who struck him, except that Ibn Abi Dawud said in his hadith: with al-Nuayman or Ibn al-Nuayman. And among them is Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him."
"2802","2455 - As Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Khushaysh al-Basri narrated to us, he said: Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Hisham narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) flogged for wine with palm stalks and sandals, and Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, flogged forty. When Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, took charge, he called the people and said: What do you see about the hadd for wine? Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf said to him: I see that you make it like the lightest of the hadds and make therein eighty."
"2803","2456 - And as Fahd narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us; and as al-Kaysani narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, they both together from Qatadah, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was brought a man who had drunk wine, and he commanded concerning him and he was struck with palm stalks about forty times. Then Abu Bakr did the like of that. When it was Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, he consulted the people, and Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf said: O Commander of the Believers, the lightest of the hadds is eighty. So he did that. Abu Jafar said: Do you not see what we reported from Ali of his saying about the hadd for wine: it is something we devised, and what is in the hadith of others besides him of the aiming mentioned therein? And in that is what indicates that there was in wine in the time of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) no known hadd, nor after him, until there was from his Companions, may Allahs pleasure be upon them, in that what was from them therein. And when what had been from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) therein was not a hadd, it was a tazir, and therein is exceeding the ten to what is above it of what is mentioned in those hadiths. And therein from Ali is what was from him concerning al-Najashi, the tazir of twenty, and in that is what exceeded the ten. And in what we mentioned from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is what indicates that the imam may exceed the ten in tazir to what is above it of what it is permissible for him to exceed it to. And in that is what opposed the hadith of Abu Burdah which we mentioned, and in its opposing it the two hadiths have become counterbalanced, since we do not know the abrogated of the two from the abrogating. So when they are counterbalanced, the view is broadened for those who differ in that, and the worthier of those two meanings is sought, so by that it is broad for them to abandon the hadith of Abu Burdah for its contrary of what had been from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of punishment for drinking wine. Rather, had an objector said: It is worthier than the hadith of Abu Burdah due to the practice of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) after him by it, he would not be reproached in that. And from Allah, mighty and majestic, we ask guidance."
"2804","Chapter 389 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his approving Amr ibn al-As for his leading the people in prayer while in janabah, when he feared death for himself from the cold if he bathed. 2457 - Abu al-Qasim Hisham ibn Muhammad ibn Qurrah ibn Abi Khalifah narrated to us, he said: Abu Jafar Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Salamah al-Azdi narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Aswad al-Nadr ibn Abd al-Jabbar narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahiah informed us, from Ibn Abi Habib, from Imran ibn Abi Anas, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Jubayr, Abu Jafar said: and he is the freedman of Nafi ibn Abd Amr al-Qurashi, from Amr ibn al-As, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) put him in command over the army of Dhat al-Salasil, and in the army were a number of the Muhajirun and the Ansar, and among them Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him. Then Amr ibn al-As had a wet dream on a severely cold night, and feared he would die if he bathed, so he performed wudu then led his companions. When he arrived, Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, came forward and complained about Amr ibn al-As until he said: And he led us while in janabah. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) turned away from Umar. When Amr arrived he entered upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and began informing of what he had done in his expedition. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Did you pray while in janabah, O Amr? He said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah, a wet dream befell me on a cold night the like of which never passed over me, so I gave myself the choice between bathing and dying, or accepting the concession of Allah, mighty and majestic, so I accepted the concession of Allah, mighty and majestic, and I knew that Allah, mighty and majestic, is more merciful to me. So I performed wudu, then prayed. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You did well; I would not like you to have left anything you did; had I been among the people I would have done as you did. Abu Jafar said: So some people among those who profess hadith went in this to what is in this hadith, of using wudu in place of tayammum, and went to that it therein is above tayammum, and among those who went to that of them was Ahmad ibn Salih. Abu Jafar said: So we examined this hadith and what the goers to it said, that wudu in this incident in their view is above tayammum: is it as they said or not? So we found that of their saying corrupt, because Allah, mighty and majestic, made wudu a purification from the ritual impurities other than what He made bathing obligatory therein of them, namely the janabahs, and made purification from the janabahs bathing. And we found Allah, mighty and majestic, made tayammum with earth at the lack of water a substitute for wudu for the prayers when there is need for that, and made it a substitute for bathing from the janabahs. So we came to know thereby that by tayammum there is purification from the janabahs, and it is like bathing, and it is above wudu at the lack of water. And since that was likewise for the janabahs at the lack of water, it becomes impossible thereby that the wudu which was made a purification from the impurities below the janabahs could be a purification from the janabahs in any state, because the things which are substitutes for things are only other than them, not a part of their parts. Then we sought the wudu which was from Amr at his need for bathing from janabah at his lacking water: why was that? So we found it possible that it was from him, while there was then no purification at the lack of water with earth nor with anything besides it, so the ruling at that was the permissibility of his performing that prayer without bathing, since he was in the ruling of one with no janabah upon him necessitating bathing, since he had no water with him to bathe with. So the obligation of bathing fell from him thereby, and he became as he would be had he not been in janabah. So wudu sufficed, as wudu suffices one who wakes from his sleep with no janabah upon him, and as it suffices one with no covering with him that he not pray naked, due to the falling of the obligation of covering from him. And we found among the actions of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) before the ordaining of tayammum, their praying while in ritual impurity without wudu."
"2805","2458 - As Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Yunus al-Thalabi al-Kufi, known as al-Susi, narrated to us, he said: Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent Usayd ibn Hudayr and some people with him seeking a necklace Aishah had forgotten in a dwelling we had alighted at. Then the prayer came, and they found no water, so they prayed without wudu. They mentioned that to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so the verse of tayammum was revealed. Then Usayd ibn Hudayr said: May Allah reward you with good; by Allah, no matter ever befell you that you dislike but Allah, mighty and majestic, made for you and for the Muslims therein good. Abu Jafar said: So what the Muslims did then was the obligation of Allah, mighty and majestic, upon them in how they perform their prayers, because when the obligation of wudu with water fell from them due to their lacking water for it, the obligation of prayer did not fall from them. So the obligation upon them was to pray them upon what they were of the impurity they were in. And that was strengthened by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) standing upon what they did of that and not rejecting it against them. So how would he reject it against them when it was their obligation, the like of which is the obligation of one unable to pray toward the Kabah which Allah, mighty and majestic, obligated the creation to pray toward, that he pray toward other than it? And like what we mentioned about the lack of clothing which covers private parts in prayer, that whoever is afflicted with that prays with private parts uncovered. So likewise one who lacks water while in janabah, with no substitute for him to bring him out of janabah into purity, neither with earth nor with other than it, prays without bathing from the janabah he is in. And likewise, if he is in janabah in a cold time and fears if he bathes for it that he will die from that bathing, the ruling of bathing for it falls from him, and his ruling thereby returns to the ruling of one with no bathing upon him from the janabah he is in. And it becomes obligatory upon him to pray with his janabah for which there is no purification upon him, as he would pray it had he bathed for it. So this is the meaning which Amr ibn al-As employed in this hadith, and which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) approved for him. And that wudu of his was not a purification from janabah, but it was a purification for the sleep from which he woke. As for the ruling in what is after the time in which was from Amr what was of what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) approved, when the concession in tayammum with earth was revealed, it is tayammum, with which no wudu suffices in place of bathing, and tayammum is indispensable therein. And in what we uncovered of these meanings is what indicates the corruption of the saying of whoever said it for what we related from these sayers who went to what we related from them in this chapter, and the establishment of the opposite of their sayings in that. And from Allah, mighty and majestic, we ask guidance."
"2806","Chapter 390 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his answer to Abu Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrah, may Allah be pleased with him, when he said to him: Is anyone better than us? We accepted Islam with you and struggled with you, by his saying to him: Yes, a people after you who believe in me though they have not seen me. 2459 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Dahhak al-Bablutti narrated to us; and Muhammad ibn Sinan al-Shayzari narrated to us: Abd al-Wahhab ibn Najdah al-Hawti narrated to us: Abu al-Mughirah narrated to us: al-Awzai narrated to us: Usayd ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to me, from Khalid ibn Durayk, from Ibn Muhayriz, he said: I said to Abu Jumuah Habib ibn Siba, a man of the Companions: Narrate to us a hadith you heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: Yes, I will narrate to you a fine hadith. We lunched with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and with him was Abu Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrah, may Allah be pleased with him, and he said: O Messenger of Allah, is anyone better than us? We accepted Islam with you and struggled with you? He said: Yes, a people after you who believe in me though they have not seen me. Then an objector said: How is it permissible for you to accept this from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when the Book of Allah, mighty and majestic, repels it, because Allah said in His Book: Not equal among you are those who spent before the conquest and fought; those are greater in rank than those who spent afterwards and fought; and to each Allah promised the best. And reports of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) besides it repel it."
"2807","2460 - And there was mentioned in that what Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Muawiyah ibn Qurrah al-Muzani narrated to me, he said: I heard Kahmas saying: I heard Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood among us as I stand among you today, and said: Do good to my Companions, then those who follow them, then those who follow them. Then lying will spread, until a man testifies to testimony without being asked for it, and until he swears an oath without being asked to swear."
"2808","2461 - And what Bakkar also narrated to us, he said: Abu Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Israil ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr narrated to us, he said: Jabir ibn Samurah narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, addressed us at al-Jabiyah, then he mentioned the same. Abu Jafar said: And we delayed the rest of what was reported from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, in this chapter, to bring it in a place of this book of ours more worthy of it than this place, if Allah wills."
"2809","2462 - And what Bakkar narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Mansur and Sulayman, from Ibrahim, from Abidah, from Abd Allah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The best of you are my generation, then those who follow them, then those who follow them. Then there will succeed a people whose testimony precedes their oaths, and their oaths their testimony."
"2810","2463 - And what Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Zurarah ibn Awfa, from Imran ibn Husayn, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The best of my nation are the generation I was sent among, then those who follow them. He said: And Allah knows best whether he mentioned the third or not? Then there will arise a people who testify though they are not called to testify, and make vows and do not fulfil them, and betray and are not trusted, and fatness will spread among them."
"2811","2464 - And what Bakkar narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us; and what Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Zayd al-Harawi narrated to us, they said: Hisham narrated to us, from Qatadah, then he mentioned the same with its chain."
"2812","2465 - And what Ahmad ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Al-Hawti narrated to us, he said: Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Hilal ibn Yasaf, he said: I entered the mosque of Basrah, and there was a man in a circle saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The best of people are my generation, then those who follow them, then those who follow them. Then there will come a people who grow fat and love fatness; they give testimony before they are asked for it. So I asked about him, and they said: This is Imran ibn al-Husayn."
"2813","2466 - And what Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from al-Jurayri, from Abu Nadrah, from Abd Allah ibn Mawalah, he said: I was walking with Buraydah al-Aslami while he was saying: O Allah, join me with my generation of whom I am, three times. So I said: And me? So he supplicated for him, then said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: The best of this nation are the generation I was sent among, then those who follow them, then those who follow them. Then a people will swear whose testimony precedes their oaths, and their oaths their testimony."
"2814","2467 - And what Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Al-Husayn al-Jufi narrated to us, from Zaidah, from Asim, from Khaythamah, from al-Numan ibn Bashir, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The best of you are my generation, then those who follow them, then those who follow them. Then there will succeed a people whose testimony precedes their oaths, and their oaths their testimony."
"2815","2468 - And what Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Abu Bishr, from Abd Allah ibn Shaqiq, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The best of the nation are my generation, then those who follow them, then those who follow them, then I do not know whether he mentioned the third or not? Then there will succeed after them successors whom fatness pleases, and they testify though they are not called to testify."
"2816","2469 - And what Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Mushir narrated to us, he said: Sadaqah ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Sharahil narrated to me, from Bilal ibn Sad, from his father, he said: We said: O Messenger of Allah, which of your nation is best? He said: I and my peers. He said: We said: Then what? He said: Then the second generation. He said: We said: Then what? He said: Then the third generation. He said: We said: Then what? He said: Then there will come a people who testify though they are not called to testify, and swear though they are not asked to swear, and are entrusted but do not discharge. He said: So in these reports is the preferring by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of the generation among whom he was sent over all his nation."
"2817","2470 - Abd Allah ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Hisham ibn Sad informed me, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Abu Said, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: We went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the year of al-Hudaybiyah, and he said: There will surely come peoples whose deeds you will deem your own deeds insignificant beside their deeds. We said: Who are they, O Messenger of Allah? Quraysh? He said: No; the people of Yemen, they are more tender in hearts and softer in hearts. We said: Are they better than us, O Messenger of Allah? He said: Had one of them a mountain of gold and spent it, he would not reach the mudd of one of you nor its half. The superiority of what is between us and the people is this verse: Not equal among you are those who spent before the conquest and fought; those are greater in rank than those who spent afterwards and fought; and to each Allah promised the best, and Allah is Aware of what you do."
"2818","2471 - And what Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Sad narrated to us, then he mentioned the same with its chain. So our answer to him therein, by the guidance of Allah, mighty and majestic, and His aid, is that what he recited to us from the Book of Allah, mighty and majestic, and what he mentioned to us from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), do not repel what we reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of the hadith we mentioned at the head of this chapter, because it is possible that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intended by what is in the hadith we reported at the head of this chapter a people who had not come to him up to his saying that saying mentioned therein, whose belief in him and assent to him, may Allahs pleasure be upon them, had preceded before that, while between them and coming to him stood what stands between them and that, of the enemy preventing from it, and of the lack of what would carry them to him and convey them to him, and that did not cut them off from assent to him and belief in him. Then they came to him after that and joined those who had preceded them before that in coming to him and in fighting with him and in spending in that, and in disposing as He disposes them, like what those who were with him before that were upon, and that was before the conquest which Allah, mighty and majestic, mentioned in the verse we recited. So they were all equal in these causes, except belief in him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and assent to him in his absence, for by that they excelled over those besides them who believed in him of those who were with him, seeing the establishment by Allah, mighty and majestic, of the proofs with which those of understanding cannot reject them nor depart from them. So this is a meaning the hadith we reported at the beginning of this chapter can bear, of what does not depart from the verse this objector recited to us, nor from the reports he mentioned to us from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And Allah knows best the reality of the matter in that, except that this is what our understanding reached of it. And from Allah we ask guidance."
"2819","Chapter 391 - Explanation of the difficulty in the rulings of those who came after those whom the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) praised in the reports we reported in the preceding chapter. 2472 - Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Muawiyah ibn Yazid ibn Salih informed us, he said: Khalaf ibn Khalifah Abu Ahmad narrated to us, from Ata ibn al-Saib, from al-Shabi, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) woke in the morning and said: Is there any water? Is there any water? Is there any waterskin? So the waterskin was brought and placed before the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he spread his fingers, and water gushed from between the fingers of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) like the staff of Musa (peace be upon him). So he commanded Bilal to call out to the people for wudu. When he finished and prayed Fajr with them, then sat, he said: O people, which creatures have the most wondrous belief? They said: The angels. He said: And how should the angels not believe when they witness the matter? They said: The prophets, O Messenger of Allah. He said: How should the prophets not believe when revelation descends upon them from heaven? They said: Then your Companions, O Messenger of Allah. He said: How should my Companions not believe when they see what they see? But the people with the most wondrous belief are a people who come forth after me, believing in me though they have not seen me, and affirming me though they have not seen me; those are my brothers."
"2820","2473 - And Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Nadr Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dimashqi narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Rabiah narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Waqid, from Busr ibn Abi Artah, from Abd Allah ibn al-Sadi, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The best of my nation are its first and its last, and between that is a crooked middle who are not of my nation, nor am I of them. Abu Jafar said: Al-thabaj is the middle. So what we mentioned in this chapter indicates that after those whom the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) disparaged in the reports we reported in the chapter before this chapter, there are a people of the nation of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) whose ways are praiseworthy, of the rank which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioned for them in what we reported in this chapter, and he informed that they are its people and made them thereby brothers, may Allahs pleasure be upon them. And that is intelligible, since there remains of his nation the Mahdi about whom has been reported what we shall mention in the rest of this book of ours, if Allah wills, and the band which will fight the Dajjal before the descent of Isa son of Maryam (peace be upon him), of whom the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) testified to belief by his saying: and the remainder of the believers will be in al-Urdunn, and those of them who choose adherence to the religion of Allah and insight therein until the Dajjal kills him upon that, for his belying him and his affirming what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said about him. And from Allah we ask guidance."
"2821","Chapter 392 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about his marrying the woman who offered herself to him to the man who asked him to marry her to him, without referring back to her in that nor consulting her therein. 2474 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Malik ibn Anas informed me, from Abu Hazim, from Sahl ibn Sad al-Saidi, may Allah be pleased with him, that a woman came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, I have offered myself to you. So she stood a long standing. Then a man stood and said: O Messenger of Allah, marry her to me if you have no need of her. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do you have anything to give her as dower? He said: I have nothing but this lower garment of mine. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If you give it to her, you will sit with no lower garment. So seek something. He said: I find nothing. He said: Seek, even an iron ring. So he sought and found nothing. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Do you have anything of the Quran with you? He said: Yes, such and such a surah and such and such a surah. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I have married her to you. Then an objector said: How is it permissible for you to accept this, in his marrying a woman who offered herself to him to another besides him, of one whom that man did not ask him to marry her to him? So our answer to him, by the guidance of Allah and His aid, is that this hadith in the narration of Malik has no addition to what we reported upon him, but Sufyan ibn Uyaynah reported it from the shaykh of Malik from whom he reported it, with an addition therein to what Malik reported upon him, which necessitates for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) his marrying her to the man to whom he married her, without seeking her permission in that."
"2822","2475 - As al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Abu Hazim, from Sahl ibn Sad, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: I was with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when a woman came and said: She has offered herself to you, so give your opinion concerning her. Then a man stood and said: Marry her to me. So he was silent until he said that two or three times. Then he said: Do you have anything? He said: No. He said: Go and seek. So he went and sought and found nothing. Then he came to him and said: I found nothing. So he said: Go and seek, even a ring of iron. So he went and sought, then came and said: I found nothing. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Do you have anything of the Quran with you? He said: Yes, such and such a surah. He said: Go, for I have married her to you with what you have of the Quran."
"2823","2476 - And as Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Yazid al-Muqri informed us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, he said: Abu Hazim narrated to us, from Sahl ibn Sad, he said: I was among the people when a woman said: I have offered myself to you, O Messenger of Allah, so give your opinion concerning me. Then a man stood and said: Marry her to me. He said: Go and seek, even a ring of iron. So he went and brought nothing, not even a ring of iron. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do you have anything of the surahs of the Quran? He said: Yes. So he married him with what he had of the surahs of the Quran."
"2824","2477 - And as Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Mansur informed us, from Sufyan, he said: I heard Abu Hazim saying: I heard Sahl ibn Sad saying: I was among the people with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when a woman stood and said: O Messenger of Allah, she has offered herself to you, so give your opinion concerning her. So he was silent and did not answer her with anything until she did that three times. Then he mentioned the rest of the hadith. So in this hadith, by what that woman addressed the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with, is her giving him free disposal to give his opinion concerning her. So in that was what made it free for him to marry her to another, so he married her to the man who asked him to marry her to him. And like this is what the people of knowledge employed after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning the mudarib forbidden from handing the money for mudarabah with which another was entrusted to him, unless its giver says to him: Act therein by your opinion. So by that it becomes his to hand it to whom he sees fit, to take his place, and to act therein as he would have acted therein had he acted therein, and for him to have of its profit what he assigns to him of it. So likewise is what was from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the affair of that woman who offered herself, when she gave him in her offering herself to him that he give his opinion concerning her. And from Allah, exalted, we ask guidance."
"2825","Chapter 393 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of what indicates the aspect of what the people of knowledge differ about, of a thing that is between two partners: does one of them have the right to use it by his right therein or not? 2478 - Ahmad ibn Hammad al-Balkhi narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abd Allah ibn Bukayr narrated to us, he said: Yaqub ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Zuhri narrated to us, from Abu Hazim, from Sahl ibn Sad, that a woman came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, I have come to offer myself to you. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) looked at her, raising his gaze to her and lowering it, then lowered his head. When the woman saw that he had decided nothing concerning her, she sat. Then a man of his Companions stood and said: O Messenger of Allah, if you have no need of her, marry her to me. He said: Do you have anything? He said: No, by Allah, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Go and see whether you find anything. So he went, then returned and said: No, by Allah, O Messenger of Allah, I found nothing. He said: Look, even a ring of iron. So he went, then returned and said: No, by Allah, O Messenger of Allah, not even a ring of iron; but this is my lower garment. Sahl said: He had no upper garment, so half of it is hers. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What will you do with your lower garment? If you wear it, there will be nothing of it upon her, and if she wears it, there will be nothing of it upon you. So the man sat until his sitting was long. He said: Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw him turning away, so he commanded him to be called. He said: What do you have of the Quran? He said: With me is such and such a surah and such and such a surah, enumerating them. He said: Do you recite from memory? He said: Yes. He said: Go, for I have given her to you in marriage with what you have of the Quran."
"2826","2479 - And Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Qutaybah ibn Said informed us, he said: Yaqub ibn Abd al-Rahman informed us, then he mentioned the same with its chain. So we examined this hadith and found therein the saying of the man mentioned therein to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): I give her half my lower garment as dower, and the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to him at that: What will you do with your lower garment? If you wear it, there will be nothing of it upon her, and if she wears it, there will be nothing of it upon you. So in that was what indicates that had the matter proceeded between them in that lower garment likewise, each one of them would wear it in its entirety at a time by his owning half of it. Had that not been so, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not have said to him this saying, just as he did not say to him: If other than you two wears it, or other than her, there will be nothing upon you nor upon her. So that indicates that of the right of each of the two owners of the like of that of garments, and of other than it of what is not divisible, or of what if divided is divided, is to use it likewise, and that alternation proceeds therein, so each one of the two owners uses it by his right of ownership therein for a known time, until they are equal in its benefits. And if division proceeds therein, it is divided between them: a part of it sufficing the right of one of them is placed in his hand for some duration, and a part of it is placed in the hand of the other of them for that duration, which he uses by his right of ownership which he owns in what is of it. And this agrees with the school of those who say about a house that is between two men, and one of them seeks dwelling in his share of it while the other refuses: alternation is employed therein between them as we mentioned. And among those who go to that of the people of knowledge is Abu Hanifah, may Allah have mercy on him, and his companions. And they have therein opponents of the people of knowledge of those who say: That belongs to neither of them except by his companion granting him that. And from Allah, mighty and majestic, we ask guidance."
"2827","Chapter 394 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about seeking forgiveness for the polytheists, of prohibition or permission. 2480 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Khalil, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: I heard a man seeking forgiveness for his parents while they were both polytheists, so I said: Do you seek forgiveness for your parents while they are both polytheists? He said: Did not Ibrahim seek forgiveness for his father? So I mentioned that to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and there was revealed: And the seeking of forgiveness of Ibrahim for his father was only because of a promise he had promised him. He said: Abu Jafar said: So in what we reported of this hadith is Alis, may Allah be pleased with him, rejection against the man mentioned therein of his seeking forgiveness for his parents while they were polytheists, and Alis mentioning that to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the revelation of what is mentioned of the Quran in that, or his reciting to him what he recited to him of the Quran in that. And it was not clarified for us in this hadith whether that mans parents were alive or whether they were dead at his seeking forgiveness for them, except that one of the two verses mentioned therein has a meaning necessitating standing upon it, namely His saying, mighty and majestic, by which He forbade seeking forgiveness for them after it became clear to them that they are companions of Hell. So in that was what indicates that seeking forgiveness for them before it becomes clear to them that they are companions of Hell is contrary to that, and in that is what permits seeking forgiveness for them while belief is hoped from them, and forbidding it from them after it is despaired of from them, and that is only after their death."
"2828","2481 - And Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Kathir al-Abdi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan informed us, then he mentioned the same with its chain."
"2829","2482 - And Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Khalil, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: I heard a man seeking forgiveness for his parents while they were both polytheists, so I said: Do you seek forgiveness for your parents while they are both polytheists? He said: Did not Ibrahim (peace be upon him) seek forgiveness for his father? He said: So I mentioned that to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: It is not for the Prophet and those who believe to seek forgiveness for the polytheists, the two verses. Abu Jafar said: So in what we reported of this hadith is Alis, may Allah be pleased with him, rejection against the man mentioned therein of his seeking forgiveness for his parents while they were polytheists, and Alis mentioning that to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the revelation of what is mentioned of its revelation of the Quran in that, or his reciting to him what he recited to him of the Quran in that. And it was not clarified for us in this hadith whether that mans parents were alive or dead at his seeking forgiveness for them, except that one of the two verses mentioned therein has a meaning necessitating standing upon it, namely His saying, mighty and majestic, by which He forbade seeking forgiveness for them after it became clear to them that they are companions of Hell. So in that was what indicates that seeking forgiveness for them before it becomes clear to them that they are companions of Hell is contrary to that, and in that is what permits seeking forgiveness for them while belief is hoped from them, and forbidding it after it is despaired of from them, and that is only after their death."
"2830","And there has been reported from Abd Allah ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, what indicates this meaning. As Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Abu Jafar said: thus is in my book, and the correct is Ibn Abi Maryam, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: Ibrahim (peace be upon him) kept seeking forgiveness for his father until he died; when he died it became clear to him that he was an enemy of Allah, so he dissociated from him."
"2831","2483 - And as Muhammad ibn al-Hajjaj al-Hadrami and Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhammad ibn al-Mughirah al-Kufi narrated to us, they said: Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Muawiyah ibn Salih narrated to me, from Ali ibn Abi Talhah, from Ibn Abbas, His saying, mighty and majestic: It is not for the Prophet and those who believe to seek forgiveness for the polytheists, even if they are near kin, after it has become clear to them that they are companions of Hell. So they used to seek forgiveness for them until this verse was revealed. When it was revealed they refrained from seeking forgiveness for their dead, and He did not forbid them to seek forgiveness for the living until they die. Then Allah, mighty and majestic, revealed: And the seeking of forgiveness of Ibrahim for his father was only because of a promise he had promised him; then when it became clear to him that he was an enemy of Allah, he dissociated from him, meaning he sought forgiveness for him while he was alive; when he died he refrained from seeking forgiveness for him. So in that was what indicates what we mentioned of what we interpreted the hadith of Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, upon. And that was strengthened by the saying of Allah, mighty and majestic, relating from His Prophet Ibrahim (peace be upon him): And forgive my father; indeed he was of those astray. And we bore the hadith of Ali ibn Abi Talhah from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, though he did not meet him, because according to the people of knowledge of chains it was taken from the book in which are these hadiths from Mujahid and from Ikrimah. And it has been reported that the cause of revelation of what we recited in the hadith of Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, was for other than the meaning for which we mentioned the revelation of what had been because of it."
"2832","2484 - As Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Yaman al-Hakam ibn Nafi al-Bahrani narrated to us, he said: Shuayb ibn Abi Hamzah informed us, from al-Zuhri, he said: Said ibn al-Musayyib informed me, from his father, he said: When the death of Abu Talib approached, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to him and found with him Abu Jahl and Abd Allah ibn Abi Umayyah ibn al-Mughirah. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Abu Talib: O uncle, say: There is no god but Allah, a word by which I shall testify for you with Allah. So Abu Jahl and Abd Allah ibn Abi Umayyah said: Do you turn away from the religion of Abd al-Muttalib? The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) kept offering it to him while those two repeated to him that saying, until Abu Talib said as the last of what he spoke to them: Upon the religion of Abd al-Muttalib, and refused to say: There is no god but Allah. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: By Allah, I shall surely seek forgiveness for you so long as I am not forbidden concerning you. So Allah, mighty and majestic, revealed: It is not for the Prophet and those who believe to seek forgiveness for the polytheists, even if they are near kin, the verse. And He revealed concerning Abu Talib: You do not guide whom you love, but Allah guides whom He wills."
"2833","2485 - And as Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn Jafar and Ubayd ibn Rijal narrated to us, they said: Ahmad ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, then he mentioned the same. Abu Jafar said: So in this hadith is that Allah, mighty and majestic, only revealed the prohibition of seeking forgiveness for the polytheists due to what was from Abu Talib, and that that was after his death upon what he died upon. And it has been reported that the cause of its revelation was contrary to that."
"2834","2486 - And as Musab ibn Ibrahim al-Zubayri narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, he said: Al-Darawardi narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Muslim narrated to us, from his uncle, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, that when the death of Abu Talib approached, then he mentioned the same, and did not go beyond Said ibn al-Musayyib with it."
"2835","2487 - As Ahmad ibn Dawud ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Harmalah ibn Yahya narrated to me, he said: Abd Allah ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Ibn Jurayj informed me, from Ayyub ibn Hani, from Masruq ibn al-Ajda, from Abd Allah ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out one day and we went out with him until we ended at the graves. Then he commanded us and we sat, then he stepped over the graves until he ended at a grave among them. Then he sat and conversed with it long, then the weeping of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) rose weeping. So we wept for the weeping of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) turned to us, and Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, met him and said: What made you weep, O Messenger of Allah, for you have made us weep and frightened us? So he took the hand of Umar, then turned to us and we came to him. He said: Did my weeping frighten you? We said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah. He said: The grave you saw me conversing with is the grave of Aminah daughter of Wahb, and I sought permission from my Lord, mighty and majestic, to seek forgiveness for her, but He did not permit me, and there descended upon me: It is not for the Prophet and those who believe to seek forgiveness for the polytheists, until the end of the verse, And the seeking of forgiveness of Ibrahim for his father. So there seized me what seizes the child for the parents of tenderness, and that is what made me weep. So Allah knows best the cause in which was the revelation of what we recited, except that it is possible that the revelation of what we recited was after all what we mentioned had been, of the cause of Abu Talib, and of the cause of Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, in what he had heard from the one seeking forgiveness for his parents, and of the Prophets (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) visit to his mothers grave, and of his asking his Lord, mighty and majestic, at that for permission for him to seek forgiveness for her. So the revelation of what we recited was an answer to all that. And there has been reported from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the permissibility of seeking forgiveness for their living."
"2836","2488 - From what Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Hamzah al-Zubayri and Ibrahim ibn al-Mundhir al-Hizami narrated to us, they said: Muhammad ibn Fulayh narrated to us, from Musa ibn Uqbah, from al-Zuhri, from Sahl ibn Sad al-Saidi, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Allah, forgive my people, for they do not know. So in this hadith is his (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) seeking forgiveness for his people who do not know, and they are those who did not believe in him nor affirm him. And there has been reported from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of what enters into this chapter."
"2837","2489 - From what Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Main narrated to us, he said: Marwan ibn Muawiyah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Kaysan narrated to us, from Abu Hazim, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I sought permission from my Lord, mighty and majestic, to seek forgiveness for my mother, but He did not permit me; and I sought His permission to visit her grave, and He permitted me. And from Allah, mighty and majestic, we ask guidance."
"2838","Chapter 395 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about his wiping over his khuffs: was it after the revelation of al-Maidah or before it? 2490 - Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ubayd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Aishah narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Ata ibn al-Saib, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wiped over the khuffs. So ask those who claim that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wiped over the khuffs: before al-Maidah or after al-Maidah? He said: By Allah, he did not wipe after al-Maidah, and for me to wipe over the back of a camel in the desert is more beloved to me than to wipe over them. So in this hadith is that the wiping of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) over his khuffs was before the revelation of al-Maidah upon him, and that he did not wipe over them after its revelation upon him. And therein is from the saying of Ibn Abbas: And for me to wipe over the back of a camel in the desert is more beloved to me than to wipe over them. So a people attached to this hadith and thereby prevented wiping over the khuffs. So we examined this hadith: does it necessitate what they carried it upon or not? So we found therein that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had wiped over the khuffs before the revelation of al-Maidah upon him, and there is not therein that he said to the people after its revelation upon him: Do not wipe over them, for what descended upon me in Surah al-Maidah of washing the feet in wudu for prayer has prevented that. Had that been so, the proof would have stood for the abrogation of wiping over the khuffs in wudu. Rather therein is only the saying of Ibn Abbas: He did not wipe over them after the revelation of al-Maidah. And it is possible that this was because he did not see the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wipe over them, while others besides him saw him wipe over them. If that was so, whoever saw him wipe over them after its revelation is worthier with what is reported than whoever reported that he did not see him wipe over them after its revelation. And we examined the saying of Ibn Abbas: And for me to wipe over the back of a camel in the desert is more beloved to me than to wipe over them, and found it possible that this was from him because he is of a people whom the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) singled out apart from the people with perfecting wudu, upon what we reported about them of what preceded in this book of ours, namely the saying of Ibn Abbas: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not single us out apart from the people except with three things: perfecting wudu, and that we not eat charity, and that we not breed a donkey upon a horse. And perfecting wudu is thoroughness therein and conveying it to its highest ranks. And in that is washing the feet, not wiping over the khuffs worn upon them. And wiping over the khuffs would be with him for others of the people remaining upon its ruling before the revelation of al-Maidah, and with that it would be his to wipe over the khuffs as others of the people wipe, even if adhering to what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) singled him out with is worthier for him than other than it. Then we looked: has there been reported from him what indicates that or not?"
"2839","So we found Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Samad ibn Abd al-Warith al-Tannuri narrated to us; and we found Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us, they said: Shubah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Musa ibn Salamah, he said: I asked Ibn Abbas about wiping over the khuffs, and he said: For the traveller three days and their nights, and for the resident a day and a night. So there was the rectification of what we reported from him in this chapter: his choosing for himself what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) singled him out with, and his informing the people who in that are contrary to him and contrary to Banu Hashim besides him, that they may wipe over their khuffs upon what is in the hadith of Musa ibn Salamah from him. And this is the finest of what presented itself to us in this chapter, after our bearing the hadith of Ata ibn al-Saib which we mentioned therein as possible, because it is of the hadith of Abu Awanah from him, and he is of those taken from in the state of confusion and before the state of confusion, so it was not known whether this hadith was of what he took before the confusion or after the confusion. Rather his hadith which was from him before his confusion is taken from four and from none besides them, namely: Shubah, and al-Thawri, and Hammad ibn Salamah, and Hammad ibn Zayd. Then we looked: has it been reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he wiped over his khuffs after the revelation of al-Maidah or not?"
"2840","2491 - So we found Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, from Hammam, he said: I saw Jarir perform wudu from the vessel for purification, then wipe over his khuffs. So it was said to him: Do you wipe over your khuffs? He said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wiping over his khuffs. This hadith used to please the companions of Abd Allah, because his Islam was after the revelation of al-Maidah."
"2841","2492 - And we found Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Abu Muawiyah al-Darir narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, from Hammam, he said: Jarir ibn Abd Allah al-Bajali urinated, then performed wudu and wiped over his khuffs. So it was said to him: Do you do this after having urinated? He said: Yes, I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) urinate, then perform wudu and wipe over his khuffs. Al-Amash said: Ibrahim said: This hadith used to please them, because the Islam of Jarir was after the revelation of al-Maidah."
"2842","2493 - And Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Abu Shihab narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, from Hammam ibn al-Harith, that Jarir ibn Abd Allah relieved himself of stool or urine, then performed wudu and wiped over his khuffs. So some of them laughed, and Jarir said to him: If you wonder, I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) do that, then wipe. Yusuf narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Abu Shihab narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, that he was pleased with the hadith of Jarir because he accepted Islam after the revelation of al-Maidah. Abu Jafar said: And in this hadith was the affirmation by Jarir of the wiping of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) over his khuffs after the revelation of al-Maidah, so it was worthier than what we reported before it in this chapter. Then an objector said: What is in this hadith of the speech of the companions of Abd Allah is without their mentioning it from Jarir, so it was a disconnected hadith. And our answer to him therein, by the guidance of Allah and His aid, is that this hadith has been reported from Jarir connected through another channel besides this channel."
"2843","2494 - As Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Bukayr ibn Amir al-Bajali narrated to us, from Abu Zurah, he said: Jarir urinated and wiped over the khuffs, and a people faulted that against him and said: This was before the revelation of al-Maidah. So he said: I only accepted Islam after the revelation of al-Maidah, and I did not see the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wipe except after it was revealed."
"2844","2495 - And as Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Abd Rabbihi narrated to us; and as Ibn Abi Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Haywah ibn Shurayh al-Hadrami narrated to us, he said: Baqiyyah ibn al-Walid narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn Adham, from Muqatil ibn Hayyan, from Shahr ibn Hawshab, from Jarir ibn Abd Allah, he said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wiping over his khuffs. So they said: After the revelation of al-Maidah? So Jarir said: I only accepted Islam after the revelation of al-Maidah. So these are two connected hadiths from Jarir, in both of them his affirmation of the wiping of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) after the revelation of al-Maidah. And from Allah we ask guidance. And in this hadith of Jarir is what Muhammad ibn Bahr ibn Matar narrated to us, he said: Al-Hasan ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Hamzah al-Zayyat narrated to us, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, he said: I never heard in wiping a hadith more beloved to me than the hadith of Jarir ibn Abd Allah, because he accepted Islam after the revelation of al-Maidah, and in the year in which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed away."
"2845","Chapter 396 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported about when the Islam of Jarir was, besides what we reported in the chapter before this chapter. Fahd narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim and from Hammad, from Ibrahim, from Jarir ibn Abd Allah, he said: I accepted Islam forty days before the death of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Ibrahim said: Jarir only accepted Islam forty nights before the death of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So in this hadith is that the Islam of Jarir was only before the death of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) by forty, either days or nights. And this in our view is a rejected hadith, and we did not find it circulating except upon Musa ibn Dawud specifically. So we looked: do we find what contradicts it or not?"
"2846","2496 - So we found Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Ali ibn Mudrik, he said: I heard Abu Zurah ibn Amr ibn Jarir narrating from his grandfather Jarir, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me in the Farewell Pilgrimage: Make the people listen, then said: Do not return after me as disbelievers, some of you striking the necks of others. So in this hadith is that he was with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his pilgrimage, and in that is what indicates that his Islam was before the death of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) by forty and forty and more than that, because what is in this hadith was in Dhu al-Hijjah, and after it passed Muharram and Safar and twelve nights of the month of Rabi al-Awwal, then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed away at that, while Jarir in all that was a Muslim."
"2847","2497 - And we found Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Yahya al-Qattan narrated to us, from Ismail ibn Abi Khalid, he said: Qays ibn Abi Hazim narrated to us, he said: Jarir said to me: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: Will you not relieve me of Dhu al-Khalasah? And it was a house in Khatham called the Kabah of al-Yamaniyyah. So I set out with one hundred and fifty horsemen of Ahmas, and they were horsemen, while I could not stay firm upon horses, so he struck my chest until I saw his fingers in my chest, and said: O Allah, make him a guide rightly guided. So he set out to it and broke it and burned it, then sent to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informing him. Then the messenger of Jarir said: By Him Who sent you with the truth, I did not come to you until I left it like a mangy camel. He said: So he blessed the horses of Ahmas and its men five times. So in what we reported was the repulsion of that also, and the necessity of the precedence of the Islam of Jarir."
"2848","2498 - And we found Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Aban ibn Abd Allah al-Bajali narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Jarir narrated to me, from Jarir, he said: Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, sent to me Ibn Abbas and al-Ashath ibn Qays, and they came to me while I was at Qarqisiya. They said: The Commander of the Believers conveys salam to you and informs you that what Allah showed you of separating is good; come to me and I shall lodge you the lodging of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in which he lodged you. So Jarir said to them: The Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent me to Yemen to fight them and call them; when they say: There is no god but Allah, their blood and their wealth become forbidden to me. So I shall never fight a man who says: There is no god but Allah, ever. So we returned upon that. And in that also is what necessitates the precedence of the Islam of Jarir and the length of the duration of his Islam in the life of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) exceeding the forty mentioned in what we reported in this chapter. And from Allah, exalted, we ask guidance."
"2849","Chapter 397 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about Surah al-Maidah: is it the last surah revealed or not? Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Muawiyah ibn Salih narrated to me, from Abu al-Zahiriyyah, from Jubayr ibn Nufayr, he said: I performed hajj and entered upon Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, and she said to me: O Jubayr, do you recite al-Maidah? I said: Yes. She said: It is the last surah revealed; whatever you find therein of lawful, deem it lawful, and whatever you find therein of unlawful, deem it unlawful."
"2850","Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Muawiyah ibn Salih narrated to me, then he mentioned the same with its chain. So in this hadith from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, is that al-Maidah is the last surah revealed. And contrary to that has been reported from al-Bara ibn Azib."
"2851","As Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, he said: Abu Ishaq narrated to us, he said: I heard al-Bara ibn Azib saying: The last verse revealed is: They ask you for a ruling; say: Allah gives you a ruling about kalalah. And the last surah revealed is Baraah."
"2852","Al-Hasan ibn Ghulayb narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Bara ibn Azib, he said: The last surah revealed complete is Surah Baraah, and the last verse revealed is the ending of al-Nisa. So we examined what was reported from Aishah and what was reported from al-Bara of this disagreement about the last surah revealed, what it is. So what we reported in that from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, was more likely in our view, and Allah knows best the truth, because the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, with Surah Baraah in the pilgrimage which Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, performed with the people before the Farewell Pilgrimage, and he recited it to the people until he completed it. And there shall come of what was reported in that hereafter in this book of ours in a place more worthy of it than this place, if Allah wills. So Surah al-Maidah had revealed of it after that in the Farewell Pilgrimage what has been reported from Umar and Ali and Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them, in that."
"2853","2499 - From what Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Numayr narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Idris narrated to us, from his father, from Qays ibn Muslim, from Tariq ibn Shihab, he said: A Jew said to Umar, may Allah be pleased with him: Had this verse been revealed upon us: This day I have perfected for you your religion, the verse, we would have taken it as a festival. So Umar said: I know the first day it was revealed upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); it was revealed upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the eve of Friday, while we were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at Arafat."
"2854","2500 - And what Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Qays ibn Muslim, from Tariq ibn Shihab, that some Jews said: Had this verse been revealed among us, we would have taken that day as a festival. So Umar said: Which verse? They said: This day I have perfected for you your religion and completed upon you My blessing. So Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I know in what place it was revealed; it was revealed while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was standing at Arafah."
"2855","2501 - Ahmad ibn Khalid ibn Yazid al-Farisi narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abd al-Hamid al-Himmani narrated to us, he said: Qays ibn al-Rabi narrated to us, from Ismail ibn Salman, from Abu Umar al-Bazzar, from Ibn al-Hanafiyyah, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: There was revealed upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was standing on the evening of Arafah: This day I have perfected for you your religion."
"2856","2502 - And what Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Habban ibn Hilal narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, he said: Ammar informed us, Abu Jafar said: and he is Ibn Abi Ammar, freedman of Banu Hashim, he said: We were with Ibn Abbas when he recited this verse: This day I have perfected for you your religion and completed upon you My blessing. Then a man of the Jews said: Had this verse been revealed upon us, we would have taken its day as a festival. He said: It was revealed on two festivals together, on the day of Arafah and on Friday."
"2857","2503 - And what Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us; and what Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, they said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, then he mentioned the same with its chain. So in what we reported was what established that the revelation of part of al-Maidah was while the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was standing at Arafah in the Farewell Pilgrimage. So that indicates what Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, said, and what al-Bara said therein is negated. And from Allah we ask guidance."
"2858","Chapter 398 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his answer to Usamah when he said to him: Alight in your house in Makkah, and: Did Aqil leave for us any quarters or houses? Yunus and Bahr together narrated to us, they said: Abd Allah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, that Ali ibn Husayn informed him that Amr ibn Uthman informed him, from Usamah ibn Zayd, that he said: O Messenger of Allah, will you alight tomorrow in your house in Makkah? He said: And did Aqil leave for us any quarters or houses? And Aqil and Talib had inherited Abu Talib, he and Talib, while Jafar and Ali, may Allah be pleased with them both, did not inherit, because they were both Muslims, while Aqil and Talib were disbelievers. And Umar ibn al-Khattab used to say: The believer does not inherit the disbeliever. Abu Jafar said: So we examined his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Did Aqil leave for us any quarters or houses, and found it joined with it in this hadith: And Aqil and Talib inherited Abu Talib, he and Talib, because they were both disbelievers, while Jafar and Ali did not inherit him because they were both Muslims. So it is possible that this is from the speech of al-Zuhri, because he used to mix his speech much with his hadith until it is imagined to be of it. And for that reason Musa ibn Uqbah said to him: Separate your speech from the speech of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), besides that we have encompassed in knowledge that this is not from the speech of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And one who argues argued with the saying of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): And did Aqil leave for us a dwelling to spend the night in, that the land of Makkah is owned, and he had no proof in this in our view, because the attribution of the house by Usamah to him, and his attributing it to himself, may be for his dwelling it was, not upon that he was its owner, as Allah, exalted, attributed the house of the spider to the spider, not that it owns it, but for its dwelling it. And as He, mighty and majestic, related to us in the story of His Prophet Sulayman (peace be upon him) of the saying of the ant: O ants, enter your dwellings, upon attribution, not upon verification. And as is said: O lord of the house, and as is said: the covering of the beast, by attribution, not by verification of ownership. So likewise is what he attributed to himself and what Usamah attributed to him: it may bear what we mentioned. And the proof of that is that nothing of the wealth of Abu Talib returned to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), because its heir was other than him, nor did anything of the wealth of Abd al-Muttalib return to him, because Abd Allah, the father of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), had died before Abd al-Muttalib. And from Allah, mighty and majestic, we ask guidance."
"2859","Chapter 399 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his saying: Whoever performs wudu like this wudu of mine, then comes to the mosque and prays two rakahs, he is forgiven what preceded of his sin; and do not be deluded. 2505 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Al-Hasan ibn Musa al-Ashyab narrated to us, he said: Shayban ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim ibn al-Harith al-Taymi, he said: Muadh ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to me that Humran ibn Aban informed him, he said: I brought Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, water for purification while he was sitting in the sitting places. So he performed wudu and perfected wudu, and said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) perform wudu in a sitting place and perfect wudu, then said: Whoever performs wudu like this wudu of mine, then comes to the mosque and prays two rakahs, Allah, mighty and majestic, forgives him what preceded of his sin. And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: And do not be deluded."
"2860","2506 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Dahhak narrated to us, he said: Al-Awzai narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ibrahim narrated to me, he said: Shaqiq ibn Salamah narrated to me, he said: Humran, freedman of Uthman, narrated to me, from Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, then he mentioned the same. And what Shayban reported this hadith upon was more likely in our view than what al-Awzai reported it upon, because al-Awzai mentioned in its chain Shaqiq ibn Salamah, while Shaqiq we do not know among those Muhammad ibn Ibrahim narrated from, nor among those he met. And as for the meaning of the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): And do not be deluded, that in our view, and Allah knows best, is: And do not be deluded and sin, then act upon coming to the mosque and praying therein two rakahs for you to be forgiven and so you are forgiven, because it is possible that death, which cuts off from the like of that, cuts them off from that. And from Allah we ask guidance."
"2861","Chapter 400 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying about charity: There is no right therein for a rich man nor for a strong earner. 2507 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Anas ibn Iyad narrated to us; and Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Jafar ibn Awn narrated to us; Anas said: from Hisham ibn Urwah, while Jafar said: Hisham ibn Urwah narrated to us; then they agreed and said: from his father, from Ubayd Allah ibn Adi ibn al-Khiyar, he said: Two men of my people narrated to me that they came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was distributing charity, and asked of it. So he raised his gaze and lowered it, and saw them both sturdy and strong. He said: If you wish, I shall do it, but there is no right therein for a rich man nor for a strong earner."
"2862","2508 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith and al-Layth ibn Sad informed me, from Hisham ibn Urwah; and Bakkar narrated to us, he said: Al-Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to me, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah and Hammam narrated to us, from Hisham, and he mentioned the same with its chain. Abu Jafar said: So we examined this hadith in its chain and found therein: from two men of the people of Ubayd Allah ibn Adi whom he did not name, so it is thereby known that they are of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so what they reported must be accepted. And it is possible that they were not of his Companions, but were of the Bedouins of those who confronted him about charity. But we examined it with that to stand upon what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intended by his answer with which he answered those two men. So we found his saying: There is no right therein for a rich man, meaning charity, that is: I have no knowledge of the reality of your affairs, of riches or poverty, while you are more knowledgeable of that than I. So act therein with what what you heard from me therein necessitates, that there is no right therein for a rich man. Then we examined his saying: Nor for a strong earner, and found charity may be lawful for the poor strong man. And the meaning of his saying: And no right therein for a strong earner, he intends (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the right which is the highest ranks of rights to charity, with which it is deserved, and it is not the strength nor the sturdiness with which one is free from need of it, as the Arabs emphasize a thing of this kind, saying: So-and-so is truly learned, when he is in the highest ranks of knowledge, and do not say it of one who is below its highest ranks though he is learned. And like that is what has been reported from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of what he said about Abu Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrah, may Allah be pleased with him."
"2863","2509 - As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Silah ibn Zufar, from Hudhayfah, he said: The people of Najran came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: Send to us a trustworthy man. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I shall surely send to you a trustworthy man, truly trustworthy, truly trustworthy. So the people craned their necks for it, and he called Abu Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrah, may Allah be pleased with him."
"2864","2510 - As Fahd narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn al-Himmani narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahim ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Zakariyya, from Abu Ishaq, from Silah, from Hudhayfah, he said: The bishop of Najran came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his household, then he mentioned the same."
"2865","2511 - And as Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Israil narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Silah ibn Zufar, from Ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, that al-Aqib and al-Sayyid, the two chiefs of Najran, came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he wanted to invoke curses with them. So one of them said to his companion: Do not invoke curses with him, for by Allah, if he is a prophet and we invoke curses with him, we shall not prosper, nor our posterity after us. But we shall give him what he asks. They said: We shall give you what you asked, so send with us a trustworthy man, and send with us none but a trustworthy man. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his household said: I shall surely send with you a trustworthy man, truly trustworthy, truly trustworthy. So his Companions craned their necks for it, and he said: Stand, O Abu Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrah. When he turned his back he said: This is the trustworthy one of this nation. So his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) therein: Truly trustworthy, truly trustworthy, is his affirmation for Abu Ubaydah of the highest ranks of trustworthiness, though there may be of its people one who is below him therein, and not of its highest ranks. So likewise is the saying of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): And no right therein for a strong earner; it is upon this meaning, and upon the highest ranks of deserving it, though among its deservers is one who is below that in deserving it. And from Allah, mighty and majestic, we ask guidance."
"2866","Chapter 401 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying, while he was upon the grave of one of his two daughters whom Uthman had married: None who approached his family last night shall enter the grave. 2512 - Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ubayd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Aishah al-Taymi narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Thabit, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: One of the daughters of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: None who approached his family last night shall enter the grave. So her husband did not enter. Abu Jafar said: So this daughter of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is Umm Kulthum; she died, and her death, may Allah be pleased with her, was in the ninth year of the Hijrah. And we examined the saying of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): None who approached his family last night shall enter the grave, and found muqarafah may be of blameworthy speech. And it was impossible in our view that he intended thereby intercourse, because a mans intercourse with his family is not blameworthy. And those to whom the descending into her grave and entering her into it belonged were of her kin with whom marriage is forbidden, and we do not know there was present then besides the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), because he is her father, and besides his uncle al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib, and besides whoever touched her by a forbidden kinship from her mothers side, namely her brother by her mother Hind ibn Abi Halah al-Tamimi, and whoever there may be between him and her a sanctity by suckling. So these were the worthiest of people to enter her into her grave. And it is possible that among them besides the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was one between whom and his family had been a muqarafah which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not praise of him, so he did not like thereby that he undertake of his daughter what he would undertake had that not been from him. And it had been of his character (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with which Allah, mighty and majestic, honoured him and placed him in the highest ranks of character, that he not confront anyone with a thing that had been from him of what he disliked of him; rather he says that allusively about him. Like what was reported from him at the saying of the people of Barirah in their selling Aishah, meaning Barirah while she was a mukatabah, a sale by which she is freed upon that her wala be ours: that he addressed the people and said: What is the matter with peoples who stipulate conditions not in the Book of Allah, mighty and majestic? Whoever stipulates a condition not in the Book of Allah, it is void, even if a hundred conditions; the decree of Allah is more worthy, and His condition more binding; wala is only for whoever frees. And we shall mention that with its chain hereafter in this book of ours, if Allah wills. So he made them hear that by his addressing all the people while they are among them thereby, for them to desist from it. And of his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): What is the matter with men, one of whom says: I have divorced you, I have taken you back."
"2867","2513 - Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Muammal ibn Ismail narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu Burdah, from Abu Musa, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), intending thereby its doers, and others besides them of those he addressed with that. So likewise bears his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): None who approached his family last night shall descend into the grave, because among those he addressed with that was one from whom that had been in that night of his, and among them was one from whom it had not been therein. So he said that saying for one from whom that had been in that night of his to hear it, so as not to enter her grave. And this is the finest of what we were able of the meanings of this hadith upon which it comes out. And as for what is therein of the saying of its reporter: So her husband did not enter, meaning her grave, that a people carried upon its bearing with them that between him and her before her death in that night had been this muqarafah. And they are those who go to that a man may wash his wife after her death. So they said: As it is his to wash her after her death, likewise it is his to enter her into her grave after her death. As for us, our school is that he does not wash her after her death, due to the severance of what was between him and her in her life by her death, and he in our view is outside that, not inside it. And this hadith has been reported through another way with an addition upon this meaning."
"2868","2514 - As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us; and as Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Dawud narrated to us, they said: Fulayh ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Hilal ibn Ali, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: We witnessed a daughter of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was sitting upon the grave. Then I saw his eyes shedding tears, and he said: Is there among you anyone who did not approach his family last night? So Abu Talhah said: I. He said: Then descend. So he descended into her grave. So what was in this hadith of what is related therein from Abu Talhah is far from the hearts, because Abu Talhah was not of her kin who undertake that of her, besides that its reporter, namely Fulayh ibn Sulayman, does not have of precision nor of firmness in narration as what the reporter of the first hadith has, namely Hammad ibn Salamah from Thabit al-Bunani. O Allah, unless there attended her grave then none of her kin with whom marriage is forbidden besides the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he needed his help upon that, and there was from Abu Talhah what was, to help him upon that. And that is broad for him, as it is broad for men who are not of forbidden kinship to dead women, when there are not in their presence kin of them, to touch them from behind garments in place of washing them. And from Allah we ask guidance."
"2869","Chapter 402 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about who was to enter those who died of the wives of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) into their graves. Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Said al-Qattan narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Abi Khalid narrated to us, he said: Amir narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Abza narrated to me, he said: I prayed with Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, over Zaynab, may Allah be pleased with her, in Madinah. So he magnified over her four times, then sent to the wives of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Whom do you command to enter her into the grave? He said: And it pleased him that he be the one to undertake that. He said: So they sent to him: Look at whoever used to see her in her life, let him be the one to enter her into the grave. So Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: You spoke truth. Abu Jafar said: So in this hadith is that Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, had been pleased that he be the one to undertake entering her into her grave. And that in our view, and Allah knows best, was because she was a mother to him, because Allah, mighty and majestic, said: The Prophet is closer to the believers than themselves, and his wives are their mothers. And he was thereby a son to her, so for that it pleased him that he be the undertaker of entering her into her grave. Then he sought corroboration in that with what was with the remaining after her of the wives of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in that, because they therein are like her, and because that ruling by which he clarified therein, she clarifies therein. And that was of what may fall in its like confusion, if she was, though she was a mother to him and he was a son to her, then it is his to marry a daughter of her daughters, and that what is between him and her of sonship and of motherhood therein differs from motherhood and sonship in lineages and in suckling. So he returned to what was with them in that, to stand upon its reality, since the like of that is of what does not fall upon them however the ruling therein was which they knew from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and he stopped them upon it. So they informed him that entering her into her grave belongs to whoever used to see her in her life. So that differed from what the matter had been upon with him therein before that. And thereby became clear with him that their motherhood to the believers and the sonship of the believers to them has a special ruling, contrary to the ruling of sonship to lineage, and contrary to the ruling of motherhood from suckling, since motherhood from lineage and motherhood from suckling both permit looking from the children to each one of those two kinds, to whoever by him is a mother to them. And motherhood by lineage which is between them and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) does not permit them that. And motherhood from lineage and from suckling both prevent marrying whoever those mothers bore, of daughters, while motherhood by the Prophets (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) marrying whom he married of women does not prevent the like of that, because it is permissible for the believers to marry what they bore of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), of daughters, and what they bore of other than him of them. So that motherhood had a ruling distinct from the ruling of the other two motherhoods. And since that was so, Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, sought it from its people, namely the remaining of the wives of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And we encompassed in knowledge that they did not take the ruling of that motherhood except from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and that they did not take it from the direction of deduction nor from the direction of extraction, because the like of that is not taken from the direction of deduction nor from the direction of extraction; rather it is taken from the direction of tawqif, and tawqif therein and in its likes is only from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). From this direction we entered this hadith among the hadiths of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and sought from it what we sought from his hadith which preceded our mentioning it in what preceded of this book of ours. And Hajjaj ibn Ibrahim reported this hadith from Abu Awanah, from Firas ibn Yahya, from Amir, and differed with Ismail in the deceased woman mentioned therein of the wives of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), mentioning therein that she is Umm Habibah, in place of what Ismail mentioned therein that she is Zaynab."
"2870","As Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Firas, from Amir, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abza, that Umm Habibah, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), died, and Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, prayed over her and magnified over her four times, and sent to the Mothers of the Believers: Who shall enter her into her grave? So they said: Whoever was lawful for him to see her in her life. Abu Jafar said: And this in our view is an error, because Umm Habibah remained after the death of Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, a long time. Then we sought this hadith from another direction besides Hajjaj ibn Ibrahim, of what returns to Firas, how it is?"
"2871","So we found Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, he said: Firas narrated to us, from Amir al-Shabi, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abza, he said: I prayed with Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, over Zaynab daughter of Jahsh, and he magnified over her four times. And we mentioned in what preceded of this book of ours what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying to his wives: The first of you to join me is the longest of you in hand, and that they used to stretch their hands, and Aishah says in that: And Zaynab daughter of Jahsh was a short woman, and she used to make with her hands what she aids with in the path of Allah, mighty and majestic. And he taught them thereby that she was the longest of them in hand with good. And from Allah, mighty and majestic, we ask guidance."
"2872","Chapter 403 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the interpretation of the saying of Allah, mighty and majestic: He it is Who sent down upon you the Book; of it are verses decisive, up to His saying: And none remembers but those of understanding. 2515 - Al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: Nafi ibn Umar al-Jumahi narrated to me, from Ibn Abi Mulaykah, he said: Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, narrated to me, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When you see them, beware of them, and when you see them, beware of them. Then he quoted: As for those in whose hearts is disease, they follow what is ambiguous of it, seeking discord, up to the saying of Allah, mighty and majestic: Except Allah and those firm in knowledge. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Those firm in knowledge are those who believed in its ambiguous and acted upon its decisive."
"2873","2516 - Ubayd ibn Rijal narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Muhammad al-Shafii narrated to us, he said: Al-Harith ibn Umayr narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Ibn Abi Mulaykah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited: He it is Who sent down upon you the Book; of it are verses decisive, they are the Mother of the Book, and others ambiguous. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When you hear those who dispute about it, they are those whom Allah, mighty and majestic, meant, or they are those whom Allah, mighty and majestic, said."
"2874","2517 - As Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Umar al-Hawdi narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Mulaykah narrated to us, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited this verse: He it is Who sent down upon you the Book; of it are verses decisive, to the end of the verse. She said: So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When you see those who follow what is ambiguous of it, those are those whom Allah, mighty and majestic, named, so beware of them."
"2875","2518 - And as Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Zayd al-Makki narrated to us, he said: Al-Qanabi narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Ibrahim al-Tustari narrated to us, from Abd Allah ibn Abi Mulaykah, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same. He said: So we examined this hadith and found therein the saying of Allah, mighty and majestic: He it is Who sent down upon you the Book; of it are verses decisive, they are the Mother of the Book. So He, mighty and majestic, informed us that of His Book are verses decisive in interpretation, namely those agreed upon in their interpretation, and the intelligible intended by them, and that of it are verses ambiguous whose interpretation is sought from the decisive verses which are the Mother of the Book, namely the verses differed about in their interpretation. Then He, mighty and majestic, said: As for those in whose hearts is deviation. And deviation is injustice away from uprightness and from justice, and abandoning fairness to its people. So they follow what is ambiguous of it, seeking thereby the like of what was from the past nations in what their messengers, Allahs blessings upon them, brought them, seeking discord, namely the corruption of relations until there is from it killing, and other than it of what it brings of hatred and rancour and division, with which matters proceed contrary to what Allah, mighty and majestic, commanded therein by His saying: And hold fast to the rope of Allah all together and be not divided. And whoever is likewise exits Islam and becomes of other than it and deserves the Fire. And there has been reported in the interpretation of this verse, from Abu Umamah al-Bahili, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), in these meanings, an addition to what is in the hadith of Aishah of them."
"2876","2519 - As Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Humran al-Humrani narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Masadah al-Bahili narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghalib narrated to us, he said: I arrived in Damascus and came to its mosque and found Abu Umamah in the mosque. So I greeted him and sat with him, then he rose and I rose with him until we ended at the door of the mosque, and there were heads raised upon spears, nearly seventy heads. When Abu Umamah looked at them, then stopped, he said: Glory be to Allah, glory be to Allah, three times. What does Satan do with these, three times. Then he said: The worst slain under the shade of heaven, three times, and the best slain are those whom these kill. And he wept. So I said to him: O Abu Umamah, you say this saying about them then weep? He said: Out of mercy for them; they were of the people of Islam then left it. Then he recited this verse which is in Al Imran: He it is Who sent down upon you the Book; of it are verses decisive, they are the Mother of the Book, and others ambiguous, until he completed the verse. Then he said: They are these. Then he recited this verse: On the day faces whiten and faces blacken, until he completed the verse. Then he said: They are these. He said: I said: O Abu Umamah, is this something you narrate from your opinion or something you heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: Glory be to Allah, glory be to Allah, three times. I would then be audacious, he said that three times, had I not heard it but once or twice or three times or four times, until he reached seven, I would not have narrated it to you. Then he said: Who are you? He said: I said: Of the people of Iraq. He said: They are with you many. Abu Jafar said: So what is in this hadith indicates what we mentioned. Then He, mighty and majestic, informed in this verse of the incapacity of creation to interpret the ambiguous which He mentioned therein, by His saying, mighty and majestic: And none knows its interpretation but Allah. Then He, mighty and majestic, informed of what those firm in knowledge say in that, for them to imitate it and adhere to it and follow them therein, namely His saying, mighty and majestic: And those firm in knowledge say: We believe in it; all is from our Lord. So likewise are the people of truth in the ambiguous of the Quran: they refer it to its Knower, namely Allah, mighty and majestic, then seek its interpretation from the decisive verses which are the Mother of the Book. If they find it therein, they act by it as they act by the decisive verses, and if they do not find it therein due to the shortcoming of their knowledge from it, they do not go beyond in that belief in it and referring its reality to Allah, mighty and majestic, and do not employ therein the conjectures which Allah forbade them to employ in other than it. And when employing them in other than it is unlawful, employing them therein is more unlawful. And of that is the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Disputation about the Quran is disbelief, and we shall bring that hereafter in a place more worthy of it than this place in the rest of this book of ours, if Allah, exalted, wills. And with Him is guidance."
"2877","Chapter 404 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about men striking their wives, of prohibition and of permission. 2520 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Ismail ibn Kathir, from Asim ibn Laqit ibn Sabirah, the delegate of Banu al-Muntafiq, from his father, he said: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), I and a companion of mine. Then my companion mentioned his wife and mentioned her foul tongue and long tongue. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Divorce her. He said: She has companionship and children. He said: Speak to her; if there is good in her she will accept. And do not strike your wife as you strike your slavegirl."
"2878","2521 - Al-Rabi al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Sulaym al-Taifi narrated to us, from Ismail ibn Kathir, then he mentioned the same with its chain. He said: So in this hadith was: So do not strike your wife as you strike your slavegirl. So we examined this speech and found it possible that he intended thereby (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he not strike her as he strikes his slavegirl, but strike her a striking below that. And that was the worthiest of what it is carried upon, since Allah, mighty and majestic, permitted striking them in His Book by His saying: And those whose recalcitrance you fear, admonish them and forsake them in beds and strike them. Then we looked: has there been reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his permitting their striking them?"
"2879","2522 - So we found Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah informed us, from Dawud ibn Abd Allah al-Awdi, from Abd al-Rahman al-Musalli, from al-Ashath ibn Qays, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: I was a guest of Umar, may Allah be pleased with him. When in part of the night he stood to his wife to strike her, and I intervened between them, and he returned to his bed. When he took his resting place he said: O Ashath, preserve from me something I heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): A man is not asked about what he strikes his wife for."
"2880","2523 - And we found Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Jafar ibn Yahya ibn Thawban, from his uncle Umarah ibn Thawban, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that men sought permission of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to strike women, and he permitted them. Then he heard a sound and said: What is this? They said: You permitted men to strike women. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The best of you are the best of you to their families, and I am the best of you to my family. Abu Jafar said: Then we wanted to stand upon that striking, what striking it is, so we sought that: do we find from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) therein anything?"
"2881","2524 - So we found Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Muhammad narrated to us, he said: Husayn ibn Azib ibn Shabib ibn Gharqadah Abu Gharqad narrated to us, from Shabib ibn Gharqadah, from Sulayman ibn Amr, from Amr ibn al-Ahwas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed in the Farewell Pilgrimage, and said in his address: Behold, fear Allah, mighty and majestic, concerning women, for they are with you as captives. You took them by the trust of Allah, and deemed their private parts lawful by the word of Allah, mighty and majestic. You have a right over them, and they have a right over you. And of your right over them is that they not permit in your houses except by your permission, nor let tread your beds whom you dislike. If they do, forsake them in beds and strike them a striking not severe. If they obey you, seek no way against them. And of their right over you is their provision and their clothing in a seemly manner. Abu Jafar said: So we came to know thereby that the striking which they were permitted against their wives is the non-severe of it. So we came to know thereby that what was forbidden in the hadith of Laqit ibn Sabirah, that a man strike of striking, is the severe striking, not the striking below it, when she deserves that of him. And from Allah, mighty and majestic, we ask guidance."
"2882","Chapter 405 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying to Husayn al-Khuzai, father of Imran ibn Husayn, when he taught him to supplicate: O Allah, forgive me what I erred, and what I intended, and what I knew, and what I was ignorant of. 2525 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Bishr al-Abdi narrated to us, he said: Zakariyya ibn Zaidah narrated to us, he said: Mansur ibn al-Mutamir narrated to us, he said: Ribi ibn Hirash narrated to us, from Imran ibn Husayn, he said: Husayn came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) before he accepted Islam and said: O Muhammad, Abd al-Muttalib was better to his people than you; he used to feed them liver and hump, while you slaughter them. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him what Allah willed him to say. Then Husayn said: O Muhammad, what do you command me to say? He said: Say: O Allah, I seek refuge in You from the evil of my soul, and ask You to decree for me rightness in my affair. He said: Then Husayn accepted Islam, then came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: I had asked you the first time, and now I say: What do you command me to say? He said: Say: O Allah, forgive me what I concealed, and what I disclosed, and what I erred, and what I intended, and what I was ignorant of, and what I knew."
"2883","2526 - Ahmad ibn Dawud ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Salih al-Azdi narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Yala narrated to us that he came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) before he accepted Islam, then he mentioned this hadith, except that he said: And what I erred, and what I intended, and what I reasoned, and what I was ignorant of. He said: So we examined this hadith and found therein his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): O Allah, forgive me what I erred. Then an objector said: And how does he ask forgiveness for what he erred, when Allah, mighty and majestic, says: There is no blame upon you in what you erred therein, but what your hearts intended? So our answer to him therein, by the guidance of Allah, mighty and majestic, and His aid, is that this error which the imaginer imagined is contrary to intent, but it is an error of the errors which they err, of what enters into the saying of Allah, mighty and majestic: Our Lord, take us not to task if we forget or err, of the faults which they err, and of what enters into His saying, mighty and majestic: Because of their faults they were drowned and made to enter a Fire. So that is upon the faults which they earned by their intending them and by their intending them, not their opposites of error which is from them of what they do not intend, nor intend to, nor fall into by their choosing it. And as for his saying, upon him be peace: And what I was ignorant of, its meaning of what I was ignorant of is: what I did ignorantly with my intending it, with my knowledge of it, and my offence against myself by my entering into it and my doing it. Then an objector said: This hadith has been reported with what contradicts it from Imran ibn Husayn."
"2884","2527 - And he mentioned what Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Said ibn al-Asbahani narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Mushir narrated to us, from Dawud ibn Abi Hind, from al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Rahman, and he is Ibn Rabiah ibn al-Harith al-Hashimi, from Imran ibn al-Husayn, that his father al-Husayn ibn Ubayd came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while a polytheist and said: Tell me of a man who used to honour the guest and join kinship and died before you. Abu Jafar said: As though he means thereby his father. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: My father and your father are in the Fire. He said: Not twenty nights passed until he died a polytheist. He said: So in this hadith is that Husayn, father of Imran ibn Husayn, died a polytheist. And in the first hadith is mentioned his Islam and the Prophets (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) teaching him what he mentioned teaching him therein. And this is a severe discrepancy. So our answer to him therein, by the guidance of Allah, mighty and majestic, and His aid, is that this, though a discrepancy as mentioned in these two hadiths, is not from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); rather it is from the reporters of these two hadiths. And Allah knows best the reality of the matter therein, what it is, except that we examined them and found them possibly coming out with no discrepancy therein, namely that Imran be the son of Husayn ibn Husayn ibn Ubayd, so the father Husayn mentioned with Islam in the first of the two hadiths we mentioned in this chapter be his nearer father, he who accepted Islam and whom the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) taught what he taught him in the hadith mentioned therein his Islam, of the two hadiths we reported in this chapter, and the one who died a polytheist be Husayn ibn Ubayd, his farther father, of his two fathers each of whose name is Husayn. So both hadiths are sound together and do not contradict. And that is worthier than what they were carried upon, so that neither of them repels its companion nor opposes it nor contradicts it. And from Allah, mighty and majestic, we ask guidance."
"2885","Chapter 406 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying when he wanted to enter a town: And Lord of the devils and what they lead astray, of what he used to seek refuge with. 2528 - Yahya ibn Uthman narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abd al-Aziz al-Wasiti narrated to us, he said: Hafs ibn Maysarah narrated to us, from Musa ibn Uqbah, from Ata ibn Abi Marwan, from his father, from Kab, he said: I testify, by Him Who parted the sea for Musa (peace be upon him), I heard Suhayb saying: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he saw a town he wanted to alight at, said: O Allah, Lord of the heavens and the earth and what they shade, and Lord of the winds and what they scatter, and Lord of the lands and what they bear, and Lord of the devils and what they lead astray, I ask You of the good of this town, and of the good of its people, and seek refuge in You from its evil and from the evil of its people and the evil of what is therein."
"2886","2529 - And Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Nasr informed us, he said: Ayyub ibn Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Uways narrated to me, from Sulayman, meaning Ibn Bilal, from Abu Suhayl ibn Malik, from his father, that he used to hear the recitation of Umar ibn al-Khattab while he led the people in the mosque of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from the house of Abu Juhaym. And Kab al-Ahbar said: By Him Who parted the sea for Musa, Suhayb narrated to me that Muhammad the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) never saw a town he wanted to enter but said when he saw it: O Allah, Lord of the seven heavens and what they shade, and Lord of the seven lands and what they bear, and Lord of the devils and what they lead astray, and Lord of the winds and what they scatter, for we ask You the good of this town and the good of its people, and seek refuge in You from its evil and the evil of its people and the evil of what is therein. And Kab swore: By Him Who parted the sea for Musa, they were the supplications of Dawud when he saw the enemy. Then an objector said: In this hadith is: And Lord of the devils and what they lead astray, while what is not for the children of Adam is only for other than them, and in its place for the children of Adam is whom. And of that is the saying of Allah, mighty and majestic: And what the beast of prey eats except what you slaughter, in examples of that in the Quran. So our answer to him therein, by the guidance of Allah, mighty and majestic, and His aid, is that the more common is other than what is mentioned, except that what is also used for the children of Adam. And of that is the saying of Allah, mighty and majestic: And the parent and what he begets, meaning Adam (peace be upon him) and whom he begot, and His saying: And wedded women except what your right hands possess, in the meaning of except whom your right hands possess, and His saying: Then marry what pleases you of women, in the meaning of whom pleases you of women, in many things of this kind of the Quran which came with what in the meaning of whom. So likewise is what came in this hadith of his saying: And Lord of the devils and what they lead astray, in the meaning of: And Lord of the devils and whom they lead astray. And from Allah, glory and exalted, we ask guidance."
"2887","Chapter 407 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the three who supplicate Allah, mighty and majestic, and He does not answer them. 2530 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Hakam narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Firas, from al-Shabi, from Abu Burdah, from Abu Musa, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Three supplicate Allah, mighty and majestic, and they are not answered: a man who gives his wealth to a fool, while Allah, mighty and majestic, said: And do not give the fools your wealth; and a man who lends on a loan and does not call to witness; and a man who has a wife of bad character and does not divorce her. Abu Jafar said: So we bore this hadith from Amr ibn Hakam, though they say in his narration what they say therein, since Muadh ibn Muadh al-Anbari narrated it from Shubah as he narrated it from him. Then we examined the meaning of this hadith and found Allah, mighty and majestic, taught His servants things by which they repel their opposites. So of that was His warning them not to hand to fools their wealth, out of mercy to them and seeking from Him the permanence of His blessings upon them. And He taught them to call to witness in their mutual lending, for that to be a preservation of the wealth of the seekers of them, and of the religion of the sought of them. And He taught them divorce which they employ when they need it. So whoever of them abandons what Allah taught him until he falls into the opposite of what he wants, opposing what He, mighty and majestic, commanded him with, his supplication is not answered for his opposing Him. And whoever besides those we mentioned in this hadith of those not disobeying their Lord, answering of supplication is hoped for him in what he supplicates. And they are those who entered into His saying, mighty and majestic: And your Lord said: Call upon Me, I shall answer you. And He warned them upon the tongue of His Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) against hastening therein the answering of supplication. And from Allah, mighty and majestic, we ask guidance."
"2888","Chapter 408 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about what was from Umm Sulaym of her taking his sweat and using it in her perfume: is it his approving that for her or his forbidding her from it? 2531 - Abu al-Qasim Hisham ibn Muhammad ibn Qurrah ibn Abi Khalifah al-Ruayni narrated to us, he said: Abu Jafar Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Salamah ibn Salamah al-Azdi narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Yahya al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: Al-Shafii narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahhab ibn Abd al-Majid narrated to us, from Ayyub al-Sakhtiyani, from Anas ibn Sirin, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to enter upon Umm Sulaym, and she would spread for him a leather mat and he would nap upon it. Then she would take of his sweat and place it in her perfume."
"2889","2532 - Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Ayyub narrated to us, from Abu Qilabah, from Anas ibn Malik, from Umm Sulaym, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to come to her and nap with her. So she would spread for him a leather mat and he would nap, and he sweated much. So she would gather his sweat and place it in perfume and bottles. Abu Jafar said: So this was of what there is not therein from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) anything indicating the ruling of his sweat, of purity and of other than it, because what is mentioned therein is only from Umm Sulaym. And it is possible that he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had not known it, so he permits it to her or forbids her from it. So we sought that: do we find it in other than this hadith or not?"
"2890","2533 - So we found Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Mutarrif ibn Abi al-Wazir narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Musa narrated to us, Abu Jafar said: and he is al-Fitri, from Abd Allah ibn Abd Allah, Abu Jafar said: and he is Ibn Abi Talhah, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) lay upon a leather mat and sweated. So Umm Sulaym stood to his sweat and absorbed it and placed it in a bottle. And the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) finished for her and asked her. She said: O Messenger of Allah, I wanted to place your sweat in my perfume. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) laughed."
"2891","2534 - And we found Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Al-Aswad ibn Amir narrated to us, he said: Israil informed us, from Umarah ibn Zadahn, from Thabit, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to nap with Umm Sulaym, and he sweated much. So she prepared for him a leather mat upon which he napped. Then she would take his sweat and place it in a bottle. He said: What is this, O Umm Sulaym? She said: Your sweat, O Messenger of Allah, I place it in my perfume. Abu Jafar said: So in these two hadiths was mention of the Prophets (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) standing upon what was from Umm Sulaym in that, and his leaving rebuke of what was from her therein. So that indicates its purity with him. And we understood thereby that sweats have the ruling of the flesh of their people, and that the children of Adam whose flesh is pure, their sweats are pure also, and that other than them of the things whose flesh is eaten is likewise also in the purity of their sweats, and that the things forbidden to eat their flesh, due to prohibition or due to dislike, their sweats have the ruling of their flesh in that. And from Allah, mighty and majestic, we ask guidance."
"2892","Chapter 409 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his making the making up of hajj on behalf of one upon whom it had become obligatory like the paying of a debt which had become obligatory upon him. 2535 - Bakkar narrated to us, he said: Abu Ahmad Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Asadi al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith ibn Ayyash ibn Abi Rabiah, from Zayd ibn Ali, from his father, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abi Rafi, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: A young slavegirl of Khatham faced the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: My father is an old man, and the obligation of Allah, mighty and majestic, in hajj has overtaken him; does it suffice that I perform hajj on his behalf? He said: Perform hajj on behalf of your father. And he turned the neck of al-Fadl ibn Abbas, and al-Abbas said to him: You turned the neck of your cousin. He said: I saw a young woman and a young man, and I did not feel safe from Satan for them."
"2893","2536 - And Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Sulayman ibn Yasar informed me, from Abd Allah ibn Abbas, from al-Fadl ibn Abbas, that a woman of Khatham said: O Messenger of Allah, my father was overtaken by the obligation of Allah, mighty and majestic, upon him in hajj while he is a very old man unable to sit straight upon the back of his camel. He said: Perform hajj on his behalf."
"2894","2537 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Abd Allah ibn Yunus al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: Fudayl, meaning Ibn Iyad, narrated to us, from Hisham, from Ibn Sirin, from Yahya ibn Abi Ishaq, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, from al-Fadl ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: I was riding behind the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when a man came to him and said: O Messenger of Allah, my mother is a very old woman; if I carry her she will not hold on, and if I tie her I fear I shall kill her. He said: Tell me: had your mother a debt upon her, would you pay it? He said: Yes. He said: Then perform hajj on behalf of your mother."
"2895","2538 - And Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Ishaq, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, he said: Al-Fadl ibn Abbas or Ubayd Allah ibn Abbas narrated to me that a man said: O Messenger of Allah, my father or my mother is a very old person; if I carry her she will not hold on, and if I tie her I fear I shall kill her. He said: Tell me: had your father or your mother a debt, would you pay it? He said: Yes. He said: Then perform hajj on behalf of your father or on behalf of your mother."
"2896","2539 - And Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abi Ishaq narrated to me that a man asked Sulayman ibn Yasar about a woman wanting to free a slave on behalf of her mother. Sulayman said: Abd Allah ibn Abbas narrated to us that a man said: O Messenger of Allah, my father entered Islam while a very old man; if I tie him upon the saddle I fear I shall kill him, and if I do not tie him he will not stay firm. Shall I perform hajj on his behalf? He said: Yes. Tell me: had your father a debt, would you pay it? He said: Yes. He said: Then perform hajj on behalf of your father."
"2897","2540 - And Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Ibn Shihab, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, from Abd Allah ibn al-Abbas, that he said: Al-Fadl ibn al-Abbas was riding behind the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when a young slavegirl of Khatham faced the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: My father is a very old man, and the obligation of Allah, mighty and majestic, in hajj has overtaken him; does it suffice that I perform hajj on his behalf? He said: Perform hajj on behalf of your father. And he turned the neck of al-Fadl, and al-Abbas said to him: You turned the neck of your cousin. He said: I saw a young woman and a young man, and I did not feel safe from Satan for them."
"2898","2541 - And Ali ibn Shaybah and Abu Umayyah narrated to us, they said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, from Zakariyya ibn Ishaq, he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed us, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that a man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: My father is a very old man unable to perform hajj; shall I perform hajj on his behalf? He said: Yes. The man said: Does it suffice him? He said: Yes. Tell me: had your father a debt and you paid it on his behalf, would it not suffice him? It is only like that."
"2899","2542 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Jafar al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Ubayd Allah ibn Amr narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Muslim al-Batin, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: A man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: My father died and did not perform hajj; shall I perform hajj on his behalf? He said: Tell me: had your father a debt, would you pay it? He said: Yes. He said: Then the debt of Allah, mighty and majestic, is worthier; perform hajj on his behalf."
"2900","2543 - And Muhammad ibn Ibrahim ibn Yahya ibn Jannad al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi al-Aswad narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Abd al-Samad narrated to us, he said: Mansur narrated to us, from Mujahid, from a freedman of Ibn al-Zubayr called Yusuf ibn al-Zubayr or al-Zubayr ibn Yusuf, from Ibn al-Zubayr, from Sawdah daughter of Zamah, she said: A man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, my father is a very old man unable to perform hajj; shall I perform hajj on his behalf? He said: Tell me: had your father a debt and you paid it, would it be accepted from you? He said: Yes. He said: Then Allah, mighty and majestic, is more merciful; perform hajj on behalf of your father."
"2901","2544 - Ahmad ibn al-Hasan ibn al-Qasim al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: Ubaydah ibn Humayd al-Nahwi narrated to us, from Mansur, from Mujahid, from Yusuf ibn al-Zubayr, from Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr, the like of it, and he did not mention Sawda."
"2902","2545 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Jarir ibn Abd al-Hamid narrated to us, from Mansur, from Mujahid, from Yusuf ibn al-Zubayr, from Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr, he said: A man from Khatham came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: My father was overtaken by Islam while he is a very old man unable to mount the camel, and Hajj is obligatory upon him, so shall I perform Hajj on his behalf? He said: And are you his eldest child? He said: Yes. He said: What do you think, if your father owed a debt and you paid it on his behalf, would it suffice for him? He said: Yes. He said: Then perform Hajj on his behalf."
"2903","2546 - Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us, and Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, and Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, they all said: Shubah narrated to us, from al-Numan ibn Salim, from Amr ibn Aws, from Abu Razin al-Uqayli, he said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, my father is a very old man unable to perform Hajj and Umrah and to travel. He said: Perform Hajj on behalf of your father and perform Umrah. Abu Jafar said: In these reports was the answer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to the one who asked him, man or woman, about performing Hajj on behalf of his father or her father, or his mother or her mother, including his saying to the questioner: What do you think, if your father owed a debt and you paid it, would that suffice for him? That is, just as that suffices for him through your paying it on his behalf, so likewise the Hajj due upon him suffices for him through your performing it on his behalf. An objector said: In that is proof that Hajj is paid on behalf of the one upon whom it is due just as a debt due upon him is paid, and he inferred from that that the wealth with which Hajj is performed on his behalf is made a debt upon him in his lifetime, and a debt upon his estate after his death until it is paid on his behalf. We countered him therein, by the guidance of Allah Almighty and His help, and said: You have no proof in that that it is a debt as you stated; rather it is a right upon the body of the one upon whom it rests until it is discharged to Allah Almighty from him, or until another discharges it to Him from him on his behalf, just as a debt rests in the liability of the one upon whom it rests until it is discharged to the one to whom it belongs from him, or until another discharges it to him from him on his behalf. Had it been a debt, it would be impossible for it to be likened to a debt, for things are likened to other than themselves and are not likened to themselves. Since that is so, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) likening it to a debt proves it is not a debt. Seeking the ruling after the death of the one upon whom it rests, whether it is paid from all his wealth or from a third of it, as it was before that, there is no indication of it from this hadith, except that in this hadith is a meaning that must be noted: whoever pays a debt on behalf of another without his instructing him to do so, the one upon whom it was due is cleared of it, without an equal amount becoming due upon him to the one who paid it on his behalf, as Abu Hanifah and his companions and al-Shafii say in that regard, not as Malik and those of the people of Madinah who followed him say, that the debt reverts to the one who paid it against the one upon whom it was due. And success is from Allah."
"2904","Chapter 410 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning one who has not performed the Hajj of Islam on his own behalf: may he perform the Hajj of Islam on behalf of another or not? 2547 - Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Harun al-Bardi narrated to us, and Muhammad ibn Jafar ibn Muhammad ibn Ayan al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Numayr al-Hamdani al-Kufi narrated to us, and Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Tarif al-Bajali al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: Abdah ibn Sulayman al-Kilabi narrated to us, from Said, from Qatadah, from Azrah, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) heard a man saying: Labbayk on behalf of Shubrumah. He said: Who is Shubrumah? He said: A brother or relative of mine. He said: Have you ever performed Hajj? He said: No. He said: Make this one for yourself, then perform Hajj on behalf of Shubrumah. He said: In this hadith is the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asking the one he heard declaring Talbiyah on behalf of Shubrumah whether he had ever performed Hajj, and that one's answer to the Messenger that he had not performed Hajj on his own behalf, and his saying to him after that: Make this one for yourself, then perform Hajj on behalf of Shubrumah. A group attached themselves to this hadith and said: Whoever performs Hajj on behalf of another while he had not previously performed the Hajj of Islam on his own behalf, that Hajj counts for himself as the Hajj of Islam, following this hadith. Then they analogized to it the ihram of a man on his own behalf voluntarily while he had not performed the Hajj of Islam before that, that his Hajj counts as the Hajj of Islam. But they did not analogize to that the rulings of fasting outside Ramadan, for they said: Whoever fasts voluntarily in Ramadan, that fast neither suffices him for Ramadan nor for voluntary fasting. Yet had this hadith we mentioned been established concerning Hajj, they ought to have analogized to it voluntary fasting outside Ramadan and made it count for Ramadan rather than voluntary, just as they made voluntary Hajj by one who had not performed the Hajj of Islam count as the Hajj of Islam rather than voluntary. Rather fasting was more deserving of this, and more fitting for that ruling, because Ramadan is a time for the fasting of all servants therein of Ramadan, not a time for fasting other than it therein, whereas the Hajj season is a time for Hajj for obligations and for Hajj for supererogatory acts. Then we examined this hadith and what else was reported of what falls under this meaning, and found this hadith turns solely upon Azrah, and this Azrah is Azrah ibn Tamim. Harun ibn Muhammad al-Asqalani mentioned to me, from al-Ghallabi, he said: Yahya ibn Said was not satisfied with Azrah, meaning the narrator of this hadith. And the standing of Yahya in this field is a standing with no equal therein. Then we examined what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on this point besides that."
"2905","2548 - We found Abu Umayyah had narrated to us, he said: Qabisah ibn Uqbah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Khalid al-Hadhdha, from Abu Qilabah, from a man among the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) heard a man declaring Talbiyah on behalf of a man, and said: If you have performed Hajj, otherwise perform Hajj on your own behalf. He said: This hadith had a better chain than the chain of the first hadith, except that we sought the man from whom Abu Qilabah reported this hadith, whether he is among those Abu Qilabah could have met and taken it from by hearing or not."
"2906","We found Ubayd ibn Rijal had narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Muhammad al-Shafii narrated to us, he said: al-Harith ibn Umayr narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, he said: Ibn Abbas heard a man saying: Labbayk on behalf of Shubrumah. He said: And what is Shubrumah? He mentioned a relative. He said: Have you performed Hajj on your own behalf? He said: No. He said: Then make it for yourself, then perform Hajj on behalf of Shubrumah."
"2907","And we found Yusuf ibn Yazid had narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Khalid informed us, from Abu Qilabah, from Ibn Abbas, then he mentioned the like of it. By that we understood that the Companion whom Abu Qilabah did not name in the first hadith is Ibn Abbas, and Abu Qilabah has no hearing from Ibn Abbas, so that hadith became disconnected, and it is not permissible for one who argues by it upon his own principle to argue with its like, since its like in his view establishes no proof."
"2908","Then we considered whether that hadith was reported from other than the channel from which we reported it or not. We found Yunus had narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith informed me that Qatadah ibn Diamah narrated to him, from Said ibn Jubayr, that he narrated to him that Abd Allah ibn Abbas was passed by a man proclaiming: Labbayk with a Hajj on behalf of Shubrumah. He said: And what is Shubrumah? He said: A man who bequeathed that Hajj be performed on his behalf. He said: Have you performed Hajj yourself? He said: No. He said: Then begin yourself and perform Hajj on your own behalf, then perform Hajj on behalf of Shubrumah. He said: This hadith from the report of Amr ibn al-Harith goes back only to the saying of Ibn Abbas, not to a report from him from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And in that is what negates the first hadith we reported at the beginning of this chapter. Likewise the hadith of Abu Qilabah from the report of Ayyub is halted upon Ibn Abbas, not raised from him to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). As for the hadith of Abu Qilabah from the hadith of Sufyan, it is raised to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), except that it contains the break between Ibn Abbas and Abu Qilabah. An objector said: There has entered into the hadith of Amr from Qatadah what has entered, namely his saying that Said ibn Jubayr narrated to him, while Qatadah never heard anything from Said ibn Jubayr, so that is proof that Amr did not record it precisely from Qatadah as Said ibn Abi Arubah recorded it from him. Our answer to him therein, by the guidance of Allah Majestic and Mighty and His help, was that Amr is regarded as precise despite what is suspected, and what came of what was suspected from Amr was not from Amr but from Qatadah, due to his skill in concealment of sources, such that that is possible from him with one who hears from him, just as its like was possible in other than him with other than Amr, among what we mentioned in our book against al-Karabisi, of which we are free from repeating here. Then we wished to look at what was reported in this chapter besides the reports we reported therein, to discern their establishment or fall."
"2909","2549 - We found Ibn Abi Maryam had narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Ibn Ata, meaning Yaqub, from his father, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) heard a man saying: Labbayk on behalf of Shubrumah. He said: Have you performed Hajj on your own behalf? He said: No. He said: Then perform Hajj on your own behalf first. He said: This hadith only goes back to Yaqub ibn Ata, and this Yaqub is not a proof in hadith according to the people of hadith. Then we considered whether another reported in this chapter."
"2910","2550 - We found Yusuf ibn Yazid had narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Layla informed us, from Ata, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) heard a man declaring Talbiyah on behalf of Shubrumah, and said: And what is Shubrumah? He mentioned a relative. He said: Have you performed Hajj on your own behalf? He said: No. He said: Then perform Hajj on your own behalf, then perform Hajj on behalf of Shubrumah. Abu Jafar said: This hadith also only goes back to Ibn Abi Layla, and Ibn Abi Layla, despite the eminence of his stature and the loftiness of his rank in jurisprudence and in other than it, is severely unsettled in memory. Then we considered whether anything besides what we mentioned was reported therein."
"2911","2551 - We found Ibn Abi Dawud had narrated to us, he said: Nuaym ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: al-Fadl ibn Musa al-Sinani narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from al-Hakam ibn Aban, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he was asked about a man who has not performed Hajj: may he perform Hajj on behalf of another? He said: The debt of Allah Almighty is most deserving to be paid. I say: This was in our view the best in chain among all we mentioned in this chapter of the hadiths we mentioned therein, except that what is in it of the answer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to the one who asked him about what he asked is only that the debt of Allah Almighty is most deserving to be paid. This differs from what is in other than it among what we reported in this chapter, and there is not in it that had he entered ihram on behalf of another that ihram would count for himself. Since we did not find in these reports what guides us to the answer in this chapter, we sought it in other than them, and found that when one asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about performing Hajj on behalf of another, he permitted it for him without asking him whether he had performed the Hajj of Islam on his own behalf or not. That proves he permitted him to perform Hajj on behalf of another even if he had not previously performed the Hajj of Islam on his own behalf. Then we considered the ruling of one who has not performed the Hajj of Islam on his own behalf and performs a voluntary Hajj on his own behalf: does it count as the Hajj of Islam, as those who said so said, or is it voluntary, as those who said so said, namely the people of Madinah and the people of Kufah?"
"2912","2552 - We found Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah had narrated to us, he said: Ubayd Allah ibn Muhammad al-Taymi narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from al-Azraq ibn Qays, from Yahya ibn Yamar, from a man among the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said:"
"2913","And Dawud narrated to us, from Zurarah, meaning Ibn Awfa, from Tamim al-Dari, all of them raising it, they both said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The first thing the servant is brought to account for on the Day of Resurrection is his prayer. If he completed it, it is written complete. If he did not complete it, Allah Almighty says to His angels: Look, do you find for My servant any voluntary acts, so complete thereby what he neglected of his obligation. And Zakah is like that. Then the deeds are taken upon that reckoning."
"2914","2553 - And we found Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud had narrated to us, he said: Asim ibn Ali ibn Asim narrated to us, he said: Hammam ibn Yahya narrated to us, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, from Hurayth ibn Qabisah, he said: I sat with Abu Hurayrah and heard him saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: The first thing the servant is brought to account for is his prayer. If it is sound, he has succeeded and prospered, and if it is corrupt, he has failed and lost. If anything is lacking from his obligation, He says: Look, does My servant have any voluntary acts, so what is lacking of the obligation is completed thereby. Then the rest of his deeds shall be upon that pattern. By what is in this hadith we inferred that a man may be one who performed voluntary Hajj while he had not previously performed the Hajj obligatory upon him. That proves it is permissible for a man to perform Hajj voluntarily while he has not performed the obligatory Hajj. And that proves it is permissible to perform on behalf of another the Hajj obligatory upon him before performing on his own behalf the Hajj obligatory upon him. And just as one upon whom the obligatory prayer has become due after its time entered may pray voluntarily then pray it after that, so it is for one upon whom the Hajj season has entered and its obligation has become due to perform voluntary Hajj on his own behalf, and to perform an obligatory Hajj on behalf of another. Then we sought the man mentioned among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the hadith of al-Azraq ibn Qays, who he is."
"2915","2554 - We found Ahmad ibn Shuayb had narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Ibrahim, meaning Ibn Rahawayh, narrated to us, he said: al-Nadr ibn Shumayl informed us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah informed us, from al-Azraq ibn Qays, from Yahya ibn Yamar, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: The first thing the servant is brought to account for is his prayer. If he completed it, otherwise Allah Almighty says: Look for My servant any voluntary acts; if voluntary acts are found for him, He says: Complete for him the obligation. And Allah we ask for guidance."
"2916","Chapter 411 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning the child, that he has a Hajj. 2555 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Uqbah informed me, from Kurayb, from Ibn Abbas, that a woman asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about a child: does this one have a Hajj? He said: Yes, and for you is a reward."
"2917","2556 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Ibrahim ibn Uqbah, from Kurayb, freedman of Ibn Abbas, from Abd Allah ibn Abbas, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by a woman while she was in her litter, and it was said to her: This is the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). She took hold of the arm of a child with her and said: Does this one have a Hajj? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Yes, and for you is a reward."
"2918","2557 - And Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Ansari narrated to us, he said: al-Qanabi narrated to us, he said: Malik narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn Uqbah, from Kurayb, freedman of Ibn Abbas, and he did not mention therein, from Ibn Abbas, then he mentioned the like of it. Abu Jafar said: This hadith from the report of Malik, none of his narrators from him raises it except Ibn Wahb and Ibn Uthmah, for they raise it from him to Ibn Abbas."
"2919","2558 - And Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Sufyan, meaning al-Thawri, narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn Uqbah, from Kurayb, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: A woman lifted a child to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: Does this one have a Hajj? He said: Yes, and for you is a reward."
"2920","2559 - And Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Abd Allah ibn Abi Salamah narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn Uqbah, from Kurayb, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"2921","2560 - And Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to me, he said: Abbas al-Duri narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Main said: Ibrahim ibn Uqbah, from Kurayb, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that a woman lifted a child of hers to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); Ibn Uyaynah erred in it, it is only mursal. Yahya said: And al-Thawri reported it from him mursal. Abu Jafar said: Yahya accomplished nothing in this, and al-Thawri reported it only raised, as we mentioned it, from Abu Umayyah, from Abu Nuaym, from him. And this hadith was also reported by Muhammad ibn Uqbah, from Kurayb, and he raised it."
"2922","2561 - As Abu Umayyah had narrated to us, he said: Qabisah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Uqbah, from Kurayb, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned the like of it. And Yahya al-Qattan and Bishr ibn al-Sari also reported it, from al-Thawri, as Qabisah reported it from him."
"2923","2562 - As Ahmad ibn Shuayb had narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna informed us, he said: Yahya ibn Said narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Uqbah, from Kurayb, from Ibn Abbas, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with this hadith."
"2924","2563 - And as Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Mahmud ibn Ghaylan informed us, he said: Bishr ibn al-Sari narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Uqbah, from Kurayb, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. And Hammad ibn Salamah also reported this hadith, from Ibrahim ibn Uqbah, from Kurayb, and did not mention Ibn Abbas therein."
"2925","2564 - As Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah had narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Uqbah informed us, from Kurayb ibn Abi Muslim, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it, without mention from him of Ibn Abbas therein. Then we considered this Hajj that comes from the child: when there occurs from the child therein what, had it come from an adult, would entail expiation upon him, and what besides it, how is that child when that comes from him, as to its being obligatory upon him or upon another among those who entered him into it, or nothing obligatory therein. We found the people of knowledge differing therein: a group among them says: Nothing is upon him therein nor upon another, among them Abu Hanifah, may Allah have mercy on him, and his companions. And a group among them says: The obligation in that is upon the one who entered him into it, among them Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim upon the meanings of the saying of Malik. And among them a group says: It is upon the child apart from any other, and likewise al-Muzani related to us from al-Shafii. We needed to seek the most deserving of these three sayings, and found that the saying of one who said the obligation in that is upon the one who entered the child into that ihram has no meaning, because that ihram did not belong to the one who entered the child into it, so that what is due therein would be upon him, and he would be responsible for clearing the child of what became due upon him therein through his entering him into it. And we found the saying of one who placed it upon the child also has no meaning, given their consensus that expiations of oaths and the rest of worships are not obligatory upon him; so the like of that worship in the like of this would not be obligatory upon him. And we found Allah Almighty made expiations for the things people incur in their Hajj a punishment for them, for His saying, Majestic and Mighty, concerning the recompense He obligated upon the killer of game in his ihram: that he may taste the consequence of his deed. And the child is not among those who enter into that, for punishments are lifted from him. When these two sayings fell and there remained only the other saying stated in this chapter, it was the most deserving of what was stated therein. If an objector says: What is the meaning of his entering into that ihram while he is among those upon whom its obligatory rulings are not binding? It is said to him: Like his entering into prayer, which is binding upon those who enter into it among adults, but not binding upon those who enter into it among non-adults."
"2926","2565 - As Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Hakam had narrated to us, he said: Harmalah ibn Abd al-Aziz ibn al-Rabi ibn Sabrah narrated to me, he said: Abd al-Malik ibn al-Rabi ibn Sabrah, my uncle, narrated to me, from his father, from his grandfather, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Teach the child prayer at seven years, and strike him for it at ten."
"2927","2566 - And as Yahya ibn Muhammad ibn Mabad al-Sabri Abu Muhammad had narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Sabrah ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to me, he said: My uncle Abd al-Malik ibn al-Rabi ibn Sabrah narrated to me, from his father, from his grandfather, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Teach the child prayer. Then he mentioned the like of it. In this hadith is the lifting of striking the child for it before ten years, while adults are struck for it in the like of that; indeed some people go beyond that with them to what is harsher than striking. An objector said: In this hadith he is struck for it at ten while he is then not adult. It was said to him: That in our view, and Allah knows best, is so he becomes accustomed to it until it becomes his character after puberty, not for other than that. And Allah we ask for guidance."
"2928","Chapter 412 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning gifts of the disbelievers to him, his accepting them and his rejecting them. 2567 - Ubayd Allah ibn Ubayd ibn Imran al-Urdunni Abu Ayyub narrated to us at Tabariyyah, he said: Khalaf ibn Hisham al-Muqri al-Bazzar narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Abu al-Tayyah, from al-Hasan, from Iyad ibn Himar, he said: He had been the close associate of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the pre-Islamic age, then he gave him a gift and he rejected it and said: We do not accept the zabd of the polytheists."
"2929","And Ubayd Allah ibn Ubayd narrated to us, he said: Khalaf ibn Hisham narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ibn Awn, he said: I asked al-Hasan: What is the zabd of the polytheists? He said: Their aid."
"2930","2568 - And Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Mamar Abd Allah ibn Amr ibn Abi al-Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Warith ibn Said narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Tayyah narrated to us, he said: al-Hasan narrated to me that Iyad ibn Himar, who had been the close associate of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the pre-Islamic age, when the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was sent came to him with a she-camel giving it to him. When he saw it he said: O Iyad, what is this? He said: I gifted it to you. He said: Lead it. So he led it. He said: Return it. So he returned it. He said: O Iyad, have you become Muslim yet? He said: No. He said: He did not accept it and said: Allah has forbidden us the zabd of the polytheists. He said: The Arabs call a gift al-zabd. Abu Ubaydah said: Al-harami may be of the people of the sanctuary, and may also be the friend, called harami."
"2931","2569 - And Musa ibn al-Hasan ibn Abd Allah al-Baghdadi, known as al-Saqalli, narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abbad al-Makki narrated to us, he said: Hatim ibn Ismail narrated to us, from Bashir ibn al-Muhajir, from Abd Allah ibn Buraydah, from his father, he said: The ruler of the Copts gifted to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) two Coptic sisters and a mule. As for the mule, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to ride it. As for one of the two slave girls, he took her as concubine and she bore him Ibrahim, and as for the other he gave her to Hassan ibn Thabit al-Ansari."
"2932","2570 - Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Yazid informed me, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd al-Qari narrated to me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent Hatib ibn Abi Baltaah to al-Muqawqis, ruler of Alexandria, with his letter to him. He accepted his letter, honored Hatib and made his hospitality good, then sent him back to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and gifted him with Hatib clothing, a mule with its saddle, and two slave girls, one of them the mother of Ibrahim, and as for the other he granted her to Jahm ibn Qays al-Abdari, and she is the mother of Zakariyya ibn Jahm, who was deputy of Amr ibn al-As over Egypt. Abu Jafar said: We only entered this hadith in this chapter because Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd al-Qari is among those born in the time of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and it is said he saw him, so he thereby entered into his companionship. A questioner asked about the reason the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) rejected the gift from Iyad, and the reason he accepted the gift from al-Muqawqis, while both were disbelievers. Our answer to him therein, by the guidance of Allah Almighty and His help, was that the disbelief of Iyad was disbelief of associating partners with Allah Almighty and denial of resurrection after death, while the disbelief of al-Muqawqis was not so, for he affirmed resurrection after death and believed in a prophet among the prophets of Allah Almighty, namely Isa (peace be upon him). Iyad and those like him were required to depart from what they were upon and leave it for its opposite, namely affirming the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and believing in him. Al-Muqawqis and other People of the Book were required to affirm the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and believe in him, while remaining upon what they were of the religion of Isa (peace be upon him). The slaughtered animals of Iyad and those like him were not eaten, nor their women married, while the slaughtered animals of al-Muqawqis and those like him were eaten and their women married. So the two groups, though all among the people of disbelief, differed in their disbelief and their rulings diverged. Every association with Allah Almighty is disbelief, but not every disbelief in Allah Almighty is association. Allah Almighty had commanded His Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) not to dispute the People of the Book except in the best manner, by His saying: And do not dispute the People of the Book except in the best manner. Into that entered al-Muqawqis and those like him who held to the Book sent down upon Isa (peace be upon him), while the polytheists who deny the Books of Allah Almighty sent down upon His prophets, the blessings of Allah upon them, were otherwise. So he accepted the gift of one whom his Lord Almighty commanded him to dispute only in the best manner, for the best is to accept his gift, and he rejected the gifts of the polytheists, for they are otherwise, and because his Lord Almighty commanded him to disavow them and fight them until religion is all for Allah Almighty. He distinguished between them in His Book, differing their names and what He ascribed to them, saying: Those who believed, and those who were Jews, and the Sabeans, and the Christians, among whom was al-Muqawqis, and the Magians, who are the associators of the non-Arabs who affirm no resurrection and believe in no Book among the Books of Allah Almighty sent down upon His prophets, and they are among the non-Arabs like idol worshippers among the Arabs except in what they differ in of taking jizyah from them, as we mentioned, and those who associated, namely the idol worshippers among the Arabs who affirm no resurrection and believe in no Book. Likewise was the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his sermon at the Farewell Hajj, distinguishing between these two groups in names and rulings."
"2933","2571 - As Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala had narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Wahb informed us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sad and Abd Allah ibn Luhayah narrated to me, from Sulayman ibn Abd al-Rahman, from al-Qasim Abu Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Umamah al-Bahili, he said: I witnessed the sermon of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of the Farewell Hajj, and he said much fine beautiful speech, and among it was: Whoever among the people of the two Books becomes Muslim has his reward twice, and has the like of what we have and upon him the like of what is upon us; and whoever among the polytheists becomes Muslim has his reward, and has the like of what we have and upon him the like of what is upon us. In what we recited of the Book of Allah Almighty and what we reported of the hadith of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is proof of the disparity of the two groups we mentioned in the disbelief they are upon, and in disavowing the people of association among them, and in not disputing the People of the Book among them except in the best manner except those of them who wrong, and in that is proof of the latitude of accepting their gifts from them. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) accepted the gift of whom he accepted from them for that reason, and rejected the gift of whom he rejected from the other group, for the reasons therein among what we mentioned in this chapter. And Allah we ask for guidance."
"2934","Chapter 413 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning his seeking help from disbelievers who sought to help him, and his barring those disbelievers he barred from fighting with him. 2572 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Malik ibn Anas informed me, from al-Fudayl ibn Abi Abd Allah, from Abd Allah ibn Niyar al-Aslami, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from Aishah, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) set out toward Badr. When he was at Harrat al-Wabarah, a man known for boldness and valor caught up with him, and the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) rejoiced when they saw him. When he caught up with him he said to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): I came to follow you and gain with you. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Do you believe in Allah Almighty and His Messenger? He said: No. He said: Return, for we shall never seek help from a polytheist. He said: Then he went on until when we were at al-Shajarah the man caught up with him and said to him as he said the first time, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him as he said the first time. He said: No. He said: Return, for we shall never seek help from a polytheist. He said: He returned, then caught up with him at al-Bayda and said to him as he said the first time: Do you believe in Allah and His Messenger? He said: Yes. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Then proceed."
"2935","2573 - Yahya ibn Uthman narrated to us, he said: Nuaym ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, he said: Malik ibn Anas informed us, from al-Fudayl ibn Abi Abd Allah, from Abd Allah ibn Niyar, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) set out to Badr until when he was at Harrat al-Wabarah a bold valiant man caught up with him. When the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw him they rejoiced at him and were impressed. He said: O Muhammad, I go out with you to fight and gain. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do you believe in Allah Almighty and His Messenger? He said: No. He said: Return, for we shall never seek help from a polytheist. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went on until when he was at Dhu al-Hulayfah he caught up with him, and that impressed the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). They said: This is so-and-so, he has returned. He said: O Muhammad, I go out with you to fight and gain. He said: Do you believe in Allah Almighty and His Messenger? He said: No. He said: Return, for we shall never seek help from a polytheist. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went on until when he was at the back of al-Bayda he caught up with him again, and that impressed the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: O Muhammad, I go out with you to fight and gain. He said: Do you believe in Allah Almighty and His Messenger? He said: Yes. He said: Then yes, in that case."
"2936","2574 - And Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Umar al-Zahrani narrated to us, he said: Malik ibn Anas narrated to us, from Fudayl ibn Abi Abd Allah, from Abd Allah ibn Niyar, from Urwah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that a man said to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he intended Badr: I go out with you. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: We do not seek help from a polytheist. Bishr said: I said to Malik ibn Anas: Does not Ibn Shihab report that Safwan ibn Umayyah traveled with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and witnessed Hunayn and al-Taif while disbeliever? He said: Yes, but he traveled with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) without the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanding him to do that."
"2937","2575 - And Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhammad ibn al-Mughirah al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Main narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi narrated to us, from Malik, from al-Fudayl ibn Abi Abd Allah, from Abd Allah ibn Niyar, from Urwah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that a man among the polytheists joined the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and fought with him. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Return, for we do not seek help from a polytheist."
"2938","2576 - And Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, he said: Malik ibn Anas narrated to us, from al-Fudayl ibn Abi Abd Allah, from Abd Allah ibn Niyar, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned the like of the hadith of Yahya ibn Uthman, from Nuaym, from Ibn al-Mubarak, except that he did not mention the meeting of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at Dhu al-Hulayfah. Abu Jafar said: In what we reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was his saying: We do not seek help from a polytheist. We mentioned in the hadith of Abu Umayyah, from Bishr ibn Umar, from Malik, from Ibn Shihab, that Safwan ibn Umayyah witnessed Hunayn and al-Taif with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while disbeliever. We sought whether we find that in a raised hadith with connected chain."
"2939","We found Fahd had narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Buhlul narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Idris narrated to us, from Ibn Ishaq, he said: Asim ibn Umar ibn Qatadah narrated to me, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Jabir, from his father Jabir ibn Abd Allah, he said: When people were routed on the day of Hunayn, Abu Sufyan ibn Harb began saying: Their rout will not stop short of the sea. Kaladah ibn al-Hanbal cried out, while with his maternal brother Safwan ibn Umayyah: Has magic become void today! Safwan said to him: Be silent, may Allah dash your mouth. By Allah, for a man of Quraysh to lord over me is dearer to me than for a man of Hawazin to lord over me."
"2940","And we found al-Rabi al-Muradi had narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Zakariyya ibn Abi Zaidah narrated to us, he said: Ibn Ishaq narrated to us, then he mentioned the like of it with its chain. So what Malik mentioned from Ibn Shihab concerning Safwan became found in the hadith of Jabir which we reported connected."
"2941","2577 - And Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: I heard Yazid ibn Harun, he said: Mustalim ibn Said informed us, he said: Khubayb ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Khubayb informed us, from his father, from his grandfather, he said: I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he intended a raid, I and a man of my people, and we had not become Muslim. We said: We are ashamed that our people witness a scene we did not witness with them. He said: And have you two become Muslim? We said: No. He said: We do not seek help from polytheists against polytheists."
"2942","2578 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun informed us, then he mentioned with its chain the like of it. An objector said: Does what you reported of the affair of Safwan, his fighting with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while polytheist, refute the rest of what you reported in this chapter from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), his saying: We do not seek help from a polytheist? Our answer to him therein, by the guidance of Allah Almighty, was that what we reported of the story of Safwan does not contradict what we reported of other than it in this chapter of the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): I do not seek help from a polytheist. For the fighting of Safwan was with him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), not by his seeking his help in that. In this is proof that he only refrained from seeking help from him and his likes, and did not bar them from fighting with him by their own choice. His leaving seeking help from them (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) may be from the saying of Allah Almighty: O you who believed, do not take intimates besides yourselves who spare you no ruin. So seeking help from them was taking them as intimates, while their fighting with him without his seeking help from them was not his taking them as intimates. An objector said: You have reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) his inviting the Jews to fight Abu Sufyan with him, though they are among those who spare him no ruin."
"2943","2579 - And he mentioned in that what Yunus had narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Shurayh narrated to me that he heard al-Harith ibn Yazid al-Hadrami narrating from Thabit ibn al-Harith al-Ansari, from one who was with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: When there reached the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the gathering of Abu Sufyan to go out to him on the day of Uhud, he went to the Jews who were in al-Nadir, and found some of them at their dwelling, and they welcomed. He said: We came to you for good; we are People of the Book and you are People of the Book, and the People of the Book owe support to the People of the Book. It reached us that Abu Sufyan has advanced toward us with a gathered people, so either fight with us or lend us weapons. He said: In this hadith is what contradicts something of what you reported in this chapter. Our answer to him therein, by the guidance of Allah and His help, was that there is nothing in that contradicting what we reported in this chapter, for the Jews whom the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) invited in this hadith to fight Abu Sufyan with him are not among the polytheists of whom the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said in the first reports that he does not seek help from them; those are idol worshippers, while these are People of the Book, whose difference from what idol worshippers are upon we mentioned in the chapter before this chapter. For these People of the Book, we and they join in belief in what they believe of the Books of Allah Almighty sent down upon whomever He sent them upon among His prophets, and we and they believe in resurrection after death, while those others believe in nothing of that. So we and these Scripturists are one hand in fighting idol worshippers, and dominance is ours, for we are the highest over them and they are followers of us in that. Thus is their ruling till now according to many people of knowledge, among them Abu Hanifah and his companions, who say: There is no harm in seeking help from the People of the Book in fighting others when our rule is dominant, and they dislike other than that when our rulings are otherwise. We seek refuge in Allah from that state. This objector said: You have reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what contradicts this."
"2944","2580 - Meaning what Ubayd ibn Rijal narrated to us, he said: Hadiyah ibn Abd al-Wahhab narrated to us, he said: al-Fadl ibn Musa al-Sinani narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Amr informed us, from Sad ibn Mundhir al-Saidi, from Abu Humayd al-Saidi, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) set out on the day of Uhud until when he left behind Thaniyyat al-Wada, he saw a rugged brigade and said: Who are these? They said: Banu Qaynuqa, the clan of Abd Allah ibn Salam, the people of Abd Allah ibn Ubayy ibn Salul. He said: Become Muslim. They refused. He said: Tell them to return, for we do not seek help from polytheists against polytheists. Abu Jafar said: The meaning of their saying in this hadith, that they are the people of Abd Allah ibn Ubayy ibn Salul, is not that Abd Allah ibn Ubayy is of them, for Abd Allah ibn Ubayy ibn Salul is not of the Jews; rather he is of the clan to whom the Ansar trace by lineage, but he persisted in hypocrisy. His lineage among them stands, and it is said they are his people by alliance, not by other than that. This objector said: This contradicts what is in the first reports in two places: one is his making them polytheists by his saying to them: We do not seek help from polytheists against polytheists; the other is his barring them from fighting with him, while in the hadith of Thabit ibn al-Harith we reported above in this chapter is the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) inviting the Jews of al-Nadir to fight with him. Our answer to him therein, by the guidance of Allah Almighty and His help, was that this hadith does not contradict that hadith nor anything we reported in this chapter, for the sense of the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to these Jews of Banu Qaynuqa in the hadith of Abu Humayd was after his (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) learning of the alliance between them and Abd Allah ibn Ubayy the hypocrite; alliance is agreement of allies with allies. By that they left the Book whose people they had been, unlike the other Jews of al-Nadir in that, by their alliance, for they did not ally with a hypocrite. Those, by allying with the hypocrite they allied, apostatized from what they had been to what he is upon, becoming like apostates of our nation to Judaism or Christianity; they are thereby neither Jews nor Christians, for their slaughtered animals are not eaten, and their women who entered with them therein are not married. Likewise Banu Qaynuqa, when they allied with Abd Allah ibn Ubayy the hypocrite and agreed with him upon his hypocrisy, left thereby the ruling of the Book whose people they had been, and became polytheists like the polytheists of the Arabs of whom the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) reported he does not seek help from them, so he did not seek their help in fighting polytheists for that reason. As for others who hold to their Book brought by the one who claims his religion, he differs from that, and there is no harm in seeking help from his like in fighting polytheists, for he is not a polytheist but a disbelieving Scripturist, an enemy of the disbelievers among idol worshippers, as we are enemies to them. And Allah Almighty we ask for guidance."
"2945","Chapter 414 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning the number for whom a badanah may be sacrificed. 2581 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Buhlul narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Idris narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from al-Miswar ibn Makhramah and Marwan ibn al-Hakam, they both said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) set out in the year of Hudaybiyah intending to visit the House, not intending fighting, and drove the sacrificial animals with him. The sacrificial animals were seventy badanah, the people were seven hundred men, and each badanah was for ten. Abu Jafar said: In this hadith each badanah of those was for ten of the people who were then with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), except that we found none among those who reported this hadith from al-Zuhri following Muhammad ibn Ishaq upon what he reported from him of the number of people then with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that they were seven hundred. Those who contradicted him therein and stated they were somewhat over a thousand: Mamar ibn Rashid and Sufyan ibn Uyaynah."
"2946","2582 - As Muhammad ibn al-Numan al-Saqati narrated to us, he said: al-Humaydi narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, al-Zuhri narrated to us, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from Marwan ibn al-Hakam and al-Miswar ibn Makhramah, they both said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) set out in the year of Hudaybiyah with somewhat over a thousand. When he was at Dhu al-Hulayfah he garlanded the sacrificial animals, marked them, and entered ihram from there. Sufyan said: My memory from al-Zuhri ends here, and it was long, so Mamar confirmed me."
"2947","2583 - Muhammad ibn Jafar ibn Ayan narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Abi Israil narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Razzaq informed us, he said: Mamar informed us, from al-Zuhri, he said: Urwah ibn al-Zubayr informed me that al-Miswar ibn Makhramah and Marwan ibn al-Hakam, each confirming the other, then he mentioned the like of it."
"2948","2584 - And Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Yaqub ibn Ibrahim, meaning al-Dawraqi, informed us, he said: Yahya al-Qattan narrated to us, reading to us from his book, he said: Abd Allah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, he said: Mamar narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from al-Miswar and Marwan, the like of it. He said: A group is worthier in acceptance and memory than one, for all the companions of al-Zuhri who reported this hadith from him agreed with Mamar and Sufyan upon what they reported from him, and contradicted Ibn Ishaq in what he reported from him. Abu Jafar said: Neither al-Miswar nor Marwan was among those who attended that or witnessed it, while Jabir ibn Abd Allah and al-Bara ibn Azib the two Ansaris were among those who witnessed it, and both report the number of the people contrary to what Muhammad ibn Ishaq reported therein."
"2949","2585 - As Yunus, al-Rabi al-Muradi, and Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Hakam narrated to us, they said: Shuayb ibn al-Layth informed us, except Muhammad, for he said: Ubayy and Shuayb ibn al-Layth informed us, then all together said: From al-Layth, he said: Abu al-Zubayr informed us, from Jabir, he said: We were on the day of Hudaybiyah fourteen hundred; we pledged to him, with Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, holding his hand under the tree, a samurah tree. We pledged to him not to flee, and did not pledge to him unto death."
"2950","And as Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Amr ibn Murrah, he said: I heard Salim ibn Abi al-Jad, Shubah said: And Husayn informed me, he said: I heard Salim saying: I said to Jabir ibn Abd Allah: How many were you under the tree? He said: We were fifteen hundred."
"2951","And as Yazid narrated to us, he said: al-Hasan ibn Umar ibn Shaqiq narrated to us, he said: Jarir narrated to us, he said: al-Amash said: Salim ibn Abi al-Jad narrated to me, he said: I said to Jabir: How many were you that day? He said: Fourteen hundred."
"2952","2586 - And as Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Zayd narrated to us, he said: al-Hulwani narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Adam narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, he said: I heard Jabir ibn Abd Allah saying: We were on the day of Hudaybiyah fourteen hundred, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You today are the best people of the earth."
"2953","2587 - And as Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Israil narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Bara, he said: We were on the day of Hudaybiyah fourteen hundred, and Hudaybiyah is a well. We drained it till not a drop remained. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sat on the edge of the well, rinsed and spat into the well, and we did not tarry long before we drew till we quenched and our mounts quenched. He said: By that it is established that the number of the people then with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was contrary to what Muhammad ibn Ishaq reported of their number. Then it was possible the badanah count was as Muhammad ibn Ishaq stated, or contrary to that, except that we have established that each badanah of them was sacrificed for seven; thus Jabir mentioned."
"2954","2588 - As Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ubayd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Aishah narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated from Qays ibn Sad, from Ata, from Jabir, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) slaughtered the cow for seven and the camel for seven."
"2955","2589 - And as Yazid narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Ismail narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated, then he mentioned with its chain the like of it."
"2956","2590 - And as Yazid narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Sulayman, from Abu Sufyan, from Jabir, he said: We sacrificed with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of Hudaybiyah seventy badanah, each badanah for seven."
"2957","2591 - And as Yazid narrated to us, he said: Abu Kamil narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Abu Bishr, from Sulayman ibn Qays, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, the like of it."
"2958","2592 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, and as Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, he said: Malik ibn Anas narrated to us, then both together said: From Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, he narrated to him that they sacrificed on the day of Hudaybiyah the badanah for seven, and the cow for seven. In this is that the seventy were only sacrificed for a portion of the people whose number was fourteen hundred. An objector said: It was reported from Ibn Abbas, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that they sacrificed with him the camel for ten."
"2959","2593 - And he mentioned what Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahim al-Harawi had narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Hanzali narrated to us, and what Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Hadiyah ibn Abd al-Wahhab narrated to us, they both said: al-Fadl ibn Musa informed us, from al-Husayn ibn Waqid, from Ilba ibn Ahmar, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on a journey, and we sacrificed the camel for ten. Our answer to him therein, by the guidance of Allah Almighty and His help, was that this hadith was reported as he stated, but it agreed with Jabir on seven and added above it, so seven became consensus, and what is above it requires proof, except that it is an addition to what is in the hadith of Jabir, and an addition is worthier. We considered: was what contradicts it reported?"
"2960","2594 - We found Ahmad ibn Dawud had narrated to us, he said: Hudbah ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: I heard Aban ibn Yazid narrating from Qatadah, from Anas, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: The camel is for seven."
"2961","2595 - And as Ahmad had narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn al-Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Aban narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Anas, raising it once and not raising it another time, the like of it. Abu Jafar said: This was worthier, for in this fixing from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of the number, which is seven, is what prevents it sufficing for more than that, except that some people argued in this for seven."
"2962","2596 - By what Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas, he said: A man asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: A she-camel is due upon me, but it has become too costly for me. He said: Buy seven sheep. He said: This proves the camel equals seven sheep. We investigated that and found this hadith corrupt in chain."
"2963","2597 - As al-Rabi al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Salim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Ata al-Khurasani, from Ibn Abbas, then he mentioned it. By that we understood that the Ata whom Ibn Jurayj reported from is not Ibn Abi Rabah, but al-Khurasani, who neither heard from Ibn Abbas nor saw him. So what we mentioned returns to requiring the ruling of seven for the badanah, namely what we reported from Anas therein, not other than it. And Allah Almighty we ask for guidance."
"2964","Chapter 415 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported concerning budn: are they of camels specifically, or of camels and cattle together? 2598 - al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Amr ibn Maymun, from Abu Hadir, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: Budn became few, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded cattle. Abu Jafar said: We reflected on this hadith, and what we found therein of the saying of Ibn Abbas, that budn became few so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded cattle, was without mention from him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he commanded cattle because they are budn. It may be he commanded them because they suffice for what budn suffice for, not that they are in themselves budn, just as he commands sheep in their place, not because they are budn."
"2965","2599 - And al-Rabi also narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Salim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abu al-Zubayr narrated to me that he heard Jabir ibn Abd Allah saying: We shared with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in Hajj and Umrah, every seven in a badanah. A man said: What of the cow, do we share in it as we share in the camel? He said: It is only of the budn. Jabir ibn Abd Allah attended Hudaybiyah and said: We shared, every seven in a badanah, and sacrificed seventy badanah that day. The inclusion of the cow among budn in this hadith is only from the saying of Jabir, without mention from him of it from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"2966","2600 - And Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Yazid informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Abu Abd Allah al-Agharr, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When Friday comes, upon every door of the mosque are angels writing the first then the first. When the imam sits, they fold the scrolls and sit listening. The likeness of the early comer is as one who gifts a badanah, then as one who gifts a cow, then as one who gifts a ram, then as one who gifts a hen, then as one who gifts an egg."
"2967","2601 - al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: al-Shafii narrated to us, and Muhammad ibn Ismail informed us, from Ibn Abi Dhib, from Ibn Shihab, from Abu Abd Allah al-Agharr, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"2968","2602 - al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: al-Shafii narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Said ibn al-Musayyab, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he mentioned the like thereof."
"2969","2603 - And Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah and Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, they both said: Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Ibn al-Had narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: The likeness of the early comer to prayer is as the likeness of one who gifts a badanah, then the one after him as the likeness of one who gifts a cow, then the one after him as the likeness of one who gifts a ram, then the one after him as one who gifts a hen, then the one after him as one who gifts an egg."
"2970","2604 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Sumayy, freedman of Abu Bakr, from Abu Salih al-Samman, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever bathes on Friday the bath of janabah then goes early, it is as though he offered a badanah. Whoever goes in the second hour, it is as though he offered a cow. Whoever goes in the third hour, it is as though he offered a horned ram. Whoever goes in the fourth hour, it is as though he offered a hen. Whoever goes in the fifth hour, it is as though he offered an egg. When the imam comes out, the angels attend listening to the remembrance."
"2971","2605 - And Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn al-Qasim narrated to us, from al-Ala ibn Abd al-Rahman, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"2972","2606 - And Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from al-Ala ibn Abd al-Rahman, from his father, he said: I heard Abu Said al-Khudri saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, then he mentioned the like of it. In what we reported in this section of this chapter is what proves to us that budn are distinct from cattle, for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) distinguished between them in names and in reward for them, even though each kind suffices for what the other kind suffices for, not because they are all budn, but because budn are the understood budn of camels, and cattle suffice for what they suffice for, not because they are budn. And Allah we ask for guidance."
"2973","Chapter 416 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning passing in front of one praying in the Sacred House and away from it. 2607 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Kathir ibn Kathir, from one of his family, who heard al-Muttalib saying: I saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) praying toward the door of Banu Sahm, with people passing in front of him, with nothing between him and the qiblah."
"2974","2608 - Ahmad ibn Dawud ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Bashshar narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, he said: I heard Ibn Jurayj narrating from Kathir ibn Kathir, from his father, from his grandfather al-Muttalib ibn Abi Wadaah, and he mentioned the like of it, except that he said: There was no screen between him and the circumambulation. Sufyan said: Kathir ibn Kathir narrated to us after I heard it from Ibn Jurayj, he said: One of my family informed me, and I did not hear it from my father."
"2975","2609 - And Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Hisham informed us, he said: The cousin of al-Muttalib ibn Abi Wadaah informed us, from Kathir ibn Kathir ibn al-Muttalib ibn Abi Wadaah, from his father, from his grandfather, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. Abu Jafar said: In this hadith is the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitting those circumambulating the House to pass before him while he prayed. An objector said: How do you accept this while you report from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)?"
"2976","2610 - He mentioned what Yunus had narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Said al-Khudri, from Abu Said al-Khudri, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you prays, let him not leave anyone passing before him; let him repel him as he can, and if he refuses let him fight him, for he is but a devil."
"2977","2611 - And what Yunus had narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Abu Said al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, the like of it."
"2978","2612 - And what Ibn Abi Dawud had narrated to us, he said: Abu Zafar narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn al-Mughirah narrated to us, from Humayd ibn Hilal, from Abu Salih, from Abu Said, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"2979","2613 - And what Yunus had narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Safwan ibn Sulaym, from Nafi ibn Jubayr ibn Mutim, from Sahl ibn Abi Hathmah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you prays toward a screen, let him draw near to it, lest Satan cut off his prayer."
"2980","2614 - And what Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud had narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Abi Yazid narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Jafar narrated to us, and what Yusuf ibn Yazid had narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Jafar narrated to us, then both together said: From Isa ibn Musa ibn Labid ibn Iyas, Yusuf said in his hadith: Ibn al-Bukayr, then both together said: From Safwan ibn Sulaym, from Nafi ibn Jubayr ibn Mutim, from Sahl ibn Sad al-Saidi, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. This objector said: In this is his (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) barring passing before one praying, and barring the praying one permitting another to pass before him, and this is the opposite of what you reported from al-Muttalib from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Our answer to him therein, by the guidance of Allah Almighty and His help, was that there is no contradiction in this, for what we reported from al-Muttalib concerns the ruling of prayer toward the Kabah by seeing it, while the other reports concern prayer by seeking the Kabah and away from it. We found prayer toward the Kabah by seeing it: people pray from its sides, facing one another, and that is unrestricted for them, not disliked. And we saw prayer elsewhere, where the Kabah is not seen, otherwise: facing one another by men is disliked, with rebuke and prohibition. By that we understood the Kabah is singled out for this ruling in prayer toward it, and in permitting people to face praying ones toward it, and to face their backs in their prayer toward it. Since that is so in their prayer toward it, passing before them in their prayer toward it is widened for them thereby, and their facing them therein with faces and backs. And we understood prayer away from it is otherwise, and since facing one another with faces and backs therein is barred, passing by them therein is constricted upon them, and permitting that therein is constricted upon those praying. By the praise of Allah and His favor it became clear there is no contradiction in anything we mentioned in this chapter, and each of the two meanings we mentioned therein is distinct in ruling from the other. And Allah we ask for guidance."
"2981","Chapter 417 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning hijrah, whether the conquest of Makkah cut it off or not. 2615 - Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Muammal ibn Ismail narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Mansur, from Mujahid, from Tawus, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said on the day of the conquest: No hijrah after the conquest, but jihad and intention; when you are called to march, march."
"2982","2616 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Qawariri narrated to us, he said: Yahya narrated to us, from Sufyan, then he mentioned with its chain the like of it."
"2983","2617 - And Fahd narrated to us, he said: al-Nufayli narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Muawiyah narrated to us, he said: Asim al-Ahwal narrated to me, from Abu Uthman, he said: Mujashi narrated to me, he said: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) after the conquest with my brother Abu Mabad to pledge him. I said: O Messenger of Allah, I came with my brother Abu Mabad for you to pledge him upon hijrah. He said: The people of hijrah have gone with all it contains. I said: Upon what will you pledge him? He said: Upon faith, or upon Islam and jihad. He said: I met Abu Mabad afterwards, he being the elder of the two, and asked him; he said: Mujashi spoke truth."
"2984","2618 - And Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Shayban, namely al-Nahwi, narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Yahya ibn Abi Ishaq, from Mujashi ibn Masud al-Bahzi, that he came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with his nephew to pledge him upon hijrah. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No, rather he pledges upon Islam, for there is no hijrah after the conquest, and he shall be of the followers in goodness."
"2985","2619 - And Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Wahbi narrated to us, he said: Shayban narrated to us, then he mentioned with its chain the like of it."
"2986","2620 - And Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Habban ibn Hilal narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Mujahid, from Safwan ibn Abd al-Rahman, or Abd al-Rahman ibn Safwan, he said: When Makkah was conquered he came with his father and said: O Messenger of Allah, grant my father a share of hijrah. He said: No hijrah today. He went to al-Abbas, and al-Abbas came out in a shirt with no cloak upon him and said: O Messenger of Allah, blessings of Allah upon you, you knew so-and-so and what was between me and him, and that he came with his father, so what bars him? He said: No hijrah. Al-Abbas said: I adjure you, O Messenger of Allah. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stretched his hand, wiped over him, entered his hand, and said: I have fulfilled my uncle's oath, but no hijrah."
"2987","2621 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Ubayd Allah ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Ubayd Allah ibn Abi Ziyad narrated to us, from Umm Yahya daughter of Yala, from her father, he said: I came with my father on the day of the conquest of Makkah and said: O Messenger of Allah, this is my father pledging you upon hijrah. He said: No hijrah after the conquest, but jihad and intention."
"2988","2622 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Uqayl narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Amr ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Umayyah ibn Yala ibn Munabbih informed me that his father informed him that Yala said: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning Abu Umayyah on the day of the conquest and said: O Messenger of Allah, pledge my father upon hijrah. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Rather I pledge him upon jihad, for hijrah has been cut off."
"2989","2623 - And Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Habban ibn Hilal narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray narrated to us, he said: Khalid narrated to us, from Abu Uthman, from Mujashi ibn Masud, that he said to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): This is Mujalid ibn Masud, pledge him upon hijrah. He said: No hijrah after the conquest of Makkah, but I pledge him upon Islam."
"2990","2624 - And Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Ubayd Allah ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Ismail narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith, from Amr ibn Shuayb, from his father, from his grandfather Abd Allah ibn Amr, he said: When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) conquered Makkah he addressed the people and said in his sermon: No hijrah after the conquest. Abu Jafar said: In these reports is the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informing that hijrah was cut off by the conquest of Makkah. That was reported from Ibn Umar and from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with them both, from their saying, mentioning the reason hijrah was cut off by the conquest of Makkah, and the reason by which hijrah occurred before the conquest of Makkah."
"2991","And as Fahd had narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Dahhak al-Bablutti narrated to us, he said: al-Awzai narrated to us, he said: Abdah narrated to me, from Mujahid, from Ibn Umar, he said: Hijrah was cut off after the conquest."
"2992","And as Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Muawiyah ibn Amr al-Azdi narrated to us, he said: Abu Ishaq al-Fazari narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik, from Ata, he said: I entered with Ubayd ibn Umayr upon Aishah, and he said to her: O Mother of the Believers, is there any hijrah today? She said: No, but jihad and intention. Hijrah was only before the conquest of Makkah, with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in Madinah, a man fleeing with his religion to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Abu Jafar said: Aishah thus reported the meaning by which hijrah occurred, and that it was cut off by the conquest of Makkah. What proves this meaning is also what we reported above in our book from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), his saying to Safwan ibn Umayyah when he came to him in Madinah after it had been said to him before that that there is no religion for one who did not emigrate, and his permitting him to return to Makkah. Had the ruling then been as it was before the conquest of Makkah upon this meaning, he would not have permitted him to return to the abode from which he emigrated, just as he did not permit that to those who emigrated to him in Madinah before the conquest of Makkah, even assigning them, when they came to it for their Hajj, a stay of three days after departure, no more."
"2993","2625 - As Yunus had narrated to us, he said: Anas ibn Iyad narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Humayd, he said: I heard Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz asking al-Saib ibn Yazid: What did you hear concerning the residence of Makkah for the emigrant? He said: al-Ala ibn al-Hadrami said, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Three days after departure for the emigrant."
"2994","2626 - And as Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Habban ibn Hilal narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Said narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Humayd, then he mentioned with its chain the like of it. Abu Jafar said: The emigrants even feared death overtaking them there, deeming it grave and dreading it for themselves."
"2995","2627 - As Yunus had narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Amir ibn Sad ibn Abi Waqqas, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: I fell ill in the year of the conquest an illness from which I neared death. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to visit me, and I said: O Messenger of Allah, shall I be left behind from my hijrah? He said: You shall never be left behind after me doing a deed seeking thereby the Face of Allah Almighty but you increase thereby in elevation and rank. Perhaps you shall be left behind after me so peoples benefit by you and others are harmed by you. O Allah, complete for my Companions their hijrah, and do not turn them back upon their heels. But the wretched Sad ibn Khawlah, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) lamented him that he died in Makkah."
"2996","2628 - And as Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik informed him, and as al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: al-Shafii narrated to us, from Malik, from Ibn Shihab, from Amir ibn Sad ibn Abi Waqqas, from Sad ibn Abi Waqqas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to visit me in the year of the Farewell Hajj from a pain severe upon me, then he mentioned the hadith. Do you not see the barring by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) after the conquest of Makkah of those who had emigrated to him before that in Madinah from returning to Makkah, since they had emigrated from it and left it for Allah Almighty for the city of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in desire for it, and from residing in it except what they cannot avoid after their Hajj to it of residing therein to prepare to leave it and return to their abode of hijrah, and the permitting by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of that to others whose Islam was after the conquest of Makkah. No proof is more proving of the cutting off of hijrah by the conquest of Makkah, after what we reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) therein in this chapter, than this. It was reported from three Ansaris in this chapter, namely Abu Said al-Khudri, Zayd ibn Thabit, and Rafi ibn Khadij, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), confirming this meaning, saying: It was from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) therein after Allah Almighty sent down: When the help of Allah and the conquest comes, and after his reciting it to the people."
"2997","2629 - As Bakkar ibn Qutaybah had narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Murrah narrated to me, he said: I heard Abu al-Bakhtari narrating from Abu Said al-Khudri, he said: When: When the help of Allah and the conquest comes was sent down, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited it till he completed it, then said: I and my Companions are one party, and people are another party; no hijrah after the conquest. Abu Said said: I narrated that to Marwan ibn al-Hakam, who was over Madinah, and he said: You lied. With him were Zayd ibn Thabit and Rafi ibn Khadij, both with him upon the couch. I said: These two, had they wished, would narrate to you, but this one, meaning Zayd ibn Thabit, fears you remove him from charity, and this one fears you remove him from the headship of his people, meaning Rafi ibn Khadij, both with him. He said: He struck me therewith with his stick. When they saw that they both said: He spoke truth. An objector said: Does this contradict what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)?"
"2998","2630 - He mentioned what al-Rabi al-Muradi had narrated to us, he said: Shuayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Abu al-Khayr, that Junadah ibn Abi Umayyah narrated to him that a man narrated to him that men among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), some said: Hijrah has been cut off, and they differed therein. I went to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, people say hijrah has been cut off. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Hijrah is not cut off while jihad lasts."
"2999","2631 - And what Ibn Abi Dawud had narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Abi Salamah narrated to us, from Ibn Zabr, from Busr ibn Ubayd Allah, from Abu Idris al-Khawlani, from Hassan ibn al-Damri, from Abd Allah ibn al-Sadi, he said: I came as delegate to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) among men of Banu Sad. They came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and fulfilled their needs, leaving me behind with their mounts. I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, inform me of my need. He said: And what is your need? I said: Has hijrah been cut off? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You are the best of them in need, or he said: Your need is the best of their needs; hijrah is not cut off while disbelievers are fought."
"3000","2632 - And what Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahim al-Harawi had narrated to us, he said: Duhaym narrated to us, he said: al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: Ibn Zabr narrated to us that he heard Busr ibn Ubayd Allah narrating from Abu Idris al-Khawlani, from Abd Allah ibn Waqdan al-Qurashi, who had been suckled among Banu Sad ibn Bakr, he said: I came as delegate among men of Banu Sad to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned the like of it."
